{"id":"see-outline","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a0","title":"See your outline","done":"2026-08-09T15:36:34-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #58 (squash ac0cba3).\n- Serve a directory, parse and validate the .jsonl files (format core: unique ids, dangling refs, cycles, stored derived state)\n- Render the tree read-only in the browser via a minimal surface view\n- **Visible:** your outline in a browser tab — or the validation error, with file:line\n\nResolved 2026-08-09:\n- Entrypoint is `olai web <dir> [--port] [--host]` (nix-built binary; just serve and nix run wrap it; loopback default)\n- UI is a sidebar of found outlines + one tree per route\n- Mirrors render the target subtree inline, marked (cycle-safe)\n- Nodes render title with styled #tags, derived done/doing/open status, date badge, and desc as sanitized markdown\n- packages/store is born here in load-once form (revision-tagged snapshot, codec seam; watcher/probe arrive with live-updates — see docs/brainstorming/architecture.live-store.md)\n- An invalid set renders an error view listing ALL errors with file:line, grouped by file, cross-file errors in their own section\n- The tree has client-local collapse/expand\n- e2e is Cucumber + Playwright in kolu's packages/tests shape, features written fresh for this item (nothing ported from the racket branch)\n- Styling is Tailwind v4 exactly as kolu does it (utilities inline; no class-validity scanner — accepted trade-off, decided 2026-08-09)\n- Specs: docs/format.md, docs/architecture.md","after":["scaffold"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"live","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a1","title":"Live updates","done":"2026-08-09T16:54:33-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #59 (squash ec5aead).\n- Probe-based staleness over the recursive directory, snapshot stream, error cell\n- **Visible:** edit a file or git pull, the page updates without reload; break a file, its outline degrades in place while the rest stays live; a cross-file break keeps last-good and shows the error banner\n\nResolved 2026-08-09:\n- Error scope is hybrid — a file that fails to decode renders its error in place of that one outline (emanote-style) while other outlines stay live\n- Cross-file validation failures (dangling refs, cycles) hold whole-set last-good with the banner (the codec passes decode failures into validate so the snapshot embeds per-outline failures; store stays generic)\n- Deletion is not special (a probe diff — dangling inbound refs are ordinary validation errors, no grace window)\n- The commit()/optimistic-concurrency write gate is deferred to the chat item, where the ops layer is born\n- The periodic backstop probe stays (~60s) alongside the watcher and refresh()\n- Stamps are mtime+size\n- This item also closes the see-outline test gap: packages/store gets real unit tests\n- Design: docs/brainstorming/architecture.live-store.md","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"navigation","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a0","title":"Zoom & navigation","done":"2026-08-09T17:18:29-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #60 (squash e036504), which also carried two server fixes approved mid-flight:\n- a busy port now falls back to an OS-assigned one with both facts logged\n- a listen failure reports as itself (ListenFailed) instead of the [object Object] runtime-fault message\n- Zoom into any node as its own page at /n/<id> — stable set-unique ids make permalinks free, surviving renames and moves across files\n- Breadcrumbs always show the canonical parent chain (stateless, even when you arrived through a mirror)\n- A per-view done-visibility toggle (client-local cell, like collapse)\n- Resolved 2026-08-09 (see brainstorming doc)\n- Depends only on see-outline's tree — can run in parallel with live-updates\n- Brainstorming: docs/brainstorming/viewing-web.md","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"search","ord":"a7V","title":"Search & filtering","desc":"Finding things: the query grammar, filter-in-place, the palette and header search.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"md-docs","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a0","title":"Documents #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T11:11:13-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #66 (squash ed3ef7f):\n- every .md is a page at /doc/<path> with its own sidebar section\n- doc-carrying nodes preview one line and render the document inline when zoomed\n- document text rides the store snapshot (same codec, same revision — no second read path)\n- /media/* pictures route with a both-ends traversal guard (e2e-tested against ..%2f)\n- GFM footnotes with per-block id minting\n- rehype-highlight bundled, no CDN (e2e asserts nothing fetched off-server)\n- Decisions recorded in docs/brainstorming/viewing-web.md §Documents\n- Render the served .md files as documents and link them from nodes' doc fields\n- Moved out of the old editor-growth bucket — it is viewing, not editing\n- **Racket parity (2026-08-09 audit):** relative images served safely (racket's /media/* with restricted extensions), highlighted code fences without a CDN, footnotes\n- Depends only on see-outline's reader; parallel-safe with everything else in this theme","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"journal","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a0","title":"Journal & calendar #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T08:48:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #62 (squash ccb77a1), including a scope addition: the shipped roadmap nodes carry date fields stamped with their PR merge instants (local -04:00), so this feature's calendar renders the project's own history.\n- **From the racket audit (2026-08-09):** a month calendar in the sidebar (day-with-content dots, today ringed, day-being-read filled) plus a /today view\n- All derived from date fields at view time — no stored year/month hierarchy (rewrite decision 11), no `daily` op\n\nResolved 2026-08-09:\n- The calendar aggregates the WHOLE set's dated nodes (any outline — the journal-is-a-query decision realized; no Daily.jsonl filename magic)\n- Clicking a day (and /today) opens /d/<date>, a day view listing every node dated that day grouped by outline with ancestors for context\n- /today with nothing dated today renders an honest empty state (creation arrives with editing ops)\n- The calendar shows the current month with prev/next paging","after":["navigation"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"theming","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a0","title":"Theming #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T13:06:09-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #69 (squash 430d572), authored by Claude/Opus.\n- Fifteen named palettes picked in the browser, chalk default\n- localStorage + data-theme, no server round-trip\n- **From the racket audit (2026-08-09):** named palettes selected client-side (racket shipped 15, chalk default with AA contrast), stored in localStorage, applied via data-theme — no server round-trip","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"mobile-pwa","parent":"feat-mobile","ord":"a0","title":"Mobile & PWA #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T11:00:12-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #65 (squash 4005580):\n- the original racket icons byte-identical (git hash-object verified)\n- a real manifest as server/src/manifest.ts via kolu's layer\n- one column below 48rem with the sidebar as a capped scrolling header\n- 44px touch targets spelled once (touch.ts)\n- visualViewport published as --visible-h/--visible-bottom (lifting the connection dot; left as the chat sheet's seam)\n- no service worker with a scenario pinning it\n- phone e2e at 390×844 plus a laptop scenario\n- **Stale:** the chat full-sheet remains owed to after #63\n- **From the racket audit (2026-08-09):** installable PWA (manifest + icons, standalone display; no offline mode — the app is live-only), touch target sizing, mobile viewport/keyboard handling (racket's visualViewport tracking), and the chat panel becoming a full sheet on phones\n- **Verified 2026-08-10:** today the server ships kolu's manifest with icons: [] — no logo, no touch icon, no favicon — while master-racket carries the complete ORIGINAL asset set (olai/web/static/: icon.svg, icon-192/512, maskable, apple-touch-icon, manifest.webmanifest)\n- Port those original logo assets verbatim; the connection-health indicator shipped separately with PR #61","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"outlines-collection","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a2","title":"Outlines as a collection #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T18:07:08-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #81 (squash e75f157).\n- The `outlines` member was a STREAM carrying the whole corpus in every frame, with the fine-grained part delegated to a client-side `reconcile` keyed on a Solid default the payload never promised\n- It is now a COLLECTION keyed by root-relative file path — declared in the protocol as `keySchema` — served with the batched `deltas` verb: the whole keyed set on (re)subscribe, then one coalesced {upserts, removes} frame per probe tick\n- Wire cost is O(changed files), so a `git pull` that rewrites forty outlines is still one frame\n\n**@olai/store:**\n- keeps the diff it already computed: a published Snapshot names `changed` and `removed`, so a consumer publishing per file does not re-derive it by comparing two whole snapshots\n- it spans the gap between two PUBLISHED revisions rather than one probe — a probe the codec refuses publishes nothing, so what it found is still owed to the next revision that validates, and a path edited-then-deleted is a remove rather than a change\n- still no olai types in the package: it is path talk\n\n**@olai/surface:**\n- declares OutlineEntry — a file's nodes, its `rev`, and its `broken`, which is the per-entity error scope as DATA: an unreadable file keeps its key and carries its errors\n- plus a `manifest` cell for the set-wide facts, whose `null` is the \"never loaded\" bit a collection cannot express (an empty snapshot means the directory holds no outlines; a first probe still running has to say something else)\n\n**Server/client:**\n- the server cuts one revision into entries and maps the store's changed/removed onto the collection's verbs (server/src/outlines.ts), published by one owned fiber so a revision's entries and manifest are one write\n- the client's read side is one module (web/src/client/outlines.ts): the collection's keys in path order are the sidebar's list, and `derive` over every entry's nodes is the one derivation the whole app reads\n- docs/brainstorming/outlines-as-collection.md moves from brainstorming to BUILT, with its open questions answered\n\n**Original filing:** Re-model the `outlines` stream as a surface collection keyed by file path, with the opt-in deltas verb: snapshot on (re)subscribe, then one coalesced {upserts, removes} frame per probe tick — wire cost O(changed files) instead of O(corpus), keying declared in the protocol (keySchema) rather than inherited from a client-library default, per-entity brokenness as plain entry data.\n- The store's probe already computes the change list; expose it as a per-publish summary so the patch computation stays encapsulated in packages/store\n- **Timing open:** bundle with the kolu writeWrappedValue fix (same client layer), or wait until corpus size makes full-snapshot frames hurt; also pushes the frame-cap horizon out","after":["live"],"see":["live"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"edges-ui","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a0","title":"Blocked dependencies & edges in the UI #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T10:42:38-04:00","desc":"Re-scoped 2026-08-10: the see half moved to the see-refs item (agents theme); this item keeps BLOCKED derivation and rendering.\n- **Filed 2026-08-10:** the format validates after/blocks/see (acyclic, blocks normalized to after) but nothing RENDERS them — racket derived and showed blockedness\n- Derive blocked at view time and render it distinctly from the marks\n- **THE RULE, corrected 2026-08-11** against this item's own filing text above (\"after-targets are not all done\" is `status !== \"done\"`, the trap docs/format.md writes the rule against): `a after b` holds `a` up while `b` is a TASK that is not done — doing or todo — so a target nobody marked never blocks\n- Racket exempted archived nodes from blocking but not from cycle checks, and that asymmetry is kept\n- No stored state — blockedness is derived from the edges and the marks together","after":["navigation"],"see":["see-refs","checkbox"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"title-markdown","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a3","title":"Inline markdown in titles #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T06:43:14-04:00","desc":"**CORRECTION 2026-08-11:** this done stamp was FALSE from seconds after the merge.\n- commit a56cb65, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (25 files; exact content-inverse of squash e1ab412, verified by the orchestrator's stamp audit)\n- The third such incident from the same morning session (with dd34849/#87 and 12ea294/#83)\n- Restoration tracked as restore-84; this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #84\n\n**Filed 2026-08-10:** racket rendered titles as INLINE-ONLY markdown (bold, links, code — no block elements); the new olai renders titles verbatim with only #tags styled.\n- Port the inline-only discipline through the existing sanitized pipeline","after":["see-outline"],"see":["md-docs"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"checkbox","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a1","title":"Status checkbox #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T15:33:37-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #75: status checkbox beside the bullet.\n- ☑ done, ◧ doing, ☐ open — from the same derived status the title already tones with\n- open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one\n- read-only (toggling is keyboard-editing)\n- E2E asserts all three faces including empty on an open node\n- **Racket original:** rendered node status as a CHECKBOX beside the bullet (checked / half / empty for done / doing / open); the rewrite showed only strikethrough/title tone\n- Port the three faces as a read-only affordance from the same derived status the styling already reads — open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one\n- No click handler: toggling belongs to keyboard-editing","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"notes-single","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a4","title":"Notes: one state, same line #ui","done":"2026-08-10T17:08:58-04:00","desc":"Handed to an agent on 2026-08-10: the work ran in Kolu terminal fd21b099 (Kolu is the tool that runs our coding agents in named terminals), with the Grok model writing the code and Claude Opus reviewing it once the tests were green.\n\n**The human's ruling**, on seeing the three-way density toggle that had shipped: delete it — the app gets no note-density settings at all.\n\n**One way of drawing a note, copied exactly from Workflowy** (checked frame by frame against a screen recording made 2026-08-10):\n- The note sits under the node's title on its own line, in smaller, dimmer text, cut off after one line with an ellipsis.\n- Clicking it expands it in place to the full note text plus the node's date and its \"see\" reference links. Clicking away, or clicking it again, collapses it.\n- Nothing happens on hover; a tap on a touch screen behaves exactly like a click.\n- A node's own zoomed-in page always shows everything, unclamped.\n\nTwo earlier designs were proposed and withdrawn the same day: the three-way toggle, and a design that expanded the note on the same line on hover.\n\nThis replaces the full / first-line / hidden switch that the desc-density item (pull requests #74 and #76) shipped, and the per-location \"is this note unfolded\" state goes away with it.","see":["desc-density"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:28:40-04:00"}
{"id":"sidebar-tree","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a3","title":"Sidebar file tree #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-11T06:33:55-04:00","desc":"**Filed 2026-08-10:** racket's sidebar was a file TREE; the new sidebar is a flat list where nested outlines render as path strings (Daily/2026-08.jsonl).\n- Group by directory once a served corpus has depth — the ported Dropbox data already does\n- **Scope confirmed & widened by the human 2026-08-10** (screenshot of the deployed sidebar): tree navigation of FOLDERS covering BOTH lists — .jsonl outlines and .md documents — today every brainstorming/*.md renders as a wrapped path string\n- Collapsible directory nodes (collapse state client-local, like the outline tree's)\n- **Resolved 2026-08-10 (human):** a SINGLE unified tree mixing .jsonl and .md — a folder shows everything it holds, as racket had — replacing the two flat sections","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"panels","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a1","title":"Panel rework: toggleable & resizable","done":"2026-08-11T18:19:57-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #104**, squash-merged as commit 407edb2 (the done date is the merge instant). Final commit on the branch before merge: b42b62f.\n- All 8 changes the reviewer required were made: phone drawer, scrim and bottom-sheet geometry; clamping panel widths against the viewport; unmounting the chat transcript when minimized; surfacing the failure of the command palette's `>` prefix; and turning the tests into ones that check behaviour rather than mere visibility. Plus drag-to-snap on the sheet and 8 cheap optional suggestions.\n- Two rounds of conflict in this roadmap file were resolved. The branch ends up carrying no roadmap edits of its own — the file only changes through merge commits that take master's version.\n- All 18 required checks in odu (the project's continuous-integration tool) passed on both Linux and macOS.\n\n**Handed to an agent on 2026-08-11**: Kolu terminal c34ee216, model Grok 4.5 (high reasoning, permissions auto-approved), working in the git worktree `.worktrees/panels` on branch `panels`; its written brief is at `.worktrees/panels-BRIEF.md`.\n- At the review step, pull request #104 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/104) was open with the implementation (commit 9d373dd), a fix to the drawer's touch targets, and a cleanup pass (commit 822589f) applying the project's two standing refactors — one for simplicity, one that looks for volatility worth hiding behind a stable interface. 527 unit tests and 173 end-to-end tests passed locally.\n- Ten evidence screenshots were checked by the orchestrator: the icon rail, the minimized chat pill and the palette shell all match the agreed decisions. The pill was only photographed in its empty state, so the reviewer was asked to confirm the last-message text and the pulse animation.\n- **Review done** by Claude Opus in a side-by-side terminal (620b1deb) on 2026-08-11. The reviewer drove the real app on desktop and phone using a scripted agent and took 21 screenshots. Verdict: the desktop half was solid, the phone half broken in ways the automated suite cannot detect.\n  - The 8 required fixes: the drawer resolves to `position: relative` and renders displaced and clipped; the scrim and sheet cover the header, making its controls untappable; widths are not clamped against the viewport; a minimized chat keeps a transcript subscription alive for the life of the page; the palette's `>` prefix swallows failures silently; the README names are stale; and the tests assert that things are visible rather than that they work.\n  - 10 optional suggestions, notably: the sheet's grab handle must drag rather than cycle through snap points on tap; hand-writing the resize logic is justified, hand-writing the sheet is not.\n- **Merge pre-approved by the human on 2026-08-11** — approval opens the gate, it does not skip the pipeline: the squash merge lands once all 8 required fixes are made, the phone evidence is checked by the orchestrator, and odu's checks are green on both platforms at a head synced with master.\n- Next step after review: Grok makes the fixes, then merges master in (past #103) and takes odu's checks to green; the reviewer is Claude, per the default of reviewing with a different model than the one that wrote the code.\n- **Reconciled with the already-shipped app header (#101) by the human, 2026-08-11:** the header keeps the connection dot and the agent toggle, so there is one home for app-wide chrome. The minimized chat pill is only the chat's collapsed state — the last agent message plus a pulse — sitting bottom-right, with no connection dot on it.\n\n**Filed 2026-08-10** out of the human's UI evaluation session, and kept as ONE item by the human's decision that day. Working prototypes: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/e8b0f5ad-f293-4982-a4cc-470565ab320e (the evaluation was grounded in master at commit 232e774 plus live screenshots). The prototype's pop-out floating chat window was explored and dropped — the human retracted it on 2026-08-10; chat stays a docked panel.\n- Every panel has exactly TWO states — open, or minimized but still showing a signal. Nothing closes to nowhere (resolved 2026-08-10 by the human; the prototype's third \"closed\" state and the chat's × are dropped). The reader owns the widths.\n- **Desktop, all resolved 2026-08-10 by the human on the Prototype 1 shell:**\n  - The sidebar collapses to a roughly 3rem-wide icon rail (expand button, outlines/calendar/docs icons, live dot — app chrome never disappears) and can be drag-resized.\n  - The chat dock can be drag-resized.\n  - The minimized chat is a pill carrying the last agent message, pulsing while a turn is running. It replaces the bare `>_` agent pill, whose sidebar-footer and corner positions go away.\n  - The connection dot joins that pill — one bottom-right strip answering \"is the app alive\" (connection state plus last agent message). This subsumes the connection indicator planned in the no-lifecycle item, on every screen.\n  - The keyboard shortcuts are reserved NOW so that keyboard editing cannot collide with them: ⌘K opens the palette, ⌘\\ toggles the sidebar, ⌘J toggles chat.\n  - A ⌘K palette is the way to summon things: navigation, panel toggles, and a `>` prefix that sends a message to the agent. This ships the palette SHELL only; the separate palette roadmap item still owns jump-to-node type-ahead and the write actions.\n- **Mobile mirrors the same design, translated:** the capped burger menu that closes on any tap becomes a real slide-over drawer with a scrim; chat becomes a bottom sheet with half and full snap points; its collapsed form is a strip above the thumb showing the last message. This discharges the \"chat becomes a full sheet on phones\" debt owed by the mobile-pwa item.\n- All layout state is stored in this browser's localStorage, like the existing chatOpen setting — never sent to the server.\n- No open questions remain; the item was ready to hand to an agent.","after":["chat"],"see":["no-lifecycle","palette"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:28:40-04:00"}
{"id":"solid-utilization","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a6","title":"Solid utilization: what the client hand-rolls that the framework does better #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T14:47:41-04:00","desc":"SHIPPED 2026-08-10 (PR #72): all five seams, one PR, each with a test.\n- **(1) ErrorBoundary:** `<ErrorBoundary>` around the shell in main.tsx drawing errors/Fault.tsx — what threw, verbatim, and TWO ways out: a reload (the framework's reloadForUpdate) for a stale bundle, and `/` for a page that is poison, since a fault is usually deterministic for its route and Reload alone would loop.\n  - The fallback also LOGS it: a boundary swallows, so without that line the fault reaches no console at all and a browser test meets it as a timeout on a missing element\n  - Documented as a FOURTH error surface beside the three that are about the files\n- **(2) createSelector:** in Sidebar.tsx (one selector, both lists: what is active is a file) and calendar/Calendar.tsx (the open day; deliberately NOT today, which moves once a day and is meant to redraw the grid).\n  - Worth more than the comment claims: `page()` mints a new object every wire frame, so the old form re-ran every row's comparison on every frame from the server, not only on navigation\n- **(3) scroll.ts:** client/scroll.ts owns every statement that moves the page — top on push, per-entry restore on popstate, and history.scrollRestoration = manual (the browser's restore runs into a page this client has not drawn).\n  - The router says only WHICH of the two a navigation gets, because a push and a pop are the one thing it knows that scroll.ts does not\n  - Positions are keyed by an entry key in history.state, read at the moment they are written rather than copied, and recorded AS THE READER SCROLLS because popstate fires after the position it was made from is gone\n  - Unthrottled on purpose: a trailing write lands after the navigation and would record the old page against the new entry\n- **(4) preference.ts:** grows preferenceChanged + watchPreference — one storage listener feeding the existing theme and chatOpen signals, both started from main.tsx where this client starts everything document-lifetime, and a cleared storage (event.key null) counts as a change rather than as silence.\n  - @solid-primitives/storage's makePersisted was evaluated and NOT adopted: `<html>` is the theme's state and the shell's boot script writes it before any module exists, and this file's degradation contract (storage may throw, the preference stands) is a promise made here rather than trusted to a dependency\n- **(5) clock.ts:** re-reads on visibilitychange beside the timer, and re-aims the timer from where the clock actually got to — in one place, so the rule the file argues for is written down once.\n- **TESTS:** unit for thrownText, preferenceChanged and the clock's wake (a fake document + setSystemTime); e2e for the fault card, for scroll on zoom/back (verified to FAIL without the fix — the page stayed at 64px), and for a theme picked in a second tab of the same browser, left open on purpose\n- SETTLED_SELECTOR now knows three shapes of the app rather than two and world.open() quotes a fault card, so no scenario meets a client fault as a bare timeout again\n\nFiled 2026-08-10 as the follow-up PR #70 asked for (the client stopped keeping a store above the framework's and adopted @solid-primitives/keyed; this audit hunts the same class — hand-rolled code where a Solid primitive is MORE CORRECT, not dependency swaps). Five verified gaps, each with evidence:\n- **(1) No ErrorBoundary anywhere:** the app has three error surfaces for DATA (errors/Page, Banner, Broken) and none for its own code, so a thrown render fault is a dead white tab; #70's RangeError crashed exactly this way. Wrap the shell in `<ErrorBoundary>` rendering an honest 'this page broke' card with the fault and a reload.\n- **(2) The sidebar marks its active entry O(n):** Sidebar.tsx:120/149 computes current={props.active === file} per Link, re-diffing every row on each navigation — while Picker.tsx:31 (theming, #69) already established createSelector as the house pattern with the rationale written out. Apply it to both sidebar lists and the calendar's open day (calendar/Day.tsx:108); matters more once sidebar-tree lands and the corpus is the Dropbox port.\n- **(3) Navigation ignores scroll:** no scrollTo/scrollRestoration outside the chat transcript — router.go() keeps the old scrollTop so zooming from deep in a long outline lands the new page mid-scroll, and back/forward restores nothing the SPA redraw doesn't accidentally keep. Scroll-to-top on push, per-entry restore on popstate; chat/Transcript.tsx's follow-discipline is the house proof this is done deliberately or not at all.\n- **(4) Preferences never sync across tabs:** preference.ts has no storage-event listener, so a theme picked in one tab leaves a sibling tab stale until reload — against the promise clock.ts argues from ('the one stale thing on the screen'). Either @solid-primitives/storage's makePersisted or one listener in preference.ts feeding the existing signals.\n- **(5) clock.ts's midnight setTimeout does not survive suspend/background-throttle:** a laptop asleep past midnight wakes showing yesterday's ring until the late timer fires — the file's own doc names this exact promise. Re-read on visibilitychange alongside the timer.\n- **Audited and REJECTED, so nobody re-litigates:** a router library (routes.ts is five shapes and the hand-rolled pushState router is documented as deliberate), Portal for the fixed panels (no transformed ancestors exist), Suspense (errors-as-data is the design)\n- One PR: the five are independent seams but each is small and they share the review","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"desc-density","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a5","title":"Notes fold to one line","done":"2026-08-10T15:31:46-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #74.\n- **Filed 2026-08-10** (human, from a screenshot: ~3 nodes per screen once every desc renders in full)\n- **Workflowy's answer, adopted:** desc renders as ONE dim truncated plain-text line by default — no markdown blocks in the preview\n- The full desc renders in exactly two places: the node's own zoomed page (it is the page's subject there), and per-node in-place expansion — click the first line to toggle the body open and shut, client-local and keyed by PLACE like collapse\n- A per-view density switch beside the done toggle: full / first-line, first-line the default (the first line toggles the body open and shut), the same client-local cell pattern as doneHidden\n- Same visual grammar as md-docs' one-line doc preview\n- Pairs with title-markdown; independent and parallel-safe","after":["see-outline"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00"}
{"id":"not-every-node-a-task","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a2","title":"A bullet is not a task","done":"2026-08-11T06:29:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-10. Today every node is a task: status is derived as done → doing → **otherwise open** (docs/format.md:62), so `open` is what a node gets for carrying no mark, and there is no way to say \"this is only a bullet\".\n- `checkbox` (PR #75) made it visible on every row on purpose — \"open is a visible EMPTY box, not the absence of one\"\n\n**Workflowy's model, which we are adopting:** a bullet is just a bullet, completion is a flag you apply to some of them, and an unticked bullet is plain text rather than an unfinished task.\n\n**No decision ever defended the current model.**\n- Nothing in docs/ argues that every node should have a status\n- The rule arrived in ac0cba3 (PR #58, the first rendering PR) as part of the derived-status rule, and racket did the same before it (`checkbox` is tagged #racket-parity)\n- Two things hid it: the only outline olai has ever served is its own roadmap, which really is all tasks, so \"everything is open\" never looked wrong; and format.md:62 argues carefully about WHERE status lives (\"a stored value that currently agrees with the computed one is still a second copy\") without ever asking WHETHER every node needs one\n\n**Why it matters now:** serving the ported Dropbox notes (see sidebar-tree) renders every paragraph as an unfinished to-do, and `search`'s planned `is:open` operator is useless if it matches the whole corpus.\n\n**The defect stated precisely:** `open` answers two different questions with one value — \"a task nobody has started\" and \"not a task at all\". The same shape the manifest cell exists to fix, where an empty collection could not distinguish \"never loaded\" from \"empty\".\n\n**Three shapes, cheapest first** (the human chose the third; the first is a legitimate first step if the screen should be honest before the format moves):\n1. **View-only**: draw the checkbox only where something in the subtree is actually marked. No format change; the model still says everything is open.\n2. **Opt in**: a `#todo` tag or field makes a node a task. Clear, but adds a concept and migrates the roadmap.\n3. **Drop `open` as the residual**: a node is either marked (`done`/`doing`) or has no status at all. Closest to Workflowy, and the honest fix.\n\n**Known cost of (3), not a free lunch:**\n- the parent rule needs a second case — \"all children done → done\" becomes \"count only the children that are tasks\"\n- `blocked` has to say what \"not done\" means for a bullet that was never a task (probably: a plain bullet never blocks)\n- `edges-ui` has NOT shipped, which is lucky: blocked-derivation can be designed against the new model rather than migrated to it\n- Also touches the query tools and the done-visibility toggle, which both read `open`","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"todo-mark","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a3","title":"`todo` — the third mark","done":"2026-08-11T07:45:28-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, straight out of using what #86 shipped.\n- `not-every-node-a-task` removed `open` because it was the RESIDUAL — the value a node got for carrying nothing, which made every bullet a task. That was the defect and removing it was right.\n- But it also removed the CONCEPT of an unstarted task, and those are two different changes\n\n**`done` and `doing` are explicit stored marks.** A third explicit mark, `todo`, obeys the new model exactly: you have to put it there, so a bullet stays a bullet unless marked.\n- What the model forbids is a DEFAULT, not a third face. Nothing about the Workflowy reading argues against it\n\n**The gap showed** the moment the human tried to say \"this is work I have not started\": there was nothing to say it with.\n- The orchestrator reached for a `#todo` hashtag, which is decoration — no checkbox, no derivation, styled like `#racket-parity`\n- The human called it hacky and is right\n\n**Ship:**\n- `Status = \"done\" | \"doing\" | \"todo\"` (packages/format/src/derive.ts:32), stored like the other two, never derived as a residual. At most one of the three on a node, same rule `done`/`doing` already have.\n- The empty checkbox comes back, for `todo` ONLY — `checkbox` (PR #75) had three faces and #86 cut it to two; this restores the third without restoring the default. A node carrying no mark still draws no box.\n- Parents count a `todo` child as an unfinished task, so a theme of unstarted work derives `doing` rather than nothing. Decide and write down what a parent whose task children are ALL `todo` derives — `todo` is the natural answer, but it must be stated, not inferred.\n- `blocked`, still unshipped (`edges-ui`), inherits it: docs/format.md:49 currently says `b` blocks `a` \"while `b` is `doing`\" — with three marks that becomes `doing` or `todo`, and the trap it warns against (`status !== \"done\"`, which reads every plain bullet as an obstacle) stands unchanged.\n- Migration, authorised by the human for this PR only: the nine `#todo` TAGS the orchestrator put in docs/roadmap.jsonl (search, edges-ui, panels, self-edit, split-merge, undo, dragdrop-multiselect, input-widgets, palette) become the real mark, and the tags come out of those titles.\n\ndocs/format.md's Status section is the text that has to change, and it should argue the three-mark shape as carefully as it currently argues the two — the next reader learns the model from there, and `edges-ui` will be built from it.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"hide-done-scope","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a4","title":"Done-hidden hides things that are not done #human","done":"2026-08-11T09:12:52-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, from the first real use of the #88 model. Two intertwined problems, hit on the same node (`orchestrator-in-chat`).\n\n**1. \"Done-hidden\" does not mean \"done nodes are hidden\".**\n- `withoutDone` (packages/format/src/derive.ts:381) drops a `done` row **with its whole subtree**\n- A parent that *derives* done — because its task children are all done — takes its unmarked-bullet descendants down with it, even though nobody ever marked them done and they may be the very findings you wanted to read\n- Live case: `agents` derives done, so the done toggle hides `orchestrator-in-chat` and the four gap-findings under it\n- The human's position, verbatim: *\"done-hidden should mean done nodes are hidden.\"*\n\n**2. A parent whose children are all bullets cannot be marked at all.**\n- The intended shape was: `orchestrator-in-chat` is `todo` (it IS the work), its four children are findings — plain bullets, correctly unmarked\n- The ops layer refuses it: `docs/format.md:49` forbids any stored mark on a node with counted children, as the price of no-stored-derived-state\n\n**The two compose into an absurdity:** the node tracking the chat-orchestrator work is unmarkable (2), so `agents` derives done, so the done toggle hides that work entirely (1).\n- The escape hatch from (1) — mark the work `todo` so the parent reads doing — is exactly what (2) forbids, so #88's fix was defeated by the model on its first day of real use\n\n**Resolved 2026-08-11 — the root cause is derivation itself, and the fix is to delete it.**\n- Deriving a parent's status from its children treats outline containment (notes under an item) as task decomposition (subtasks)\n- It also violates #86's own creed — \"a node is a task because someone said so, never by default\": derivation makes every parent-of-tasks a task by structure, nobody having said so, which is the `open` defect promoted one level up\n- The no-stored-derived-state rule exists only to defend derivation from contradiction; without derivation it has nothing to defend and dissolves rather than needing an exception\n- Neither candidate patch (vacuous-derivation exception, per-node hiding) was taken: each keeps the conflation and adds cases to it\n\n**The design** (the Workflowy model; org-mode likewise stores status per headline and keeps rollup display-only):\n- **Marks are plain stored facts on any node**, children or not. Status is never derived. `read this book` can be ticked with three notes hanging off it.\n- **Delete the machinery**: the `statuses` walk as status, `fromChildren`/`InTheWay`, both refusal sites (validator load error, ops `derived` refusal), and the format.md:83 rule. Net deletion.\n- **Done-hidden means exactly**: nodes whose stored mark is `done` are hidden, subtree included — a done mark on a parent is a human's claim about the whole branch, so the sweep is now justified rather than inferred.\n- **Rollup survives as display only**: a parent with task children may show derived progress (e.g. `3/5`), never a status — it feeds neither hiding nor blocking. Write-time nudges are policy, not invariants: when the last unfinished task child goes done, suggest marking the parent done; `set_done` over unfinished task children may warn. Never a load invariant — a merged file always loads.\n- **`blocked` (edges-ui) reads stored marks**: `doing` or `todo` blocks, a markless node never does. To block on a branch, mark the branch.\n- **Merge safety improves**: marks merge line-wise with no cross-line invariant a clean merge can break. (Today: mark a leaf in branch A, add a task child under it in branch B — clean textual merge, unloadable set.)\n- **Migration**: stored marks on the roadmap parents that should read as something (e.g. `bugs`/`infra` done, `viewing`/`agents` doing), through the format's own writer, like #88's migration.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"md-render","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a1","title":"Markdown rendering, perfected","done":"2026-08-11T13:08:50-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 at the human's request. Documents (.md files) and node notes both render through one markdown pipeline (remark and rehype, with GitHub-flavoured markdown, HTML sanitising and syntax highlighting, all bundled rather than fetched from a CDN). That rendering had never had a polish pass.\n\n- **Method, set by the human:** the agent takes screenshots of real rendered markdown, the orchestrator gives concrete visual feedback, and they iterate until it looks right — the same evidence loop the orchestrator protocol now requires anyway.\n\n**Status on 2026-08-11:**\n- The work ran in Kolu terminal `7cc6c6da` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved), in the git worktree `.worktrees/md-render` on branch `md-render`. The reviewer worked in a side-by-side terminal `f26ebf9d` (Grok).\n- The pull request had been reviewed and approved by srid; what remained was resolving the branch's merge conflicts against master, after which it would merge once the automated checks were green.\n- Pull request: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/92","changed":"2026-08-27T19:28:40-04:00"}
{"id":"doc-toc","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a2","title":"Documents need a table of contents","done":"2026-08-11T14:31:32-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human while reading his real brainstorming/*.md corpus through the perfected renderer: a long document (architecture notes run hundreds of lines) has no way to survey or jump — \"we need ToC.\"\n\n**Shape settled at dispatch:** heading anchors first (rehype-slug + autolinked headings, sanitize schema admitting the anchor ids deliberately), then the ToC derived from the heading tree at view time — nothing stored.\n- In-page collapsible won over a floating rail; notes never get one (a note is a tree row, not a page)\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-11 as PR #100** (squash cc899c5):\n- platform `<details>` contents, heading self-anchors\n- sanitise.ts asserting against the live default schema\n- six e2e scenarios asserted against the page and mutation-tested by the reviewer\n- single-pipeline-run pinned by identity, ToC keyed on file\n- **Known trade, disclosed hard:** the id namespace hashes the whole body, so ANY edit remints every heading fragment — stable permalinks are a different, future change","see":["md-render"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"app-header","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a2","title":"A header bar: the app's chrome above every column","done":"2026-08-11T15:40:03-04:00","desc":"**Design:** the header carries what is about the APP — wordmark, connection, agent toggle, theme; the sidebar carries what is about the DIRECTORY — calendar and the file tree.\n- Collapses the corner-pills special case on the sidebar-less screens: the header is the one home for chrome on every page, error report included (the ErrorBoundary fault card is the noted sole exception)\n- On the phone the burger joins the header's left edge\n- Settled slice of the layout rethink (take 2): https://claude.ai/code/artifact/cfe8c88b-9011-4838-86fb-7ddd412817af\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-11 as PR #101** (squash 875dec7).\n- Mid-flight design ruling from the human: the agent pill is a permanent TOGGLE (aria-pressed while the drawer is open), and the drawer's × went — with permanent chrome, the toggle is the one closer\n- **Review round** (the day's hardest): two measured blockers invisible to CI:\n  - Header clipping its pills at phone widths in three connection states\n  - The grid's `min-h-full` resolving to 0 inside a `min-h-0` flex item, ending the sidebar rule mid-screen\n  - Plus `settle()` weakened on a false premise, popover Escape/focus, ARIA — all fixed, re-measured dead in a real browser\n- Locked with geometry-per-connection-state, short-page height, error-report chrome, and picker-promise scenarios, the connecting one deterministic (holds the socket in CONNECTING or fails)\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"wf-styling","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a7","title":"Workflowy-exact styling: outline, sidebar, everything #ui","done":"2026-08-11T16:50:23-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #102**, squash-merged as commit c2dea45; the done mark carries the merge instant. Final commit before merge: 8c11745.\n- All 5 changes the reviewer required were made, plus 8 of the optional suggestions.\n- All 20 required checks in odu (the project's continuous-integration tool) passed on both x86_64 Linux and ARM macOS.\n- Six optional suggestions were skipped; the human agreed to that and moved them into the wf-gutter-debt item.\n\n**Handed to an agent on 2026-08-11**: Kolu terminal `bb7211d5` (Grok 4.5, high reasoning, permissions auto-approved), working in the git worktree `.worktrees/workflowy-styling` on branch `workflowy-styling`.\n- At the review step, pull request #102 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/102) was open with the implementation commit plus a cleanup pass applying the project's two standing refactors — one for simplicity, one that looks for volatility worth hiding behind a stable interface.\n- The orchestrator checked the evidence screenshots against the Workflowy reference images: bullets, the halo around a collapsed bullet, the hover ••• menu and triangle, checkboxes, tag pills, margins and the sidebar all match. The Open Sans font is fetched from nixpkgs and converted to woff2 at build time, with no CDN.\n- **Review done** by Claude Opus in a side-by-side terminal (`3c57bd38`) on 2026-08-11: all builds, tests and fonts reproduced green.\n  - Visual measurements against Workflowy: row pitch 22.6px against roughly 23, indent 33px against roughly 32.\n  - 5 required fixes were filed in PR-REVIEW.md: the menu's Zoom action reloads the document instead of using the in-app router; the gutter measurements in touch.ts have drifted from what the DOM actually renders, causing a note-alignment regression on the day view; the hover end-to-end test still passes even with the feature deleted; the docs are stale; plus one more.\n  - 9 optional suggestions.\n- After review: Grok made the fixes, then merged master in and took odu's checks to green on both Linux and macOS.\n- The brief and the human's reference screenshots were staged at `.worktrees/wf-refs/` (BRIEF.md, olai-current-dark.png, workflowy-full.png, workflowy-hover-closeup.png); the agent's own evidence screenshots landed in `.worktrees/wf-refs/out/`.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 from the human's side-by-side screenshots (olai in dark mode next to Workflowy).\n- **Goal:** make olai's visual language match Workflowy exactly — bullets, checkboxes, spacing, line height, fonts, tag styling, the outline's left margin and content gutter, and the sidebar tree, hover affordances included.\n\n**Decisions made by the human on 2026-08-11, all resolved:**\n- (1) Colours ride olai's existing theme system — the Workflowy-derived palettes in theme/palettes.ts already mirror Workflowy's themes, so this item changes shapes, metrics and typography, not the colour tokens.\n- (2) Hover: the collapse triangle becomes hover-only, as in Workflowy, and a new ••• hover menu appears to the left of the triangle.\n  - Scope limit set by the orchestrator: the menu carries only actions the browser can already perform (zoom, expand/collapse, copy link). No writing or editing in this pull request — the web client has no write operations yet — but it is structured so future actions can slot in.\n- (3) Bullets: exact match — a filled round dot, a grey circular halo when the node is collapsed (that is, has hidden children), and clicking the bullet zooms into the node.\n- (4) Font: Workflowy's actual font, identified from their shipped CSS, fetched and served through Nix itself — no CDN, and no font binary committed to the repository.\n- (5) Tags render as Workflowy renders them: a highlighted pill around the tag text.\n- (6) The sidebar tree matches Workflowy's sidebar styling too.\n- Key files: web/src/client/Tree.tsx, Bullet.tsx, Checkbox.tsx, NodeLine.tsx, touch.ts, styles.css's @theme block, theme/palettes.ts, Sidebar.tsx.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:28:40-04:00"}
{"id":"dates-all-views","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a1","title":"Calendar & today derive from ALL dates — dated marks included","done":"2026-08-11T17:47:08-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #103**, squash-merged as commit cbc636e; the done mark carries the merge instant. Final commit before merge: 58e5e17, after two review rounds and two rulings from the human folded in:\n- Only a node's `date` field, or a `done` mark that carries a date, place it on a day. The broader \"any dated mark places it\" rule was retracted as the orchestrator's own extrapolation, and a test now pins that it does NOT happen.\n- Only `set_done` stamps a date (an instant with the local UTC offset, never a bare `true` and never a day without a time). The `doing` and `todo` marks store a bare `true`.\n- Archived nodes keep their days.\n- A test gap under the UTC timezone was closed against the reviewer's deliberately-planted fake implementation.\n- All 20 required checks in odu (the project's continuous-integration tool) passed on both platforms.\n\n**Handed to an agent on 2026-08-11**: Kolu terminal `90cd9d21` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved), working in the git worktree `.worktrees/dates-from-marks` on branch `dates-from-marks`; brief at `.worktrees/dates-from-marks-BRIEF.md`.\n- At the fix-up step, pull request #103 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/103) was open with the implementation, the two standing cleanup passes, and the /simplify pass (the project's automated quality-cleanup command) pushed.\n- 525 unit tests and 159 end-to-end scenarios passed locally; evidence screenshots in `.worktrees/dates-refs-out/`.\n- The reviewer was Claude — the human's pick on 2026-08-11, overriding the usual rule of reviewing with a different model than the author — working in a side-by-side terminal.\n- The author flagged for the orchestrator: the /simplify pass added `./testlib` to the exports of the @olai/ops package, mirroring what @olai/format does.\n- No continuous-integration run yet, per the pipeline order.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human, right after the migration that turned done marks into datetimes (commit 49d11d9).\n- docs/format.md says the journal, calendar and today views are derived from the `date` field at view time. But a mark (done/doing/todo) may itself carry an ISO date or datetime, and the roadmap now stores completion instants in `done` — so shipped work had vanished from the calendar.\n- **The requirement, in the human's intent:** the views must take ALL dates into account, including the done date.\n  - A node completed at some instant appears on that day's page and lights up that calendar day exactly as a node scheduled for that date does.\n  - A node carrying both dates appears on both days.\n  - Day pages let the reader quietly tell \"scheduled on\" from \"completed on\".\n- docs/format.md was updated to the new truth.\n- **Second half**, added by the human on 2026-08-11 and given to the same agent: the MCP done operation stamps the completion datetime automatically.\n  - `set_done` stores the current instant with the local UTC offset, never a bare `true`, never a day without a time.\n  - Existing `true` and day-only values already in files keep loading and rendering (they are validated as text; the write path must not rewrite other nodes' values).\n  - Whether `doing` and `todo` should stamp too was left to the agent to decide and justify — the human only mandated it for `done`.\n\n**Review done** by Claude in a side-by-side terminal (`3e65a774`) on 2026-08-11: the brief was met; 527 unit tests and 169 end-to-end tests reproduced green; behaviour was portable across four timezones; and a real olai MCP call round-tripped a file byte for byte.\n- **Two required fixes:** the branch's diff reverts docs/roadmap.jsonl, because the orchestrator's rebase had rewritten master underneath it (resolve wholesale in master's favour); and the promise that a stamp never ends in `Z` is not pinned by any test under the UTC timezone.\n- Seven optional suggestions.\n- **The human's decisions on 2026-08-11 for the two escalated questions:** only `done` stamps a date — `set_doing` and `set_todo` go back to storing a bare `true`; and archived nodes keep their days on the calendar and day pages (dimmed is fine), as a decided, tested behaviour.\n- Opus folded the fixes in, then merged master and took odu's checks to green.\n\n**Ruling** by the human on 2026-08-11, on seeing /d/2026-08-11 in the running branch: the \"any dated mark places a node on a day\" rule was the orchestrator's extrapolation in the brief and was never ratified — it is retracted.\n- Only the `date` field and a dated `done` mark place a node on a day page or light up a calendar day.\n- Dates on `doing` and `todo` marks are ignored by the views entirely (no row, no badge), though they remain valid in the file format.\n- \"When does this node happen\" therefore reduces to date-or-done, and a test pins that the other cases do NOT appear.\n- Correspondingly, the stored day-only dates on `doing` and `todo` marks in this roadmap file were scrubbed back to a bare `true` in this commit.\n- Opus reworked it, then merged master and took odu's checks to green.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:28:40-04:00"}
{"id":"wf-gutter-debt","parent":"feat-mouse","ord":"a0","title":"Workflowy gutter follow-ups #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:44:54-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 at PR #102's merge; the human ratified the reviewer nice-to-haves the author skipped (rationale on the PR conversation).\n- **Decomposed 2026-08-13** (the human approved, for the night slate) into the three child items below\n- Order: Kobalte menu primitive + shared dismiss helper first, then (after edges) the touch affordance and the polish batch — since all three touch NodeMenu\n- This parent is done when the children are\n","see":["wf-styling"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"sidebar-collapsed","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a4","title":"Sidebar tree collapsed by default","done":"2026-08-11T18:01:18-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #105**, squash-merged as commit 163c9b3; the done mark carries the merge instant.\n- The stored setting tracks which directory paths are EXPANDED (a path that is absent is shut), which is the inverse of how the outline tree stores its folds.\n- The reader's toggles are stored in this browser only and are never wiped.\n- The directory chain leading to the currently open file is force-opened without writing into the stored set, using a pure `ancestorDirs` helper that has unit tests.\n- End-to-end tests pin three behaviours: a fresh page is collapsed; a nested chain is expanded to the selection; and it re-collapses when the selection moves elsewhere.\n- All 20 checks in odu (the project's continuous-integration tool) passed on both platforms; merged under the human's pre-approval, with the review round waived.\n\n**Filed and handed to an agent on 2026-08-11** at the human's request: Kolu terminal `5c5481e8` (Grok, permissions auto-approved), working in the git worktree `.worktrees/sidebar-collapsed`.\n- Directory nodes in the sidebar's file tree start collapsed.\n- A reader's own stored toggle wins over that default.\n- The active file's directory chain expands automatically so the selection is never hidden.\n- End-to-end tests pin \"fresh page is collapsed\" and \"nested selection is expanded\".\n- **Process, set by the human:** no review round; the squash merge was pre-approved once the automated checks were green — tests plus continuous integration are the whole gate.","see":["sidebar-tree"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"daily-notes","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a2","title":"Daily notes: a date-named document IS the day's note — detected, composed into the day view","done":"2026-08-12T17:48:09-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #127**, squash-merged as commit f3fb7dd on 2026-08-12. A view-only first version, with all four ratified decisions intact:\n\n**(1) Detection by naming convention:**\n- A document whose filename (without extension) is exactly an ISO date IS the daily note for that date, wherever it lives. The vault's Daily/YYYY/MM/ layout matches automatically; 2026-08-10-recap.md, 2026-8-12.md and 20260812.md do not.\n- If two files claim the same date, both are listed in path order; no conflict rule was invented.\n- The date is derived in exactly one place, beside the existing dated-days derivation, in format/src/dates.ts. A search found no second place that parses a date out of a filename, and loosening the pattern turns two tests red.\n\n**(2) Composition:**\n- The /d/<date> and /today pages render the note FIRST, through the existing HTML-sanitising pipeline (verified with a cross-site-scripting probe).\n- The groups of dated nodes below are unchanged; empty days stay inert.\n\n**(3) Calendar:**\n- A calendar cell has four states: nothing, nodes, a note, or both.\n- The note marker is a different shape from the node dot in both light and dark themes.\n- Days that only have a note are clickable, and the accessibility labels carry both facts (an optional suggestion from the reviewer, taken).\n\n**(4) Relative links:**\n- A link from one .md file to another routes to the in-app /doc page, resolved against the note's own directory even when it is being shown on a day page.\n- Links that climb with ../ were verified.\n- A link to a missing target honestly does nothing.\n- Links with a #fragment degrade to plain navigation; the stated reason for not handling them was verified true.\n\n- The \"the journal is a query\" doctrine was amended in architecture.md: the note JOINS the query's answer, it never replaces it.\n- **Reviewer:** nothing required fixing; all four optional suggestions were taken (accessibility labels, a stale header comment in Calendar.tsx, an end-to-end test for the two-claimants case, and the two non-ISO filenames named in the brief that must be refused).\n- **The continuous-integration trouble:** the failing Linux end-to-end runs turned out to be the health of the build machines, not the branch. The machines kolu-ci-3 and kolu-ci-4 run about 30% slower and make timing-sensitive scenarios flaky on master too — the author proved this by running the branch and master on those machines themselves.\n  - It was green on a healthy machine: 20 of 20 checks on both platforms, plus one final run after merging master reset the checks. That wasted run is exactly what the CLAUDE.md rule \"only merge master when there are conflicts\" now prevents.\n- The human approved it on 2026-08-12.\n- **Flagged and awaiting the human:** the health of the build-machine pool itself — slow machines fail unrelated pull requests.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"preferences-panel","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a1","title":"Preferences panel: themes, done-visible, and friends in one place","done":"2026-08-12T22:49:23-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #131** (commit 999dcec), squash-merged 2026-08-13.\n- One place for the browser's preferences, using kolu's SettingsPopover shape over olai's deliberately browser-local preference.ts.\n- Everything lives in localStorage, carries across tabs via the storage event, and is never sent to the server — that promise is written in the panel's own footer text.\n\n- **Contents, version 1:**\n  - Theme: the fifteen palettes as chips coloured with their own colours. The old standalone picker was deleted (Picker.tsx is gone).\n  - Done-visible: this is the DEFAULT; any individual page's own toggle still wins, and that precedence is pinned by an end-to-end test.\n  - Home: the app header, as a fourth control.\n- **Review:**\n  - One finding the reviewer required to be fixed — a real keyboard regression: the panel opened in a portal that left keyboard focus on the trigger button, so pressing Tab never entered the panel. Fixed by moving focus, with a test; and verified by deliberately breaking the fix (deleting the `queueMicrotask` focus call leaves the caret on the trigger and the assertion fails).\n  - Three optional suggestions taken: a test for the Done setting in a second tab (deliberately breaking the code proved this was a real hole); a focus ring on the theme chips in the accent colour; and two stale comments.\n  - Two suggestions were skipped on principle, and the reviewer accepted the reasoning: the muted hint text's contrast is the app's existing contract for muted text, and widening contrast.test.ts to all fifteen palettes would be a NEW promise — architecture.md only pins WCAG AA contrast for one palette. Both are decisions about the palettes as a whole, not fixes to this panel.\n  - The reviewer re-checked the changes made in response and confirmed they were correct.\n- **Conflict round:** pull request #130 had moved master; one conflicting import block was resolved keeping both sides' truths. All 345 end-to-end tests passed; the continuous-integration run was 10 of 10.\n- **Follow-up filed:** preference-receptacle — six different spellings of read → signal → write → watch want one shared `createPreference` helper.\n- **Evidence, checked by the orchestrator:** the panel in the header; a theme flip from chalk to pitch; the Done row showing both hint states; the panel on a 390pt-wide phone; and keyboard Tab landing on the first chip with the accent ring. Archived to the session scratchpad.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"agenda","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a3","title":"Agenda: due dates as a reading — one overdue predicate, one /agenda page","done":"2026-08-12T20:11:56-04:00","desc":"Due dates got a user interface: a derived \"overdue\" test and an `/agenda` page — the forward-looking half of the journal that the day pages serve backwards. Shipped as pull request #128 (commit ffc1cdf), squash-merged 2026-08-13.\n\n- **The design as ratified:** no new field. A `date` plus a mark is the whole reading — a `date` on its own means an occurrence and is never late; a `date` together with `todo` or `doing` means work that is due. A node is overdue when it is marked todo or doing AND the day part of its date is before today. That rule is written once, in packages/format/src/agenda.ts, comparing the first ten characters of the date strings. The `/agenda` page has three sections: Overdue (oldest first), Today (excluding done items), and Upcoming (only days that have something, with the headings linking to day pages), all grouped by outline file. The date badge takes the attention colour wherever the overdue rule holds.\n- **Rulings honoured** (the human, 2026-08-12): a node marked `doing` CAN be overdue; plain occurrences never appear in Overdue and are never coloured amber; `done` items appear nowhere on the page; undated tasks are absent; the empty state says nothing; and there is no snooze or recurrence. One extension the reviewer noticed, consistent with having one rule: an archived `todo` with a past date IS overdue — the exemption archived nodes get belongs to being blocked, not to being late.\n- **Review:** nothing required fixing, across five separate lines of investigation. The reviewer deliberately broke the overdue rule in two ways and confirmed the tests went red both times. All six optional suggestions were taken — `isOverdue` now slices `today` (the datetime-vs-date hazard), plus five corrections to documentation and prose — and the reviewer re-checked the changes and confirmed them.\n- **Continuous integration:** Linux green; macOS skipped under the standing rule for platform-neutral pull requests. That skip revealed that olai's branch-protection ruleset hard-required nine macOS checks; they are no longer required (ruleset 20608873 edited 2026-08-13).\n- **Design:** docs/brainstorming/agenda.md.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00","custom":{"superseded-by-356":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: #356 narrowed membership after this closed — the page lists dated todo/doing ONLY (agenda.ts:20,:248); occurrences left the page entirely, not just Overdue"}}
{"id":"depth-pass","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a2","title":"Depth pass: three altitudes — elevation replaces borders across the app","done":"2026-08-13T11:22:17-04:00","desc":"Ratified 2026-08-12 from the design artifact (https://claude.ai/code/artifact/c22b5168-ecf5-43f3-876e-d32e54c4f344).\n- **The flatness diagnosis:** everything at altitude zero\n- **The five moves:** three altitudes; elevation replaces borders; accent spent not sprinkled; interactive lifts; the paper is the hero\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-13 by the human's own hand: PR #136** (branch surfaces, squash befa8dd) — \"The chrome sits on a desk: three surfaces, and a type of our own\" — authored and merged by the human directly, superseding both orchestrated attempts.\n- Attempt 1: PR #134 (branch depth), closed — right tokens, failed the human's visual review on real data (nine findings)\n- Attempt 2: PR #135 (branch depth2, Grok), closed — addressed all nine, orchestrator-verified, rejected in favor of the human's own take\n- Both branches remain on origin as prior art\n- Follow-up still filed: quiet-pill-unify\n- The website depth touch-up idea was DISCARDED by the human 2026-08-13 — no website restyle follows #136\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"preference-receptacle","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a3","title":"createPreference: one receptacle for the read→signal→write→watch preference spelling","done":"2026-08-13T14:29:06-04:00","desc":"Flagged by pull request #131 (the preferences panel), and made the headline finding of the 2026-08-13 design debate that hunts for volatility worth hiding behind a stable interface (docs/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-13.md, item 1). **Shipped as pull request #142** (squash commit a66154a) on 2026-08-13.\n- One factory function, `createPreference`, in web/src/client/preference.ts: an encoder/decoder per key, one option (`persist: false`, so the drag handles keep their split between the live value and the committed one), and `stored()` as the escape hatch that fold memory's union-merge reads through.\n- Eight preference circuits were migrated: sidebar open state and width, chat open state, width and snap point, folds, sidebar folders, and the done-visible default.\n- The theme deliberately stays OFF this factory, per the debate: the boot script owns the first read before paint, and theme/state.ts keeps its own primitives.\n- A test grips the rule directly: the pattern `/\\b(read|watch)Preference\\s*\\(/` must not appear in any client file outside preference.ts, its own test, and theme/state.ts.\n- The fold semantics shipped in #138 survive word for word — and are now LOCKED. The one finding the reviewer required to be fixed was that deleting the union-merge on the write side left all 24 tests passing; the fix round added a test of `setFolded` against real storage that goes red under exactly that sabotage.\n\n**Pipeline:**\n- Grok reviewed it, verifying the claim that the resize nudge is inert against master's own Solid version, hunting for places where distinct things had been flattened together, and finding only the missing lock.\n- The fix round wrote the failing test before the fix.\n- Optional suggestions: per-setter option tests and the claim-checking regular expression were taken, along with an architecture.md update; re-fitting the resize was declined as a behaviour change, since the point was to keep behaviour identical.\n- The reviewer re-checked the changes made in response and confirmed them.\n- Then pull request #141 landed: master was merged in (one documentation hunk, both sides preserved) and the continuous-integration run was 10 of 10 at commit dc5128d on the kolu-ci-1 machine.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"quiet-pill-unify","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a4","title":"One quiet-pill: the button class string spelled five times across five modules","done":"2026-08-13T17:09:18-04:00","desc":"Flagged by PR #134 (the depth pass); debate 2026-08-13 reclassified it plain cleanup (a class string is not an axis). **Shipped as PR #146 (squash 5c591e6), 2026-08-13**, under the human's pixel-parity-binding ruling.\n- One definition (`web/src/client/pill.ts` QUIET_PILL) worn by the three sites that render identically: chat Header, git Unpushed's dismiss sibling, menu confirm's Cancel — whose bg-transparent dropped only because Tailwind preflight already paints it, verified in built CSS\n- The two sites with a geometry of their own stay divergent with comments saying why: AskForm's h-8 dismiss paired to the accent answer; Unpushed's py-0.5 Push inside a text-xs line\n- Claim test: only pill.ts spells the string (sabotage-verified naming both files)\n- Evidence: all 12 before/after shots byte-identical (cmp clean), chalk+pitch, desktop+phone\n- CI 10/10 on kolu-ci-1\n- Review skipped on the human's word; merged on their approval\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"fold-memory","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a7","title":"Fold memory: the outline remembers how you left it #ui","done":"2026-08-13T11:23:14-04:00","desc":"Shipped as pull request #138 (squash commit 8098561, merged 2026-08-13).\n- Which nodes are folded shut is a preference of this browser, stored under the key `olai.folds` through preference.ts.\n  - The collapsed node ids are grouped by the file that defines them and keyed by NODE ID, so folds survive a reload, a round trip into a zoomed node and back, and moves.\n  - Mirrors fold together with their target, per the ratified ruling — one vocabulary for folding, in fold/rows.ts.\n- Sidebar folders get the same memory (`olai.sidebar.folders`), inverted, because folders start shut.\n- **Grok's review** found one thing that genuinely had to be fixed: the pruning logic treated a MOVE (archiving) as a deletion. It was fixed by writing the failing test first, at both levels, plus a matching test on the write side.\n- Writes from another tab were upgraded from \"last write wins\" (where the most recent tab's whole value overwrites the other's) to a union merge, so an unrelated fold made in a sibling tab survives.\n- The changes made in response to review were re-checked; the continuous-integration run was 10 of 10 green on Linux at the final commit.\n- Documentation was corrected wherever it had encoded the bug's premise.\n- **Note for the preference-receptacle item:** the shared `createPreference` factory was deliberately declined here as out of scope — it remains that item's job.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"done-pref","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a5","title":"The Done pill belongs to Prefs","done":"2026-08-13T20:53:53-04:00","desc":"The human's call, 2026-08-13, from a screenshot of the floating \"Done: Hidden\" pill. **Shipped as PR #151 (squash 8ae7ed87), merged 2026-08-14.**\n- #131 had already put the Visible/Hidden control in Prefs; this PR makes Prefs the ONE home.\n  - The floating pill retires entirely, and the per-page override dies with it (\"a default for a switch nobody can press is a lie\").\n  - The empty-state copy on an all-done zoomed node stays, now the honest explanation rather than duplicate chrome.\n- Rides `olai.done.hidden` on the `createPreference` factory — persisted like theme (the human's requirement).\n  - Cross-tab follow and reload both pinned (reload pin sabotaged red-first: drop the write, reload forgets).\n- **Authored by GROK, reviewed by Claude Opus** (the alternation's other direction).\n  - The review verified the #131 claim, re-ran the author's sabotages.\n  - Demanded the master merge (five files from #147/#148/#152, resolved with the font PR's prefs work preserved).\n- All 15 checks green at c77cd395 including e2e 6m27s.\n  - An earlier local readout claiming five undo reds was a load-era artifact contradicted by the posted statuses.\n- Merged on the human's approval.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"font-pick","parent":"feat-theming","ord":"a6","title":"The typeface is a pick: Atkinson by default, a catalog in prefs #human","done":"2026-08-13T19:25:10-04:00","desc":"Authored and merged personally by the human (PR #152, squash a112993f, 2026-08-13, via their own Grok terminal).\n- Atkinson Hyperlegible becomes the default typeface.\n- Font catalog in the Prefs panel: `theme/fonts.ts` + FontSelect, `fontCss` with its own tests, `nix/fonts.nix` packaging, e2e `fonts.feature`.\n- Rides the `createPreference` factory.\n- **Known ripple at merge time:** #151 (done-pref) went DIRTY on the shared Prefs panel and `preferences.feature` — resolved in that lane's round.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"agenda-fire","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a5","title":"The Agenda entry burns when work is overdue","done":"2026-08-13T21:19:50-04:00","desc":"The human's request, 2026-08-13, from a screenshot of the sidebar. **Shipped as pull request #154** (squash commit 78052f04), merged 2026-08-14.\n- The Agenda entry in the sidebar now burns for overdue work (alarm-coloured ink plus a count badge) and nudges quietly for work due today (a quiet count), and shows nothing otherwise.\n  - When both conditions hold, the louder face wins; the order of the faces is pinned by three unit tests.\n- It is derived from the SAME computation the Agenda page uses — one shared place that decides what is overdue. Deliberately breaking that computation froze the page too, which is how the claim proves itself.\n- It stays live as marks and dates change, pinned by four tests, and works on the phone sheet (a scenario that taps the burger menu).\n- The /simplify pass (the project's automated cleanup command) caught a real painting bug before merge: the alarm text colour was losing to the ordinary ink colour because of the order Tailwind emits its CSS.\n- 18 evidence screenshots (3 states × 2 themes × desktop, phone and close-up), archived.\n- The continuous-integration run was 10 of 10 locally at commit 7e72a68 under the run lock, with the end-to-end suite taking 1m50s on the new parallel runner; no background processes were left behind.\n- Written by Claude Opus (the model was verified at spawn time), merged on the human's approval.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:29:36-04:00"}
{"id":"gutter-menu-primitive","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a0","title":"Menu primitive: Kobalte adoption + shared dismiss helper","done":"2026-08-14T00:51:21-04:00","desc":"Split out of the gutter follow-up bundle on 2026-08-13. **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #158, squash-merged. It ran in the overnight batch of work and was classed SIMPLE, so the orchestrator merged it without waking the human, under the standing overnight authorization.\n- The ••• row menu now uses Kobalte's DropdownMenu component, with the Dots trigger mounted lazily.\n- `dismiss.ts` is a shared helper for outside-click and Escape dismissal, used by `popover.ts` and `note/expand.ts`.\n- **Grok's review** found one thing that blocked merge, plus small suggestions; all were addressed at commit ffc7d70.\n  - The blocker: pressing Escape did not return keyboard focus to the ••• button, because Kobalte's `onCloseAutoFocus` effect never re-runs under the `<Show>` mounting shape used here.\n  - Fixed with an explicit focus restore, plus a scenario for reopening the menu from the keyboard — a path nothing had walked before.\n  - The small suggestions: the rationale comment about focus was rewritten after the real cause was confirmed in a browser.\n- **Evidence re-shot:** the screenshots are byte-identical, or differ by zero pixels, except for the intentionally changed caret band (1.765% of pixels) and the new Escape focus ring (0.075%).\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed.\n- This unblocked the two sibling items: the touch affordance and the menu polish.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"gutter-touch-menu","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a1","title":"Phones reach the ••• menu: long-press (or another touch affordance)","done":"2026-08-14T03:02:35-04:00","desc":"Split out of the gutter follow-up bundle on 2026-08-13. **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #161, squash-merged at commit 2a4a1665. It ran in the overnight batch of work and was classed SIMPLE, so the orchestrator merged it without waking the human, under the standing overnight authorization.\n- A long press on a row now opens the ••• menu on phones, where it had previously been hidden entirely.\n  - This is driven through Chromium's own gesture recogniser, via the browser's debugging protocol, in five phone-tagged scenarios.\n  - One signal in `door.ts` hands out both doors (the hover menu and the long-press menu).\n- The /simplify pass (the project's automated cleanup command) caught that spreading `{...menu.hold}` put the hottest element on Solid's runtime spread path, which re-diffed the class list for every row on every frame.\n- **Grok's review:** nothing blocking, three small suggestions, all addressed.\n  - The claim that the page still scrolls became a scenario proven by mutation: putting `touch-none` on the row makes it fail, as does a scroll position of 0, and nothing else does.\n  - The reviewer's suggested example of calling preventDefault on pointerdown was corrected on the record — what actually claims a gesture is `touch-action`, a captured pointer, or a prevented touchmove.\n  - The half of `swallowGhost()` that deals with title collisions is now asserted.\n  - Three stale bits of documentation were fixed.\n- **Deferrals accepted by the reviewer:** no on-screen hint that the gesture exists (the same price Workflowy pays), and iOS is unverified (there is a `-webkit-touch-callout` rule in the stylesheet, but no real Safari in continuous integration).\n- **Evidence:** the laptop screenshots are byte-identical; only the held-phone screenshot differs.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 2a4a1665.","after":["gutter-menu-primitive"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"gutter-menu-polish","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a2","title":"Menu polish: copy-link toast, z-index plan, NodeMenu file split","done":"2026-08-14T04:11:18-04:00","desc":"Split out of the gutter follow-up bundle on 2026-08-13. **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #163, squash-merged at commit b317637. It ran in the overnight batch of work and was classed SIMPLE, so the orchestrator merged it without waking the human under the standing overnight authorization; it was the last of the nine items in that batch.\n- **Delivered:**\n  - The \"copied link\" / \"copied text\" toast rides `src/client/saying.ts`, sharing one SAID_MS countdown with the Trash remark.\n  - A real two-band z-index scale: the page stack is kept and the sealed numbers are remapped, with a sweep in `claims.test.ts` asserting that no other file spells a `z-*` class.\n  - NodeMenu was split along the seams that adopting Kobalte had exposed (`MenuSaid.tsx` named after `UndoSaid.tsx`).\n- **Grok's review:** nothing blocking, two small suggestions (documentation pointers left stale by the sweeps), and the author fixed three places after checking each pointer against reality.\n- **Evidence:** five layered-chrome scenes byte-identical to master; two sub-pixel differences proven to be run-to-run flakiness by producing identical signatures on the same build.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit b317637.\n- The sibling debt items menu-bundle-cost and dismiss-layer-stack remain recorded and untouched.","after":["gutter-menu-primitive"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"menu-bundle-cost","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a3","title":"Kobalte DropdownMenu rides the first-paint chunk (~24 kB brotli) #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T21:31:30-04:00","desc":"Surfaced by pull request #158 (2026-08-13): adopting Kobalte's DropdownMenu added about 80 kB raw / 23 kB compressed with brotli to the JavaScript chunk loaded on first paint. (The ~85/24 figures in this item's title were the numbers before re-measuring.)\n- **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #171, squash-merged at commit 3ae28cd, from the overnight batch of work, under the human's standing overnight authorization to merge without waking them.\n  - `menu/chunk.ts` is the dynamic-`import()` split point, following the pattern of `markdown/chunk.ts`.\n  - `Panel.tsx` and `Confirm.tsx` follow the menu component into that separate chunk.\n  - `src/client/arriving.ts` (`createArrival`) is a shared helper both chunk files use for the \"waiting for the chunk to arrive\" state.\n  - `features/menu_arrives.feature` pins WHEN the bytes are actually fetched over the network, using `tests/support/chunks.ts`, shared with the markdown tests.\n- **Review:** Grok raised 4 small suggestions and opencode 3; nothing blocking; all were addressed as commits touching only docstrings and tests.\n  - The emitted client code was byte-identical afterwards, so the bundle-size table, six byte-identical screenshots and the network trace all held without re-shooting them.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 3ae28cd.\n- **Leftovers recorded and deliberately not taken:**\n  - `dismiss.ts:53` still keeps about 10 kB raw / 3 kB brotli of Kobalte in the first-paint chunk, because more than 20 first-paint panels import it — the reviewer verified it is not removable.\n  - No prefetch on hover (that is a second request on a different trigger; `menu/Dots.tsx` is where it would go).\n  - The sentence shown when a chunk fails to load has no countdown, matching the decision already made in `Markdown.tsx`.\n- The stray flaky end-to-end test that Grok attributed to `input_widgets.feature:209` got its own bug item.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"dismiss-layer-stack","parent":"wf-gutter-debt","ord":"a4","title":"Dismiss is not one layer stack: Escape with popover + menu up shuts both #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:44:50-04:00","desc":"Surfaced by pull request #158 (2026-08-13).\n- The two callers of `dismiss.ts` were not registered on Kobalte's DismissableLayer stack, so pressing Escape while both a popover and the menu were open closed BOTH instead of only the topmost one.\n- `chat/Sessions.tsx` had no dismissal handling at all.\n- **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #172, squash-merged at commit efe934b5, from the overnight batch of work, under the human's standing overnight authorization to merge without waking them.\n  - `topmost.ts` is the one stack: the popover, the menu, Sessions AND the ⌘K command palette (which the author's own top-down review pass found to be off-discipline — `whileEditing: true` opened it over popovers, and one Escape took both).\n  - The rule that cannot be enforced by the compiler is written down where the stack lives: a layer answers a dismissal that came from the document or from later in the stack, never one from inside its own box.\n- **Review:** Grok and opencode, nothing blocking.\n  - opencode walked combinations the end-to-end suite does not: three layers at once, and the phone viewport.\n  - All five small suggestions were fixed as separate commits, including a `dismiss_stack` scenario that opens the layers in reverse order.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit efe934b5. Two menu scenarios that fail on master pass here.\n- **Leftovers on the record:**\n  - The `palette/Shortcuts.tsx` modal still ignores Escape — a missing affordance of the same class Sessions had; both reviewers agreed it deserves its own item.\n  - SlashMenu's capture handler swallows Enter presses aimed elsewhere (pre-existing, judged against the new rule during review).\n  - Mobile scrims where one tap dismisses two things: both reviewers walked it and concluded the picker riding inside the sheet's dismissal is lawful, because it IS in the sheet.\n  - There is no unit test for `topmost.ts`, because Solid's server build does not propagate signal-to-memo updates. It is held instead by end-to-end tests and two sweeps in `claims.test.ts`, following the precedent set by `arriving.ts`; Grok endorsed this.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"sidebar-pierre-tree","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a5","title":"The directory tree gets file icons: adopt @pierre/trees, Workflowy-exact or fall back to icons-only","done":"2026-08-15T00:22:04-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-14 by the human, from a sidebar screenshot in which markdown files, olai outlines and folders were indistinguishable.\n- Ruled the same night: adopt the `@pierre/trees` component library, with a pre-approved fallback of just adding icons if it did not work out.\n- **Merged 2026-08-15** as pull request #174, squash-merged at commit 8ea77c16, from the overnight batch of work under the human's standing overnight authorization. The FALLBACK was taken — not because of the wall we expected, but on measured evidence.\n  - Styling could pierce the shadow DOM fine. But once pierre was mounted inside the real sidebar it was declined on four measured problems, each reproduced by both reviewers (Grok's 62 kB figure came from a byte-identical rebuild):\n    - Its rows are never anchor elements, whereas every entry in this column is a real `<a>` link.\n    - It virtualizes rows at fixed heights, which truncates text where `fileTree.ts` wraps it.\n    - It has an internal scrolling region, against the app's one-scroll-region rule.\n    - It ships 252,776 bytes raw / 62,250 brotli-compressed of Preact onto a surface that is painted first.\n  - A fifth reason, for whoever tries again: expansion emits no event and there is no `getExpandedPaths`, so persisting folds would require polling.\n- **Delivered instead:**\n  - `client/file/icons.tsx` — the folder and document icons copied verbatim from pierrecomputer/icons (Apache-2.0 licence, with attribution and a note in the file header about when a real NOTICE file becomes owed).\n  - The olai outline icon drawn to that icon set's own metrics; it uses `currentColor` so the accent and muted colours follow the row's text.\n  - The rail's divergent icons were unified — the app had had two answers to \"what does an outline look like\". A scenario now pins both faces, proven by deliberately breaking it.\n  - The bundle grew by 2,009 bytes raw / 776 brotli at the merged commit; the rule that such measurements must share a base is now stated beside the table in `packages/web/README.md`.\n- **Review:** nothing blocking; five small suggestions all fixed or answered on the record.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 8ea77c16; 590 end-to-end scenarios ran at the merged commit.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"shortcuts-modal-escape","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a6","title":"The shortcuts modal has a scrim but ignores Escape","done":"2026-08-15T17:58:54-04:00","desc":"Found by PR #172's altitude pass and both its reviewers (2026-08-14): `palette/Shortcuts.tsx:22` is a modal with a scrim and no Escape dismissal at all — the same missing-affordance class `chat/Sessions.tsx` was before #172 fixed it.\n- Deliberately not folded into #172 (new affordance, not stack discipline; author + both reviewers concurred it is its own item).\n- The fix joins `topmost.ts`'s stack like every other dismissable.\n- **Closed 2026-08-15:** #172 delivered the fix after all (commit 138c66af touches `Shortcuts.tsx` — it joins `topmostWhileOpen` and answers Escape on the window; the file's own header documents it).\n- Human confirmed Escape dismisses the dialog.\n- The \"kept as its own item\" plan was overtaken before this node was ever picked up.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"html-preview","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a3","title":"PR: .html joins the previewable types — through a registry future types register in once","done":"2026-08-15T17:46:07-04:00","desc":"Filed and handed to an agent on 2026-08-15 by srid, and put in front of him for a decision the same day. Pull request #189; the continuous-integration checks were green after merging in the master that carried the Escape-key fixes.\n\n- The registry is real: adding a future previewable file type is one entry, and a sweep test goes red if a file suffix is handled outside it.\n- The sandbox held against every fresh attack both reviewers tried.\n- **Best fix from the round of small suggestions:** the Content-Security-Policy is now asserted as an equality between parsed sets of directives, rather than by matching substrings.\n  - The substring version would have let `img-src data:` straight through — proven by deliberately introducing exactly that example.\n- The empty `sandbox=\"\"` attribute now carries its warning ON the attribute line, where someone reading only the diff will see it.\n- **Awaiting srid** under his rule that deferrals need his word; four deferrals were posted in full on the pull request:\n  - (1) No pictures inside a previewed .html — the policy is `img-src 'none'`. Closing that means parsing foreign markup into /media rewrites, which is exactly where a preview could start serving files nobody meant to serve.\n  - (2) Every .html body stays resident for the life of the process, with no cap — memory scales with vault content.\n  - (3) A fixed 70dvh frame height, because sandboxed frames cannot report their own height.\n  - (4) The `.htm` extension is unclaimed; it is one line on the day somebody has one.\n- **Two judgement calls flagged for his eye:** the file kind is called `hypertext` in the source but \"page\" in the one user-facing sentence; and `write_document` and the `doc` field stay markdown-only.\n- Kolu terminal `1ae9b6fc` was left idle until he ruled.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00"}
{"id":"html-images","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a4","title":"PR: pictures inside a previewed .html — /media rewrites under the sealed frame's CSP","done":"2026-08-16T10:43:54-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): the sealed frame ships img-src 'none', so a saved page's pictures do not draw.\n\n- Closing it means parsing foreign markup to rewrite src into /media/… — exactly where a preview could become a way of serving files nobody meant to serve.\n- The rewrite must be allowlist-shaped: vault-relative only, no traversal, no remote fetch.\n- Security review non-negotiable when taken.\n- **Receipt:** docs/format.md:120, sealed.ts:44.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/201"}}
{"id":"html-frame-height","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a5","title":"PR: the sealed frame sizes to its page — 70dvh stops being a guess #techdebt","done":"2026-08-16T08:43:21-04:00","desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): a script-less sandboxed frame cannot report content height, so Hypertext.tsx fixed it at 70dvh.\n\n- **RESOLVED** by PR #197.\n- The old rule here — \"the empty sandbox attribute is not negotiable\" — is AMENDED by the human's ruling (2026-08-16, \"accepted and approved\").\n  - The frame runs sandbox=\"allow-scripts\" with allow-same-origin forbidden by name and by e2e test.\n  - Execution sealed to one hash-pinned measuring script under default-src 'none'.\n  - A load-count gate re-seals the frame if a previewed page navigates it away (grok's review found that delta; it is closed and probe-tested).\n- **Not negotiable now:** allow-same-origin never joins the token list.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/197"}}
{"id":"seal-base-uri","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a6","title":"Decide whether the HTML preview's security policy also locks down its base URL","done":"2026-08-16T14:40:15-04:00","desc":"Answered by pull request #206, found while sweeping through open deferrals on 2026-08-16.\n\nThis item asked whether the sealed HTML-preview frame's Content-Security-Policy should add a `base-uri` directive (which stops a previewed page from redirecting relative links by declaring a different base URL) alongside the `form-action` directive it already had. The preview rebuild in #206 added `base-uri 'none'` to that policy — it became necessary once the seal stopped winning by the order of elements in the document tree — which is exactly the second protection this item asked to have decided.\n\nThis item was originally deferral 1 of pull request #201.","created":"2026-08-16T10:35:10-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:30:30-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/206"}}
{"id":"preview-pictures-coverage-gaps","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a7","title":"Preview pictures: the named test-coverage gaps #techdebt #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T16:00:26-04:00","desc":"#201's own report names these gaps; both reviewers ruled them bounded and correctly deferred. Filed here so they're tracked rather than left in prose.\n- No e2e for `srcset`, `<picture><source>`, or CSS `background: url()` — they work by consequence of the shared base; only unit-level agreement is asserted.\n- A `loading=\"lazy\"` picture arriving after load keeps its pre-load height (clamped by CSS) — named in the seal's comment, untested.\n- No component-level unit test for the two-readings height logic (this client has no harness for Hypertext.tsx) — covered by the held-picture e2e and its negative control.\n- **Not included here:** the relative-links cost — that's its own bug, `html-preview-relative-links`.","created":"2026-08-16T10:35:15-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"201","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/227"}}
{"id":"preview-body-not-shipped","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a8","title":"A previewed page's body crosses the wire twice — the page only needs to know the file changed #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T03:57:34-04:00","desc":"#206's deferral 4, sharpened by its #204 merge.\n- To preview a .html, the page's subscription makes the server read and ship the whole body over the websocket — and then the frame fetches the same bytes again from the `/media` route, which is the copy actually drawn.\n- The websocket copy is only used to notice the file changed on disk.\n- **Fix:** a head/revision-only member on the documents collection (the page learns \"it changed\" without the bytes) — and #204's own client notes such a change should be measured, not guessed at.","created":"2026-08-16T13:46:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"206","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/219"}}
{"id":"quiet-outline","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"a8","title":"PR: The Quiet Outline — titles first, notes behind a ¶, tags inline but quiet","done":"2026-08-16T17:21:00-04:00","desc":"**The decluttering proposal** the human commissioned 2026-08-16 (artifact \"The Quiet Outline\", rev 2), dispatched with its two rulings baked in:\n- Tags stay INLINE in the title Workflowy-style (quieted by contrast, never boxed, never moved to a column).\n- Nothing table-shaped anywhere.\n\n**Six moves, one PR:**\n- Notes fold behind a small ¶ per row, opened by click/space, with a view-wide density switch Compact/Cozy/Open (Open = today's always-shown behavior, kept).\n- The outline column caps near 80 characters, opened notes wrap near 62.\n- Tags render dim inline, brightening on hover/open; one hot fact may ride inline after the title (pr number on shipped work, fraction on a branch).\n- Top-level sections get a heavier name with the fraction beside it and stick while their branch scrolls.\n- One fixed-width glyph column — bullet, todo, doing, done — with doing the only accented glyph.\n- Done rows dim and a collapsed branch says \"+N done\" instead of hiding silently.\n\n**Open state** has exactly three inline layers:\n- Title line.\n- A properties run (key value pairs, dot-separated, one dim wrapping line — a byline, not a form).\n- The note.\n\n**Pipeline note:** this lane carries an extra #human gate — the human reviews the design (screenshots) after implement, BEFORE any reviewer agent is spawned.","created":"2026-08-16T14:22:55-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:39-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/210"}}
{"id":"search-relative-dates","parent":"search","ord":"a0","title":"PR: date: understands relative words — yesterday, last-week, this-month #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T02:24:41-04:00","desc":"Today date: takes only literal dates and ranges (2026-08, 2026-08-01..2026-08-14). Add the relative vocabulary — yesterday, last-week, this-month — resolved against the server's today, refused verbatim like every other bad operator value.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T02:24:42-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/217"}}
{"id":"search-quoting-or","parent":"search","ord":"a1","title":"PR: quoted phrases and OR join the search grammar #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T03:37:12-04:00","desc":"Today every word is AND-ed and matched alone; \"exact phrase\" quoting and an OR between terms are the two compositions people reach for first. One grammar change, both faces, refused verbatim on bad input like the rest.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T03:37:12-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/220"}}
{"id":"search-is-blocked","parent":"search","ord":"a2","title":"PR: is:blocked finds what is waiting on something #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T01:31:31-04:00","desc":"The format already derives blockedness (Derived.blocked, drawn on every row) — the operator just reads it. is:blocked = the node is waiting on at least one unfinished task; -is:blocked composes like every operator.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:29-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T01:31:32-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/215"}}
{"id":"search-everywhere","parent":"search","ord":"a3","title":"PR: the day, agenda and trash pages honor the filter box like the outline pages do #deferral","done":"2026-08-17T05:17:24-04:00","desc":"Filter-in-place works on outline pages; the day page, the agenda, and the trash ignore the same box. Same grammar, same ancestors-kept behavior, one filter meaning everywhere.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-17T05:17:25-04:00","custom":{"from":"168","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/221"}}
{"id":"search-saved","parent":"search","ord":"a4","title":"PR: saved searches — name a query, get it back with one click #deferral","done":"2026-08-20T11:53:32-04:00","desc":"A named query the reader can re-run — where it is stored (client preference vs a node in the vault) is the one design question to argue in the PR; the vault-node answer would make saved searches shareable and agent-visible for free.\n- **The storage question was answered by Pins before this lane ran** (format.md §Pins): a pin is a node whose title is an address, and `?q=` on one is how a saved query is a pin at all — one click back, shareable (a file), agent-visible (add_node/move_node/trash_node).\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — #282 (Opus, merged fae7a1446; grok + opencode do-not-object).** The missing slice was NAMING where the thought arrives: ⌘⇧P / ⌘K \"Pin this page\" on a page carrying `?q=` asks for a name in the palette's own box (placeholder = the derived name; Enter with nothing pins unnamed as before; Enter with words writes `[name](/…?q=…)` in ONE op; Escape writes nothing; the chord is dead while its own question stands). A shelf row's ✎ asks the same question and resolves to `set_title` — the op an agent sends. `@olai/format` gained `pinTitle` as `linkedTitle`'s inverse (one spelling on both sides of the wire; a `]` in a name is refused, a `(` in the address escaped); the `pin` verb gained an optional `name`; no new record shape, field or client store.\n- A bug the screenshot found and fixed: the shelf's `×` had never appeared on hover (group-hover sat on the `<Link>`, controls were its siblings).\n- The human used it on this vault during the lane: `[created today](/roadmap.olai?q=created%3Atoday)` is on the shelf.\n- Not this slice, by design: a pin's QUERY is not a search operator; no naming door on a node's ••• (a node's name is its live title); no shelf •••.","created":"2026-08-16T14:35:37-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T11:53:32-04:00","custom":{"from":"168"}}
{"id":"kolu-preview-packages","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"a9","title":"Adopt pieces of kolu's file-preview packages? #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"- Question from #209: should olai use kolu's solid-browser / solid-fileview?\n- Assessment: no wholesale adoption. Olai's preview iframe does more than kolu's plain one, and the history controller isn't needed.\n- Two pieces worth taking:\n  - `resolveRelativePath` — one shared resolver for relative links in rendered markdown, instead of olai's private copy.\n  - The image/pdf/video renderers, if media files ever get their own pages (today a png or pdf in the vault has nowhere to open).\n- Waiting on the human's pick.","created":"2026-08-16T14:39:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T16:08:01-04:00","custom":{"premise-update":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: \"a png or pdf in the vault has nowhere to open\" is no longer true — #362 gave csv/pictures/pdf pages and kinds-table rows. The live remainder narrows to VIDEO (still absent from kinds.ts) and the resolveRelativePath half"}}
{"id":"preview-links-deferrals","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"aA","title":"What #209 left open, each its own call","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:40-04:00","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"209"}}
{"id":"preview-olai-links","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a0","title":"A preview's link to a sibling .olai outline should open that outline in Olai #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:20-04:00","desc":"#209 claimed .html and .md siblings; an .olai link keeps #206's in-frame behavior because an outline is not a bodied kind — its page is /o/, its existence check a different list. A second route and membership question, same navigate-the-app shape as #209.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T14:15:06-04:00","custom":{"from":"209","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/209"}}
{"id":"doc-page-anchors","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a1","title":"The document page cannot land on an anchor — so fragment links to siblings stay in-frame #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:24-04:00","desc":"bar.html#section stays the frame's click because /doc/ has no anchor support; #209 backs the kept behavior with a scenario. The honest fix is anchor support on the document page, then fragment links can carry.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T14:15:07-04:00","custom":{"from":"209","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/209"}}
{"id":"preview-open-channel-budget","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a2","title":"The preview's open channel needs a budget — a page naming itself can spam history #deferral #human","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:34-04:00","desc":"Found by #209's refactor passes, confirmed by a verifier, deliberately not fixed.\n- `router.go` pushes a history entry and scrolls to top per message, unbounded.\n- A page naming itself never re-keys DocumentPage, so the frame survives to send again — the WALK_OFFS hazard class on a new channel.\n- **Three candidate rulings, each a behavior change:** refuse the self-open; a budget that outlives one mount; replace over push.\n- Needs the human's pick, then its own scenario.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:01:35-04:00","custom":{"from":"209","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/209"}}
{"id":"preview-dropped-click-silent","parent":"preview-links-deferrals","ord":"a3","title":"A dropped preview click tells the reader nothing #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:29-04:00","desc":"When the host refuses a posted path (not in membership — e.g. a .html under a pruned directory, servable yet unlistable), the click silently does nothing, against HACKING's never-silently-ignore rule. Surfacing it is a behavior change; the odd seam it rides has its own scenario in #209.","created":"2026-08-16T15:54:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T14:15:08-04:00","custom":{"from":"209","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/209"}}
{"id":"backlinks","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aA","title":"Backlinks: a zoomed node shows what references it","done":"2026-08-18T05:20:00-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17; SHIPPED in PR #237.\n\n- **Feature:** zoom into a node and see a \"referenced by\" section — every node whose see-edge points here, plus every node whose title/note mentions this node by its `@<id>` spelling (#228).\n- **Implementation:** the reverse index is one new table in the derivation, `mentionedBy`, keyed by the WORD a title or note writes after an `@` rather than by a node.\n  - This is what lets a mint or a delete cost nothing (existence is asked at the READ, `backlinks.ts`).\n  - See-edges needed no new index; `namedBy` already carried them.\n- Patched per changed file like the rest of the view, so the section follows the disk with nothing reloaded.\n- **RULED**, documented in docs/format.md's new References section:\n  - A mirror is NOT a reference (a placement is a view, not a claim — `read_node`'s mirrors already answers where a node is drawn).\n  - An after/blocks is not one either (the ordering graph, already drawn both ways on the page).\n  - An archived referrer is left out (#226).\n- Collapsed by default, each entry navigates, and `read_node` answers the identical reading as `referencedBy` so a person and an agent are told one thing about one id.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/237"}}
{"id":"drag-across-panes","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aB","title":"Drag across panes: pick a node up in one pane, drop it in the other","done":"2026-08-18T09:30:00-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17, SHIPPED in PR #238.\n\n- **Scope change:** with split panes merged (#225), a drag was still a fact about ONE page; it is now a fact about the WORKSPACE.\n- **Mechanism:** every editable page on screen joins a register as it mounts and leaves with itself (web/src/client/drag/fields.ts).\n  - A lift measures all of them; which pane the pointer is in is decided per move on X alone, because a split is columns (drag/aim.ts, unit-tested).\n- Over a page of the carried file, the arithmetic and affordance are the ones that already shipped — the same `planDrop`, the same drop line with the same `data-parent`/`data-after`/`data-depth` — drawn over whichever pane the pointer is in.\n- **SAME FILE IN BOTH PANES** reconciles for nothing: the write goes to the file and both trees redraw off the one store, so the row leaves one pane and arrives in the other on the same frame.\n- **DIFFERENT FILES is a REFUSAL raised at the AIM, not the write:** the pane fills with the reason before the hand lets go, and the same sentence lands on the selection bar after (drag/Refusal.tsx, one spelling carried on the aim).\n  - Sending the drop and quoting the ops layer would have been wrong twice: the line would have promised a landing the release could not keep.\n  - A drop at the other file's TOP LEVEL names `parent: null` — not a refusable request but a legal move to the top of the row's own file, landing silently in a pane nobody was pointing at.\n- **Two things a second pane broke** (rather than extended), both fixed here:\n  - A `Row.key` is a chain from the roots of ITS page, so `measureLines` now takes the page to read — both row gestures scope to their own pane (unscoped, a drag in the left pane was planned against the right pane's boxes).\n  - \"A branch is never offered a place inside itself\" is asked of the RECORDS in a row's chain plus the page's zoom ancestry, not the key's prefix — across two pages there is no shared path, and the branch a pane is zoomed into is not drawn there at all.\n- No new wire verb, no new op: a drop is still one `place` per row.\n- **Pinned by** features/drag_across_panes.feature (5 scenarios) plus drag/aim.test.ts; the in-pane suite is unchanged and green.\n- **Evidence:** two sections in packages/tests/evidence.ts.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/238"}}
{"id":"agenda-spine","parent":"feat-dates","ord":"a4","title":"The agenda is a spine of time — now is a place on it, and the future recedes","done":"2026-08-18T11:52:08-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-18, from a three-way design exploration.\n\n- **Decision:** the `/agenda` page redraws as ONE continuous spine of time — no OVERDUE/TODAY/UPCOMING section boxes.\n- **Why:**\n  - The flat sections gave every item equal claim on attention (an item 73 days out shouted as loudly as one due Monday).\n  - ISO dates demanded mental subtraction to feel urgency.\n  - The repeated chrome (file heading + crumb per day) outweighed content ~4:1.\n- **Spec:** the design canvas at https://claude.ai/code/artifact/3d13d467-18ed-49e2-8c95-64d9287683ef — opens on the chosen **B · Spine** artboard, drawn in the chalk palette with the app's real row anatomy.\n  - A \"Fog\" and C \"Horizon\" on the same canvas are the rejected alternates (A's distance-fade ramp is folded INTO B).\n\n- **The spine:**\n  - A 2px vertical line in the left gutter running the page's height, its ink a gradient down the flow of time — alarm above the today point, accent at it, then ink → muted → rule fading to transparent in the far future.\n  - Each listed day is a 7px dot on the line, the dot's ink fading with distance (ink, muted, rule); overdue days sit ABOVE the today dot with alarm dots.\n  - TODAY is an 11px accent dot with a paper ring, drawn even when nothing is due on it (muted italic \"nothing due today\") — now is a place on the line, not a section that vanishes.\n- **Days say felt distance, not just dates:**\n  - \"Mon, Aug 24\" + muted \"in 6 days\"; overdue \"Yesterday · Mon, Aug 17\" in alarm ink with the pill reading \"1 day late\"; today \"TODAY · Tue, Aug 18\" in small caps accent.\n  - Rounding is fine (19 days → \"in 3 weeks\"); roughly: under two weeks in days, then weeks, past ~2 months in months-and-halves.\n- **Gaps are content:**\n  - The stretch between consecutive listed days draws a small italic muted label beside the spine (\"two quiet weeks\", \"seven quiet weeks\").\n  - The vertical whitespace grows with the wait — log-ish, never linear, so a 52-day silence is visibly longer than a 2-day one without costing a screen.\n- **Rows fade with distance**: whole entries ramp ~1 → 0.78 → 0.74 → 0.5 opacity as they get further out.\n- **Chrome cut:**\n  - No per-day file heading (the \"Tasks.olai\" over every group goes).\n  - The ancestry trail is one muted line UNDER the row.\n  - The per-row date pill is dropped in the future half (the day label already says the date) and kept only where it adds a fact — the \"14:00\" time on a datetime.\n  - Overdue keeps DateBadge's alarm-tone semantics; occurrences still never amber, `done` still nowhere on the page.\n\n**Where the code goes, for whoever implements:**\n- **Date arithmetic lives in @olai/format** (packages/format/src/agenda.ts, beside `isOverdue`/`agendaOf` — the one place already allowed to compare dates): weekday + relative-label phrasing, and the gap sizing between days.\n  - `DateBadge.tsx` stays verbatim-printing and dumb; its header states the verbatim principle and this is its sanctioned extension — keep the parsing out of components.\n- **AgendaPage.tsx** loses its three `Section`s for the spine layout.\n  - `DayGroups.tsx`/`DayNode.tsx` are SHARED with the day pages, which keep their current reading — branch by surface or give the agenda its own entry renderer over the same `situate`d `DayEntry` data.\n- **Tones are tokens, never hex**: alarm/accent/ink/muted/rule via the theme custom properties (color-mix where a fade is needed) so all fifteen palettes follow.\n- **The scenario contract moves**: `data-section`/`agendaSection` testids disappear or change meaning — reshape packages/tests scenarios in the same PR; `data-overdue`, `data-date`, `data-occasion` keep carrying their facts.\n- **The filter keeps working** (`keepingOwed` narrowing): removed rows leave, gap labels recompute from the days still drawn.\n  - A filtered-empty page says the filter's sentence (never \"Nothing is due.\"), and the sidebar's unnarrowed owed count (`agenda/owed.ts`) is untouched.\n- **Empty agenda** still says \"Nothing is due.\" and draws no spine — a lone today-dot on an empty page was not ruled on; default to none.\n- **Keyed rendering rules hold**: the `Key by=\"date\"` / `by=\"file\"` reasoning documented in AgendaPage.tsx and DayGroups.tsx applies to whatever replaces them.","created":"2026-08-18T10:04:21-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00"}
{"id":"filter-row-says-why","parent":"search","ord":"a6","title":"A filtered row says WHY it is drawn — highlight the hit, dim kept ancestors, excerpt a note-only match","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:19-04:00","desc":"Ruled with the human 2026-08-18, from one screenshot: a `#deferral` tag-click over roadmap.olai.\n\n- Every number and every row was CORRECT, and the page was still confusing, because a filtered page never says why any given row is in front of the reader.\n- **Three cases, all present in that one screenshot:**\n\n**1. A match does not show its needle.**\n- `ir-deferral-tag`'s title merely *talks about* the tag (\"a deferral node wears #deferral\") yet matched exactly like a row that wears it as a label.\n- The format cannot tell use from mention — a `#word` in a title IS a tag, deliberately — so the UI's job is not to distinguish them but to make the reason visible: show WHERE the query landed and the reader resolves the ambiguity themselves.\n\n**2. A kept ancestor looks like a match.**\n- `instructions-reconcile` (\"Update instructions.md to match how we now work\") carries no `#deferral` anywhere; it was drawn only as the ancestor chain leading to `ir-deferral-tag`.\n- Nothing marks it as context rather than result.\n\n**3. A note-only match shows no reason at all.**\n- A row whose hit lives behind the ¶ (matched on `desc`) draws a title containing nothing the query said.\n\n**THE FIX, three parts, all view-time (nothing is stored):**\n- **Highlight the matching substring in a matched row's title** — the classic search-needle treatment, applied to whichever title text (word, phrase, or tag pill) satisfied the query.\n- **Dim rows that are drawn but did not match** — kept ancestors.\n  - The distinction already exists in the model: membership in the `matched` id-set vs merely surviving `keeping`'s prune (`matchedIn` counts exactly this); the row just never draws it.\n- **For a row matched only in its note: draw a one-line excerpt** of the note around the hit, highlighted, under the title (or at minimum light up the ¶ itself).\n  - Explicitly NOT full auto-expansion of the note — ruled out because notes here run to ~1.5KB paragraphs (see `bun-watcher-bump`'s).\n  - The filter re-evaluates per keystroke, so full notes would reflow the page violently while typing, and it would trample the reader's own open/closed state, which would then need saving and restoring.\n  - A clamped excerpt is the same idea with bounded cost.\n\n**IMPLEMENTATION POINTERS (as of master f35af476):**\n- The matcher already knows which field carried a hit — `@olai/format`'s `filter.ts` scores per `SEARCH_FIELDS` (`title`/`id`/`tag`/`desc`) and `search_nodes` hits report `matched`.\n  - But the browser's filter path throws that away: `web/src/client/filter/narrowing.ts` reduces `matching(...)` to a `Set` of ids.\n  - The field (and the needle's position, or just the query's words for the row to re-find) has to ride along to the row — extend what `matching` hands back or derive per drawn row.\n- Title highlighting must hold on BOTH title paths, which are held to each other by test: the no-markdown fast path (`markdown/plain.ts` / `tags.ts`'s `taggedHtml`) and the HAST walk (`title.ts` / `styleTags`).\n  - `plain.test.ts` renders the same titles both ways; the highlight belongs under that same sweep.\n- Matching is case-folded substring over the folds in `filter.ts`'s `foldOf` — a tag is indexed bare AND as written — so highlight offsets must come from the same fold, not a fresh `indexOf` with different case rules.\n- The note excerpt can be plain text (escape, don't render markdown for one clipped line); `desc` is matched folded, so find the hit the same way.\n- The dim-vs-highlight styling wants the same narrowable-page condition tags already key on (`data-narrowable`, `styles.css`) so pages that take no filter change nothing.\n\n**Out of scope here**, filed separately if wanted: the count line's mixed denominators (\"8 of 57 — 17 more matches hidden as done\" reads as 8+17≠57; the 57 is page rows, the 17 is matches).","created":"2026-08-18T11:03:50-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/9ba1c6b5","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/240"}}
{"id":"filter-count-honesty","parent":"search","ord":"a7","title":"PR: the count line says what the page holds, and what is hidden as done #deferral","done":"2026-08-18T00:00:00-04:00","desc":"The deferral `filter-row-says-why` filed, taken.\n\n- **Problem:** the count line's mixed denominators (\"8 of 57 — 17 more matches hidden as done\" read as 8+17≠57) — the 57 was page rows LEFT after the done preference, the 17 was matches it had taken away.\n- **Fix:** the line now says three truths, each only where it applies — how many rows MATCHED and are drawn, of how many the page HOLDS, and how many matched rows are NOT drawn and WHY.\n- The denominator moved from what the preference left (`visible()`) to what the page holds (`all()`), so every part is counted inside one set and a reader who adds them up is not lied to.\n- A part that is zero is not said; \"more\" is dropped when nothing is drawn — because `is:done` under a done-hiding reader draws nothing and \"no matches of 57 — 3 more matches hidden\" contradicts itself.\n- The wording is a function of three numbers (`web/src/client/filter/count.ts`) rather than a JSX binding, so it is unit-tested.\n- The numbers stay `narrowing.ts`'s, off the same three readings the page is drawn from, never a recount that could disagree with the prune it counts.\n- **Still deferred**, named on the PR: the ⌘K palette and the header box draw eight hits of however many matched and say nothing about the rest — `SearchAnswer.total` has been on the wire, uncounted, since the matcher was split out.","created":"2026-08-18T00:00:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T14:15:09-04:00","custom":{"from":"#240 (filter-row-says-why)"}}
{"id":"count-line-honesty","parent":"search","ord":"a8","title":"The count line says what the page holds, and what is hidden as done","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:56-04:00","desc":"Filed and shipped in one overnight lane (2026-08-19, run #3).\n\n- **Feature:** under a filter the count line now says all three truths — matched-of-held (\"2 of 10\", the denominator measured before the done preference takes rows off) plus \"— N more matches hidden as done (Prefs)\".\n- The wording is a pure function (web/src/client/filter/count.ts); the numbers come off the same sets the renderer draws from (filter/drawn.ts), never a recount.\n- **Findings round** made all 31 count-line assertions exact reads (a substring pin would have passed with a spurious hidden clause — both reviewers caught it independently, fix sabotage-proven).\n- **Deferral filed at merge:** the ⌘K palette and header search box draw at most 8 hits and say nothing about a ninth — SearchAnswer.total is on the wire and uncounted (see palette-count-cap).","created":"2026-08-19T10:57:56-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e419c67f","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/248"}}
{"id":"has-repeat-search","parent":"search","ord":"a9","title":"has:repeat joins the filter grammar","done":"2026-08-19T11:00:38-04:00","desc":"Shipped 2026-08-19 (overnight run #3).\n\n- **Feature:** one row in HAS_FIELDS (format/src/filter.ts) — parsed, taught in the refusal, evaluated, negated with the grammar's own dash — reaching all five doors through the single matcher, costing what `has:desc` costs.\n- Both reviewers do-not-object; grok's close: \"Ship it.\"\n- docs/search.md carries the row and the `has:date` containment note.","created":"2026-08-19T11:00:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/a38b0640","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/252"}}
{"id":"empty-trash","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a5","title":"Empty the trash — the one write in olai that deletes","done":"2026-08-19T11:00:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped 2026-08-19 (overnight run #3).\n\n- **Feature:** a new ops op `empty` naming EVERY archive the request empties in one op — the union holder-set holds by construction, list order means nothing.\n- A cross-pile cycle is not a special case (grok's objection, probe-verified, fixed at the root and withdrawn on re-verdict after he re-ran his own topology).\n- **Both faces in one change:** MCP `empty_trash` + the web's Empty trash verb, behind a confirmation counting over the SET and saying plainly that only git remembers.\n- Optional count-shaped `was` checked per planning attempt.\n- Refused while anything outside the Trash still names something in it.","created":"2026-08-19T11:00:42-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (brief-born; captured retroactively)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/557a268e","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/250"}}
{"id":"pin-to-sidebar","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"a8","title":"Pin to sidebar: any node, document, or saved query, one click away","done":"2026-08-19T11:06:26-04:00","desc":"Approved on the evening of 2026-08-18; shipped as pull request #246.\n\n- **The shelf:** `_olai/Pins.olai`. This is the third file olai finds by name (the code that finds it, `pinsIn`, walks the directory the same way `inboxIn` does). It holds ordinary nodes whose titles are addresses.\n  - A bare address draws the live, resolved title of whatever it points at; a markdown link, `[label](address)`, draws the human's own words instead. Ruled 2026-08-19: a chip is fine, pills are not.\n- **No new write operation and no new agent tool:** `read_subtree`, `add_node`, `move_node` and `archive_node` ARE the agent's face for this, and the browser's single \"pin\" action resolves to the same add.\n- The shelf sits below the calendar, beside the file tree (ruled).\n- **Two defects Grok objected to were fixed, each with a test proven to fail first:**\n  - A malformed URL such as `/n/%` raised a URIError that killed the whole sidebar — fixed inside `routeOf`, where typing it in the address bar hits it too.\n  - Reordering used coordinates relative to the list rather than the page.\n- **Future:** minting the Archive and Inbox files into `_olai/` as well (see the olai-convention-dir item).\n\nThe pull request was approved by the human on 2026-08-19.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:44-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e44e8ae3","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/246"}}
{"id":"search-stamp-operators","parent":"search","ord":"aC","title":"created: and changed: read the stamps — plus a sweep for every other field the grammar cannot ask about","done":"2026-08-19T16:13:24-04:00","desc":"Filed and handed to an agent on 2026-08-19, from the human's question: \"how do I search for newly added roadmap items? like added in last 30 mins?\"\n\n- **Today:** the answer is to run `git diff` on the file, because a node record carries `created` and `changed` timestamps (the write layer sets them; node.ts lines 134-148) but `filter.ts` never reads them.\n- **The feature:** `created:` and `changed:` search operators sharing exactly the value grammar `date:` already has (`created:today`, `created:2026-08-19`, `created:last-week..`).\n- A node with no timestamp matches nothing — a node written before the timestamps existed has no past to invent.\n- **Plus, ruled by the human:** an honest survey of every other field in a record that no operator can ask about, implementing the ones with a real use in the same pull request and recording the declined ones with reasons.\n\n**Outcome:** approved by the human on 2026-08-19. The Grok reviewer initially objected, then re-reviewed at commit 1a5df1b6 and withdrew the objection; the opencode reviewer had no objection; and the evidence screenshots were checked visually. Shipped: `created:` and `changed:` with `date:`'s grammar, plus `is:mirrored` found by the survey. `has:blocks` was declined because it needs a reverse index of the derived blocked relation; `has:children` and `has:tag` were deferred.","created":"2026-08-19T14:45:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","branch":"search-stamp-operators","from":"human 2026-08-19","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/224e05c2","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/257","terminal":"a44f2fe4-7edd-4d75-ac5d-437e49b733c4"}}
{"id":"prefs-git-autopush","parent":"feat-git","ord":"a0","title":"Prefs gain a Git section with Auto-push — a commit from this browser is followed by a push","done":"2026-08-20T11:50:23-04:00","desc":"The human's ask, 2026-08-20 10:37: add 'Git' to prefs, with an 'Auto-push' setting — when enabled, olai auto-pushes after committing. Reviewer opencode; merge pre-approved by the human.\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — #283 (Grok, merged b1fb7efdd).** Prefs has a **Git** row with **Auto-push** (default off; browser-local like every other pref, \"sent nowhere\" stays true). When on, a commit from THIS browser's Commit button is followed by the same push the panel's Push button runs (current branch to its upstream, never a force); a failed push shows git's own words in the panel and the commit stands; the `· N unpushed` count stays honest. Agent `commit` ops and `--commit=auto` are untouched — a server-wide policy would be a `--push` flag beside `--commit`, not a pref (stated in the PR body; the human did not redirect).\n- opencode's one finding closed before the merge: the Commit button and `commit()` wait on the same `pushing()` gate as the Push button (`canRecord` in record.ts, pinned), so a commit answered during an in-flight push can no longer silently skip its auto-push.\n- Tests: autopush.test.ts, record.test.ts (mocked push), one Auto-push scenario in committing.feature. Docs: git.md, running.md, web README, website.\n\n**merge** — pre-approved","created":"2026-08-20T10:41:09-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20","merge":"merge-human"}}
{"id":"olai-dir-hidden","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aC","title":"_olai/*.olai are hidden from the sidebar by default","done":"2026-08-20T17:22:54-04:00","desc":"The human's ask, 2026-08-20 13:2x.\n- Today the sidebar's file tree draws `_olai/` as an ordinary folder (visible in #282's shots), so `_olai/Pins.olai` shows up as a row even though the shelf already IS that file's face, and `_olai/Trash.olai` has its own \"Trash\" entry.\n- Rule: outlines under `_olai/` — the files olai names itself (format.md: Pins, Trash, and whatever comes next) — are not drawn in the file tree by default. A **Prefs** switch (\"show hidden outlines\", browser-local like every other pref, off by default) draws them, so a person who wants to open `Pins.olai` as an outline still can; the files stay part of the set either way (search, agents, `list_outlines` unchanged — this is a drawing rule, not a set rule).\n- Docs: editing.md (sidebar + prefs sections), format.md §Pins/§Trash one sentence each, running.md's prefs list; keys/⌘K untouched. Test: a sidebar scenario with the pref off and on.","created":"2026-08-20T13:21:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20"}}
{"id":"inbox-under-olai-dir","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aD","title":"Inbox.olai moves to _olai/, with its own sidebar entry","done":"2026-08-20T17:22:54-04:00","desc":"The human's ask, 2026-08-20 13:2x. **This reverses a documented ruling:** format.md ~line 150 says the inbox \"has deliberately NOT moved under `_olai/`\" (#266's shape); the human now wants it there, beside Pins and Trash, with the sidebar giving it a door of its own the way Trash has one.\n- Minting: a directory with no inbox gets `_olai/Inbox.olai` (today: `Inbox.olai` at the root), by the same single operation that writes the first line into it. Finding: keep reading by NAME, case-insensitively, shallowest first, then path order — so a directory that already keeps `Inbox.olai` at the root, or `notes/inbox.olai`, goes on capturing into the file it has (no migration forced; say whether this vault's own `Inbox.olai`, if any, should be moved by hand as the Archive files were).\n- Sidebar: a dedicated **Inbox** entry (beside Trash, or above the file tree — the implementer argues the spot), opening the inbox outline; with `olai-dir-hidden` in force the file itself is not also a tree row. The entry should read the same resolver `@olai/format`'s `inboxIn` uses, so the web and the MCP (`list_outlines`' inbox rule, ⌘K `+`) never disagree.\n- Docs: format.md (the inbox paragraph rewritten — the \"deliberately NOT\" sentence goes), editing.md (sidebar, ⌘K `+`), list_outlines' tool description in the MCP. Tests: capture with no inbox mints `_olai/Inbox.olai`; capture with a root `Inbox.olai` present uses it; the sidebar entry opens it.","see":["olai-dir-hidden"],"created":"2026-08-20T13:21:55-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20"}}
{"id":"prefs-git-autocommit","parent":"feat-git","ord":"a1","title":"Prefs Git gains Auto-commit — edits settle into commits on their own","done":"2026-08-20T19:58:58-04:00","desc":"The Git prefs section (Auto-push, #283) grows an Auto-commit setting. Goal: with both on, every change syncs to Git with no button. Ruled with the human 2026-08-20: commits record on a debounced flurry — a quiet window after edits stop arriving, one commit per burst — not per-op. Single-user is the shipped case; team hosting (several people + agents writing concurrently) is explicitly deferred — the PR must only be conflict-safe enough not to corrupt (rebase before push, stop and surface on conflict), with the concurrency work filed as a #deferral node from the PR. Merge pre-approved after visual evidence.","created":"2026-08-20T18:16:40-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20 18:2x"}}
{"id":"chat","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a0","title":"Chat #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-10T12:12:41-04:00","desc":"Shipped as pull request #63 (squash commit 66d64ff), the largest pull request of the rewrite, through three review rounds plus the human's own hands-on rounds. What landed:\n- The write-operations layer, the store's write gate, an internal MCP server, and a single long-lived Claude session over the Agent Client Protocol (ACP), keeping the same session semantics the earlier Racket version had.\n- A chat panel at full parity with that version: replies stream in place, tool-call frames stay folded as you left them, tool progress is live, the model name comes from two sources, the transcript is an append-only log with markers between sessions, and slash commands complete with a show-all option.\n- The fact that the agent is busy is visible in three places.\n- Messages typed mid-turn are queued.\n- packages/chat was extracted out of the composition root.\n- A fix for a hang on SIGINT (the interrupt signal sent by Ctrl-C), with a regression test that runs a real process.\n- The mobile burger menu, putting the sidebar and chat one tap away on phones.\n- One pull request delivered the chat panel: an ACP session (via @agentclientprotocol/sdk) projected into surface events, with send, stream and cancel.\n- The write-operations layer is born here (add, mark, move, archive; insertion by fractional ordering key; write to a temp file, re-validate, rename, then commit), because chat's agent is the first writer. Whole-file writes by an agent are still rejected.\n- **Visible result:** ask the agent in the web app to check something off and watch the outline update live; a refused write (because of derived state) shows its unfinished children in the chat.\n\n**Decided 2026-08-09:** the former agent-editing and chat items collapse into this one; MCP tools for external coding agents are moved to their own item.\n\n**Resolved 2026-08-09:**\n- Claude first, through a claude-code ACP adapter.\n- The server owns one single ACP session, because olai is single-user.\n- The channel from the agent to the write layer is an internal MCP server, passed in via the `session/new` call; write tool calls are auto-approved (a permission UI is deferred).\n- The agent reads only through internal MCP query tools (probably including grep), never the filesystem.\n- Transcripts persist as Claude Code's own session for the served directory, so `claude --resume` works from a terminal.\n- Errors are never silently ignored — only a StaleWrite retry that then succeeds is invisible; every genuine failure renders in the chat with structured detail.\n\n**Resolved 2026-08-09, round two:** the earlier version's chat semantics were adopted wholesale (on boot, adopt the most recent session for the working directory via `session/list` plus `session/load`; a session picker; foldable tool frames; slash-command completion; model display; a Nix-pinned adapter) — full parity in the one pull request. Query tools work over parsed nodes with file and line numbers, never raw grep.\n\n**Parity with the earlier Racket version to preserve (audited 2026-08-09):** the session picker (list, switch, new — the old version adopted the most recently updated session on boot); slash-command completion in the chat input; foldable tool calls in the transcript; write operations auto-committing with the old message convention (`capture:`/`done:`/`doing:`/`move:`/`archive:` TITLE) when the directory is a git working tree, with an opt-out; and the archive operation rebuilding the ancestor title chain in Archive.jsonl so that references keep resolving after the move.\n\nBrainstorming: docs/brainstorming/acp.md.","after":["live"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a0","title":"MCP for external agents","done":"2026-08-10T13:16:40-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #68 (squash 64455f7), authored by Claude/Opus, twice re-based over master moves (theming #69, set-fat #70) with conflicts resolved by the agent on instruction: the ops layer as a command an agent launches.\n- **Punted 2026-08-09:** expose the ops layer as MCP tools so a coding agent outside olai (e.g. in a terminal) can edit outlines\n- Chat's internal agent→ops wiring is expected to produce most of this for free","after":["chat"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"create-outline","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a1","title":"Create an outline","done":"2026-08-10T16:36:06-04:00","desc":"Agents cannot start a new outline file: add_node's plan refuses any file not already under the served directory, and no create op exists.\n- **Fix:** ship create_outline(file, seed?): a validated relative .jsonl path (traversal-guarded segment-by-segment like /media/*), refuse an already-existing file, optionally seed one first node (same shape add_node mints), ride the store's temp-file → validate → rename → commit write gate, with the capture:/create: git auto-commit convention when the directory is a work tree\n- Expose through both faces of the ops layer (MCP tools list and therefore chat)\n- Unit tests on the plan refusals; e2e that a live tab shows the new outline without reload","after":["mcp"],"see":["mcp"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"see-refs","parent":"feat-tasks","ord":"a6","title":"see references: rendered, and writable over MCP","done":"2026-08-10T16:26:44-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #79 (squash c04269c).\n- **Filed 2026-08-10 from the leg-pain conversation:** `see` is the format's free cross-reference — validated, and today invisible and unwritable by agents\n- **(1) RENDER:** a node's see refs draw as links to their targets' /n/<id> pages (target's title as the text), on the zoomed page and on rows carrying them — the see half of edges-ui, moved here; edges-ui keeps blocked-derivation\n- **(2) OPS/MCP:** a set_see-shaped op (add/remove see targets on an existing node) through the standard write gate, validation refusing unknown targets with the usual teaching error; MCP read tools (search_nodes, read_subtree) carry see in their output so an agent can traverse references\n- Backlinks (inbound rendering) remain unfiled — this is the forward direction only","after":["mcp"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-mcp","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a2","title":"Kolu integration: auto-configure its MCP in the ACP chat","done":"2026-08-10T21:32:52-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-10.\n- If Kolu is running on olai's host, the chat agent's session automatically gets kolu's MCP server — a stdio entry (`command: kolu, args: [\"mcp\"]`, forwarding `PADI_SOCKET`, per juspay/kolu's own `.mcp.json`) added to `session/new`'s `mcpServers` alongside olai's internal tool server (`mcpServersOf`, packages/chat/src/agent.ts — today it only speaks the http shape; this teaches it stdio)\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **Detection targets padi, not kolu web.** The web server may live on another machine reaching this host as a remote (the motivating topology: kolu web on pureintent, this host runs only the padi daemon — https://kolu.dev/padi/). \"Kolu is running\" = the padi daemon is reachable: `$PADI_SOCKET` when olai was launched inside a kolu terminal, else padi's state-root-keyed default socket; plus `kolu` on PATH to spawn the stdio server.\n- **Per session/new**: detect fresh each time a chat session is created, not once at boot — padi starting after olai still gets picked up.\n- **Automatic, no knob**: no flag/env; a knob can come later if ever wanted.\n- **Kolu-specific**: no generic extra-MCP-servers mechanism until a second server shows up.\n- **Upstream option** (#upstream): the detection logic can live in Kolu itself — e.g. a cheap \"is padi reachable\" probe verb, or `kolu mcp` failing fast when no daemon — so olai's side reduces to spawn-and-fall-back rather than reimplementing socket discovery.\n\n**Hazard found 2026-08-10 while orchestrating**, worth knowing before implementing:\n- `kolu` on PATH is not necessarily the host's kolu. Inside a kolu-spawned terminal the padi daemon prepends its own baked agent-tools bundle, and that bundle's `kolu` was an older build reporting the same version string while missing most verbs — filed as juspay/kolu#2146, fixed by kolu#2147 (the injected binary now resolves to the same build as the host's)\n- The lesson survives the fix: a `kolu` on PATH still SPAWNS when it is the wrong build, so detection should confirm the spawned server answers rather than assume the binary is current\n- The #upstream option above covers this too\n\nRefs: https://kolu.dev/mcp/, https://github.com/juspay/kolu/blob/master/.mcp.json","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"commit-button","parent":"feat-git","ord":"a2","title":"Commit changes on purpose, not on every write","done":"2026-08-11T06:57:37-04:00","desc":"**CORRECTION 2026-08-11:** this done stamp was FALSE from 23 seconds after the merge.\n- commit 12ea294, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (59 files, +390/−4415; exact content-inverse of squash c5bcbdf outside the roadmap, verified)\n- Second of three such incidents from the same morning session (dd34849/#87, a56cb65/#84)\n- Restoration tracked under mcp-commit-manual (option A: restore-83 PR first, then the MCP-face delta); this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #83\n\n**Filed 2026-08-10.** Today every op commits itself (packages/ops/src/git.ts), so one train of thought becomes a dozen commits — a roadmap gardening session produced eleven, each with a full node title as its subject line.\n- Replace that with a commit someone asks for\n- Design: docs/brainstorming/git-commits.md\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **The feature earns its place for ONE reason: an audit trail of what the tool wrote.** Chat auto-approves its ops and there is no web editor yet, so every write olai makes is one the human did not type. Not for history (descs carry their own dates), not for sync (olai never pushes), not for undo (the `undo` item plans real op inverses).\n- **Manual, not automatic.** Two doors to the same action: a Commit button in the UI, and a `commit` MCP tool the agent calls when it knows its work is done (and can say why). `--commit=off | manual | auto`, default `manual`; `--no-commit` keeps meaning `off`. Never `--amend`.\n- **Derive from git, store nothing**; **never show a text diff**; **writer intent via X-Olai-Writer trailer**; **messages prefixed `olai`**; **busy-repository check decides manual over automatic**; **one derivation serves the Changes view and per-node history**. (Full resolution detail in git history and docs/brainstorming/git-commits.md.)","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"paste-image","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a1","title":"Paste an image into the chat","done":"2026-08-11T06:38:54-04:00","desc":"**CORRECTION 2026-08-11:** this done stamp was FALSE from 19 seconds after the merge.\n- commit dd34849, mis-titled as this item's roadmap stamp, reverted the entire feature out of master (29 files, -1830 lines, tests included; CI stayed green because the tests left with it)\n- Discovered when the human pasted an image and nothing happened\n- Restoration tracked as paste-restore (split from silent-errors 2026-08-11, human's call: separate quick PR, no review); the wider audit tracked as silent-errors; this stamp stands for the original merge instant of #87\n\n**Filed 2026-08-10.** Paste (and drop, and pick on a phone) an image into the chat composer and have the agent see it.\n- Today the whole chat path is `string` end to end and the composer has no paste handler at all\n\n**What hardcodes text, layer by layer:**\n- `packages/surface/src/chat.ts` (`send: { input: { text: Schema.String } }`)\n- `packages/chat/src/chat.ts` (`begin(prompt: string)`, queue is `string[]`)\n- `packages/chat/src/agent.ts:115` (`prompt: (text: string)`) and `:549`, where the ACP call is a hardcoded single block `[{ type: \"text\", text }]`\n- `ChatEntry` (packages/surface/src/chat.ts) has only `text`, so a transcript row has nowhere to name an attachment\n- `Composer.tsx` mentions none of paste/clipboard/File/Blob\n\nResolved 2026-08-10:\n- **The agent receives a PATH, as text.** The prompt carries the tmp path; Claude Code reads the file itself. Kolu's model exactly, and it does not depend on `promptCapabilities.image` — which ACP 1.3 does define (`ContentBlock` has an `image` variant carrying base64 `data` + `mimeType`), and which olai could switch to later without redoing the transport. Inline base64 was rejected for now: the bytes would ride the prompt into the persisted Claude Code session.\n- **Bytes land in a TMP dir, not under the served root.** That keeps screenshots out of the store's probe and out of anything `commit-button` would commit. One dir per chat session, removed when the session ends or the server shuts down (the scope finalizer).\n- **Transport is CHUNKED over an existing surface procedure**, not a new HTTP route — a procedure inherits the origin gate and session `listener.ts` already enforces for the websocket, where an upload route would need its own.\n- **The transcript shows a filename chip.** The tab that pasted previews from the Blob it already has; other tabs and any reload show the chip. `/media/*` cannot help — it is guarded to the served root and the file is in tmp — and a second guarded route was judged not worth a thumbnail.\n\n**Copy kolu's derivation rather than re-deriving it** (packages/padi/src/upload.ts, and `scratch.write` in packages/padi/src/surface.ts, at the pin):\n- Two independent numbers: `MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` = 50 MiB is a POLICY cap on the file; `UPLOAD_CHUNK_BYTES` = 3 MiB is what one frame carries.\n  - Before chunking they were the same number and a 26 MB drop closed the socket with 1009, taking every other subscription on that tab's multiplexed wire down with it.\n- 3 MiB is chosen, not rounded: it must be a MULTIPLE OF 3 so base64's 3-bytes-to-4-chars grouping divides exactly and every chunk boundary lands on a 4-character group, which is what lets each chunk decode independently (4 MiB fails this — 1048576 is not divisible by 3).\n  - 3 MiB raw is exactly 4 MiB base64; plus a <64 KiB envelope that is 4.06 MiB against the 16 MiB budget, ~3.9x headroom.\n  - Base64's alphabet contains nothing JSON escapes, so there is no third expansion inside the JSON string — the one expansion it is tempting to forget, and it is genuinely absent.\n- First chunk creates the file (no `appendTo`); each later chunk passes back the path it was returned and appends.\n- Three details worth taking with it: ONE policy module consumed by both the client's pre-flight gate and the server's authoritative gate, so the two cannot drift on the rejection threshold; `appendTo` carries no authority, because the server re-derives the scratch dir and refuses any path outside it (\"a continuation token that happens to be readable\"); the dropped filename is sanitized to a safe basename and collision-suffixed.\n\nAlso: a phone has no Ctrl+V, so a file input belongs beside the paste handler (mobile-pwa shipped the viewport work this sits on). Drag-and-drop onto the panel is the same machinery once paste works.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-detect-primary","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a3","title":"Kolu detection must find the primary padi — delegate detection to kolu #upstream","done":"2026-08-11T09:58:21-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, from the same incident. Running several padi daemons on one machine is EXPECTED and normal — development and test state roots sit beside the main one. (Padi is kolu's background daemon.)\n- But olai's probe ran `kolu mcp` with no `PADI_SOCKET` environment variable set, and `kolu mcp` refuses to choose among several daemons — so detection answered \"no kolu\" on a perfectly healthy machine.\n- **The human's position:** choosing among daemons belongs in kolu; olai deliberately refuses to reimplement daemon discovery.\n- The upstream path was resolved on 2026-08-11 with the human's personal approval, and the item was marked done then.\n\n**The leftover — making the probe itself the evidence — shipped 2026-08-14 on both sides.** (It was item 9 of the design debate; Grok ruled it should be tracked against this already-paid item rather than get a second node.)\n- kolu pull request #2168, merged, ships `@kolu/detect`: a dependency-free leaf package whose `detect()` and `probe()` answer with a tagged `Detected` or `ProbeFailure`. It reads the live daemon's identity resource, races that against the process failing to launch, and editorializes nothing — `notOnPath` deliberately carries no reason, because the judgement belongs to whoever consumes it. It reads no environment variables and throws nothing. The one thing the review required to be fixed was a leak where the probe inherited `PADI_SOCKET` and so could disagree with the launch recipe it returned; that was fixed with a failing test written first.\n- olai pull request #150 (squash commit be9dc95c, merged on the human's approval) shrinks packages/chat/src/kolu.ts down onto the socket, holding one hard constraint end to end: kolu.test.ts stayed byte-for-byte unmodified through authoring, the review fixes and the re-pin. The sentences promised by the mcp-fail-visible item survive word for word, proven by those untouched tests and by the `a_failed_mcp_server` end-to-end scenario.\n- olai's dependency pin was moved to kolu master commit 39b97df6, which contains kolu #2166 and #2168; the next routine pin bump picks up #2167.","see":["mcp-fail-visible","kolu-mcp"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00"}
{"id":"surface-mcp","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a4","title":"One surface for browser and agents: project the surface into MCP #upstream","done":"2026-08-14T23:32:06-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11: adopt kolu's surface-over-MCP mechanism so the browser and external agents share ONE surface (the set of live cells and verbs the server offers).\n- Most of it landed via pull request #94, covering both MCP faces.\n\n**The audit merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #167; the human approved it, and it was squash-merged at commit 1fd9457 after resolving an overlap with pull request #168. That resolution exercised the audit's own property against #168's fields: SearchRequest gained `file`, `under` and operator prose; SearchAnswer gained refusals; filter.ts's `Refusal` was promoted to a schema; and the duplicate `SearchRefusal` was deleted. Dropping refusals now fails to compile in the operations layer, the web client and the server together, where before only one side would break.\n- docs/brainstorming/surface-mcp-positions.md holds the audit with receipts checked against master.\n- Two of the audit's positions were already true (cells are subscribable as resources; dependency checking happens by construction).\n- Two were closed (drift between verbs is now unrepresentable, via format/searching.ts; and the refusal contract is pinned across all four kinds of operation failure on both faces).\n- The bridge was priced — 418 MB of memory and 2,099 open file descriptors, with the measuring method written down — and gated on an upstream change.\n\n**The human's ruling** (2026-08-14, during the overnight batch of work): the query-shaped reads move to the format layer that night; the file-descriptor cost of the watcher is investigated that night and fixed only if the fix is contained; and the per-face exposure work and the MCP bridge move out to the Agents theme (a request was filed upstream in kolu and a kolu pull request drafted overnight, but the merge is the human's).\n- This parent item closes when its two remaining children do.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00"}
{"id":"surface-mcp-viewing","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a0","title":"Viewing over surface-mcp: agents subscribe the browser's cells","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, split out of the parent item as the slice that could be adopted before web editing existed.\n- **Scope:** adopt `@kolu/surface-mcp` (its `serveSurfaceAsMcp` function) for olai's READ side, so the surface's cells are exposed as subscribable MCP resources behind a deny-by-default `expose` list. An agent then watches the same live rows the browser draws, while the existing query tools continue to ride as purpose-built tools, unchanged.\n- Design: docs/brainstorming/surface-mcp-viewing.md.\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-11 as pull request #94** (squash-merged), the whole chain in one day:\n- First the upstream change kolu #2155 (adding tool-failure reporting, structured content, server instructions and titles), then bumping olai's kolu pin, then the final commit that had been gated on it.\n- ops/mcp.ts (227 lines) and mcp/stdio.ts (83 lines) were deleted, and both faces were switched onto `serveSurfaceAsMcp`.\n- Documents are exposed as a resource, in the shape decided by pull request #95.\n- **Four gaps in the MCP SDK were closed along the way:** input on standard input was not drained when it ended; a purpose-built half-duplex HTTP transport was needed because server-sent events hung the end-to-end tests; an empty schema struct was being wrapped in an `anyOf`, which broke every call that takes no arguments; and two requests with the same id could collide while waiting.\n- **Behaviour changes recorded and fenced by tests:** an unknown tool now returns an error result; the \"this is destructive\" hint is derived from whether the tool mutates; and there is drift in how additional properties are handled.\n- **One known leftover:** a handler that never replies leaves the drain counter stuck. This is a pre-existing class of problem and was stated on the pull request.\n- Write tools stay purpose-built until the Editor theme's design settles the parent item.","after":["edges-ui"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00"}
{"id":"batch-capture","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a5","title":"Batch capture: a subtree in one MCP call","done":"2026-08-11T19:17:10-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #106**, squash-merged as commit 839ab6a; the done mark carries the merge instant. Final commit before merge: 258382f.\n- `add_node` grew the ability to add nested children AND — after the human ratified the opposite of what the author had proposed — `create_outline` can seed a whole tree in the same shape. A refused seed leaves NO file behind.\n- One call, one plan, one validation, one atomic write, one commit. Marks travel through the single `stampOf` helper, so the day-placement rules from pull request #103 still hold.\n- The reply names every node it minted.\n- MCP host programs reject JSON Schema `$ref` references, so the recursive schema is unrolled to three levels and the planner refuses anything deeper BY NAME — guarding against silently losing leaf nodes that the schema dropped.\n- The schema cost was measured: 1,523 bytes larger after de-duplicating the prose. A further 600-byte saving by stripping descriptions was DECLINED, and the human endorsed that: an agent-facing schema should teach its own field shapes.\n- Grok's review: approve, nothing required fixing, probes run independently.\n- 577 unit tests and 183 end-to-end tests; 24 of 24 checks green on both platforms.\n\n**Handed to an agent on 2026-08-11**: Kolu terminal d94c7864 (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved), working in the git worktree `.worktrees/batch-capture`; brief at `.worktrees/batch-capture-BRIEF.md`.\n- At the review step, pull request #106 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/106) was open with the implementation plus the project's two standing cleanup refactors and the /simplify pass pushed. 553 unit tests and 172 end-to-end tests passed locally, and the evidence (one call creating 15 nodes across 4 levels in 19ms, one commit, a schema with no `$ref`) was in `.worktrees/batch-capture-out/`.\n- **Notable:** because MCP hosts reject `$ref`, the recursive schema is unrolled to three levels and the planner refuses deeper nesting by name — a child dropped by the schema must never silently lose its leaves. De-duplicating the prose in the nested schema shrank the tool table from 13,239 to 11,085 bytes. One cleanup finding the author declined was flagged for the reviewer.\n- **Review done** by Grok in a side-by-side terminal (02207bef) on 2026-08-11: APPROVE, nothing required fixing. Atomicity, the schema unroll, the shared stamping helper, the reply naming what it minted, placement, and neighbour round-tripping were all verified under the reviewer's own independent MCP probes; the declined cleanup finding was endorsed. Four optional suggestions.\n- Three of the four optional suggestions were taken (the neighbour test was hardened to assert that untouched lines are byte-identical), and continuous integration was fully green at commit 60b9921 (20 checks on both platforms, 574 unit tests).\n- Then the human ruled the other way on the fourth (2026-08-11): `create_outline` seeds a whole TREE in this pull request, using the same recursive shape, and a refused seed refuses the whole creation (no empty outline is left). The doubling of wire cost was to be mitigated or at least measured.\n- Opus built it, continuous integration was re-run, and it was put in front of the human at green — no pre-approval; he decided the merge. Reviewer: Grok (a different model from the author).\n- **The shape was decided by the human on 2026-08-11:** `add_node` grows an optional recursive `children` field (title plus optional note, date, id and mark per child) — no dedicated `add_subtree` tool, and no generic batch envelope. One plan, one validation, one atomic write, one commit.\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 after the human watched a chat agent capture a house outline: every child was its own `add_node` call, so a subtree was N sequential tool calls (a screenshot showed about 13), each riding the FULL write gate — revalidating the whole set, writing a temp file, renaming — and each costing the agent a round trip.\n- This is structural, not incidental: the MCP surface (packages/ops/src/tools.ts) has no batch operation at all.\n- **The fix is also an atomicity fix:** the write gate is already all-files-or-nothing, so ONE operation planning N records validates once and lands atomically. Today a failure part-way through a sequence leaves a half-captured subtree behind.\n- **The shape to decide at dispatch time was:** (a) `add_node` grows an optional recursive children field (each child: title plus optional note, date, id, mark; ids minted as today), or (b) a dedicated `add_subtree` tool taking a nested tree. Either keeps one plan, one validation, one write, one commit.\n- Explicitly out of scope unless separately wanted: a generic batch envelope mixing arbitrary kinds of operation in one call — transactionality across different operation kinds is a bigger design than capture needs.\n- Also worth looking at when dispatching: whether `set_desc` and `set_date` on a just-added node can ride the same plan, since capture-with-a-note is the common agent gesture.","see":["mcp"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:31:32-04:00"}
{"id":"smv-kolu-pr","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a0","title":"kolu: ToolFailure + instructions + title land in surface-mcp #upstream","done":"2026-08-11T13:13:36-04:00","desc":"This was the upstream change that everything else waited on: attaching structured content to error results (a `ToolFailure` type), passing server-level instructions through, and adding a `title` to purpose-built tools.\n- Scope: structured content on error results (ToolFailure); passing server-level instructions through; a title on BespokeTool; and adopting ToolFailure in kolu's own MCP `sendInput`.\n- **The design was approved** by srid on 2026-08-11.\n- The work ran through kolu's own pre-merge review-and-check routine on branch `surface-mcp-structured`, in Kolu terminal `7be1fc95`.\n- **Done when:** srid merges the kolu pull request.\n- Pull request: https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2155\n\n**Status on 2026-08-11:**\n- Kolu terminal `7be1fc95` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved), in the kolu repository, on branch `surface-mcp-structured`.\n- At the review step: the pre-merge reviews and the evidence were posted and the continuous-integration checks were settling; srid merges the kolu pull request personally.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"smv-pin-bump","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a1","title":"Bump olai's kolu pin to a revision carrying it","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"olai consumes @kolu/* hydrated from the Nix-pinned kolu revision — an open PR is not consumable.\n- **Fix:** update-pins to a kolu master SHA containing the surface-mcp changes\n- check-kolu-deps.sh and the root dependency list move with the pin\n- Whatever other upstream movement rides the bump comes along (normal)\n- **Optional shortcut (pre-ratified):** once the kolu PR's API is final post-gauntlet, the PR #94 agent may prove the final commit against a temporary pin of that revision\n  - Lands only after the real bump\n","after":["smv-kolu-pr"],"changed":"2026-08-19T19:14:56-04:00"}
{"id":"smv-gated-commit","parent":"surface-mcp-viewing","ord":"a2","title":"The final commit that was waiting on kolu: delete the hand-written dispatcher and switch both faces over","done":"2026-08-11T14:38:08-04:00","desc":"Both of olai's MCP faces — the `olai mcp` command that talks over standard input/output, and the server's `/mcp` HTTP route — are switched onto kolu's `serveSurfaceAsMcp`, with `bespokeFrom(TOOLS, ops)` supplying each tool's name, title and description. The hand-written dispatcher goes: ops/src/mcp.ts (227 lines) and mcp/stdio.ts (83 lines) are deleted.\n\nThen pull request #94 comes out of draft, its continuous-integration checks are taken to green using odu (the project's CI tool), it goes through a review round, and it is put in front of srid for his approval.","after":["smv-pin-bump"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp-mirror-op","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a6","title":"The ops layer cannot place a mirror","done":"2026-08-11T22:13:42-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #117** (squash commit 7866e08), merged automatically by the orchestrator at 2026-08-12T02:13:25Z under the human's overnight pre-approval — the second fully autonomous merge.\n- **What satisfied the merge gate:** Grok's review (six findings, all taken — teaching refusals when retiring a referenced placement, making the current state enumerable through the `placed` and `mirrors` reads, query tests, and syncing with master); all 20 odu continuous-integration checks green on both platforms at commit 4d0dd84, with no dirty state and no diff at all in the roadmap file; and the orchestrator spot-checking the evidence transcript (a containment-cycle refusal that names the loop, a did-you-mean suggestion, and a placement retirement that leaves the target file byte-identical).\n- `add_mirror`, `remove_mirror` and `set_after` ship on both faces. The write operations can now express everything in the ledger — which means the orchestrator's era of editing files with scripts ends as soon as its MCP server reconnects to this build.\n\n**Handed to an agent on 2026-08-11** in the overnight batch: Kolu terminal `1bb73fb1` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved); reviewer Grok in a side-by-side terminal `11d79c7f`.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-node-context","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a2","title":"Ask the agent about this node: selection becomes chat context","done":"2026-08-13T14:19:23-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11. **Shipped as pull request #141** (squash commit 80e4d65) on 2026-08-13.\n\n**Direction one — arming the chat from a row:**\n- A row's ••• menu gains \"Ask agent\", which loads the shown node into the chat composer as context and opens the panel. On a mirror, it arms whatever the mirror is a placement of.\n- On the wire the context is ids only, resolved on the server through the same `Query.foundOf` that answers `read_node`, so the context line and a tool call cannot disagree.\n- The agent gets one line under the message — a handle it can act on through MCP, never a copy of the content.\n\n**Direction two — the transcript points back at rows:**\n- Chips appear on the sent message.\n- The node an olai write was about is now identified (`Applied.id` finally crosses the wire).\n- Node ids the agent writes in backticks become pressable, exactly when the loaded set declares them — and they are marked with the RESOLVED id, so an id that names a placement lands on the node it shows.\n- Pressing one focuses the node in place when it is already on the page (the address bar stays put, pinned by an end-to-end test) and navigates when it is not.\n- If an armed node has gone away, the send is refused using the write layer's own not-found wording, and the composer is restored.\n\n**Pipeline:**\n- Grok's review found two things that had to be fixed — focusing in place had no test, so an always-navigate implementation kept the suite green; and ids naming placements were marked with the wrong id. Both were fixed by writing the failing test first.\n- All six optional suggestions were taken, including a scenario for a node that has gone away, and the palette's `>` prefix sending the armed context.\n- The reviewer endorsed keeping see and after references as ordinary links: \"an href is a promise about leaving\".\n- Then pull request #140 landed in packages/chat first: master was merged, conflicts were resolved preserving both features (384 of 384 end-to-end tests passed together), and the resulting changes were verified at two layers and confirmed correct.\n- The continuous-integration run was 10 of 10 at commit b4c72a2 on the kolu-ci-1 machine.\n- The prototype artifact could not be fetched, so the interaction was built from olai's own idioms instead.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"form-elicitation","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a3","title":"Form elicitation: the agent's questions render as forms","done":"2026-08-11T20:26:45-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as pull request #110** (squash commit 4a7ad66), merged 2026-08-12T00:26:28Z after the human lifted his hold; this done mark is the write-layer timestamp minutes afterwards.\n- The agent's questions now render as answerable forms in the chat (Claude's AskUserQuestion works over the Agent Client Protocol).\n- If an answer is refused, the typed draft is kept.\n- Plan mode reaches the human instead of being silently auto-approved.\n- Review verdict: approve with fixes; all findings addressed. All 18 required checks green on both platforms at commit 9054de8.\n\n- All five scope items were delivered, verified against the adapter version actually pinned (0.66.0).\n- **Scope item 5 was tightened:** auto-approval now requires POSITIVE RECOGNITION of an MCP server that was handed to the session; anything else falls back to asking the human.\n- **Carried forward:** the collision on the cancel path noted in the silent-errors item stays on that branch's side.\n\n**Filed and handed to an agent on 2026-08-11** on the human's decision (\"We want that\"): Kolu terminal `5931d974` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved); reviewer Grok in a side-by-side terminal `47b4e8c9`.\n- **Origin:** the adapter presents AskUserQuestion as a protocol-level form request when the client advertises `elicitation.form`, and disables the tool otherwise — and olai advertised nothing.\n- agent.ts had been auto-answering every permission request with the first \"allow\" option, which would have silently approved leaving plan mode and switched the session into auto mode.\n- Out of scope: URL-based elicitation, and a permission-settings user interface.\n- Pairs with the orchestrator-in-chat item.","see":["chat","orchestrator-in-chat"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp-commit-manual","parent":"feat-git","ord":"a3","title":"The MCP face never got manual commits","done":"2026-08-12T09:34:34-04:00","desc":"**Shipped in pull request #116** (squash commit 324f8b5, merged 2026-08-12T13:33:55Z, approved by the human).\n- It was discovered mid-work that pull request #83 had been reverted entirely (see the root-cause analysis for that incident). After #114 restored it, this pull request delivered the original scope.\n- `olai mcp` now takes the same three-way `--commit=off|manual|auto` option that `olai web` takes, with manual as the default; `--no-commit` still means off.\n- A `commit` verb joins the MCP tool table (last in TOOLS, after the mirror operations from #117), calling the one shared `Ops.commit`. A message is required.\n  - The reply reports a summary of the pending writes it swept up.\n  - It refuses with a reason when the repository is busy.\n- The pending writes, the last commit, and the recorded writer intent are readable identically over MCP, per the consistency rule in HACKING.md.\n- Under manual mode, the \"why\" field of an applied write reads as \"expected to be pending\", never as a git error.\n- **A test asserts the two faces are byte-identical:** both produce identical commits for the same set of pending writes.\n- **Continuous integration caught a real startup race:** the first read of pending writes accepted the unpublished seed value and asserted Off against Ready — a timing problem that only appeared on macOS. It was fixed by waiting for a published value, and the README now teaches that pattern.\n- **Final step:** merged past pull requests #109, #112, #115, #117, #118 and #119.\n  - The roadmap file was verified byte-identical to master by comparing blob hashes.\n  - Noted: origin/master advancing by a push in the middle of a merge (shared refs across worktrees) can shortchange that merge — the byte-hash check catches it.\n- **Next:** reconnect the olai MCP server to activate manual mode — one commit per orchestration beat via the commit verb — and #117's mirror operations.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-drop-files","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a4","title":"Drag and drop files into the chat","done":"2026-08-12T17:42:12-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as PR #126**, squash 4851ad5 (2026-08-12). Drop lands on the existing attachment machinery — nothing new below the gesture.\n- The panel body (header excluded) is the drop target with a visible affordance\n- enter/leave COUNTED (not flagged — crossing transcript rows is one drag; sabotaging the counter to a boolean fails the e2e)\n- dragend clears a cancelled drag's overlay (both endings asserted)\n- Text drags left alone\n- Multi-file drops attach in order all the way to the order the agent reads them\n- **Refusals survive each other:** the batch sorts through the ONE shared gate, Chat.attach answers (stored/refused/gone) instead of drawing, and holding.take says every refusal once on the panel's one line\n  - Closes a real silent-loss path (file 3 used to rub out file 2's reason; the reviewer proved nothing went red without its test, so the fix is pinned by a take unit test with type+size reasons)\n- **MID-FLIGHT HUMAN RULING** (the human hit it driving the build): the gate widened beyond pictures\n  - attachmentRejection now takes DOCUMENT_EXTENSIONS (.pdf .txt .md .csv .json) with honest name+size chips\n  - The picker's accept= matches the gate\n  - .svg keeps its scriptable-document refusal\n  - The 50 MB cap stays\n  - Loosening the gate to accept .svg turns four tests red\n- **Two reviewer rounds:** initial — one MUST-FIX + the dragend bug upgraded by the orchestrator; re-verify 1 and 2 both \"the PR stands clean\", every fix sabotage-verified\n- CI green both platforms at each head\n- Human approved 2026-08-12\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"adapter-interpret","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a5","title":"Adapter interpretation out of agent.ts: one testable module #techdebt","done":"2026-08-12T15:25:48-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as PR #125**, squash e7fc004 (2026-08-12) — same day the kitchen-sinks debate ratified it (debates/kitchen-sinks/conclusion.md item 4; filed and dispatched on the human's word).\n- packages/chat/src/interpret.ts now owns the adapter's interpretation as pure functions:\n  - `allowedWithoutAsking` (the debate's shouldBypass, renamed because it answers with WHICH option, never a boolean — positive recognition of `mcp__<server>__` against the given names, never the first allow-flavoured option, so plan mode's \"Ready to code?\" cannot silently flip the session to auto)\n  - `toolNameIn` (`_meta.claudeCode.toolName`)\n  - `liveModelIn`/`modelPickerIn`/`labelsOf` (the forwarded CLI init on `_claude/sdkMessage` + the free-form \"model\" config select)\n  - Plus `NEW_SESSION_META`/`SDK_MESSAGE`/`BYPASS_MODE`\n  - Two strays (`id===\"model\"`, `bypassPermissions`) the author proved are adapter vocabulary, not protocol (SessionConfigId and SessionModeId are free-form strings; bypassPermissions appears nowhere in the SDK)\n- 22 unit tests, no subprocess — the safety rule is unit-testable for the first time\n- `onPermission` is four lines\n- agent.ts 1119→1033 with all closure state (session lifecycle, toolNames map, model debounce) unmoved\n- interpret.ts deliberately NOT on the package's export socket\n- **The reviewer passed it with zero MUST-FIX** after two sabotage probes (dropping the ours gate and weakening the prefix match both went red) and arm-by-arm equivalence against the old inline logic\n- Zero behavior change; e2e green throughout\n- Human pre-approved on the green gate\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-edit-diffs","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a6","title":"Chat shows the agent's file-edit diffs, trimmed, expand on click","done":"2026-08-12T22:05:05-04:00","desc":"Shipped as pull request #130 (commit c7f8fdb), squash-merged 2026-08-13. Two kinds of preview, two vocabularies, one folding idiom — as ratified.\n\n- **Direct file edits:** the Agent Client Protocol's diff block travels structured (a FileDiff carrying the path plus the old and new text), and the panel renders it Claude-Code-style: trimmed to 6 rows, with unchanged runs collapsed into gaps first, expanded in place on click. The line diff is hand-written — strip the common prefix and suffix, then a suffix-table longest-common-subsequence walk of about 100 lines. (The available diff micro-dependencies bring character-level algorithms and patch application along for one function — the same trade already made in choosing @olai/git over simple-git.) It is bounded: over budget it says \"rewritten whole\" rather than showing a wrong diff.\n- **Writes made through olai's own MCP tools:** never a text diff. Instead the node-level story (marked done, note rewritten, moved) comes from the `Applied.sort` field on the write layer's reply, derived by the same `changesOf` function that classifies rows in the commit panel. The wording and glyph tables moved to changes.ts, so ONE table serves both panels. The never-a-text-diff rule is a fact about the FILE, not just about the write path: a direct Edit on a .jsonl file routes to the story row too (via `fileKind` → OutlineDiff).\n- **Review:** one thing had to be fixed — there was no property test that a diff can reconstruct the new text, so sabotaging a diagonal index left all ten tests green. (The reviewer property-checked the implementation itself over 22 cases first.) Plus six optional suggestions, ALL taken, two of them upgraded: the .jsonl file rule, and the now-false \"no filesystem\" paragraph in docs/chat.md. The reviewer re-checked the changes made in response and confirmed them. The /simplify cleanup pass caught a literal NUL byte that had made folds.ts count as binary to git.\n- **Conflict round:** pull request #129 moved master; resolved keeping both documentation truths (+2/−2 lines), with the full suite run on the merged commit and continuous integration 10 of 10 at commit 6d8fafd.\n- **Evidence, checked by the orchestrator:** the trimmed card, expansion in place, a `set_done` story row with the outline agreeing beside it, and the .jsonl fallback — archived to the session scratchpad.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:32:20-04:00"}
{"id":"ask-vocabulary","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a7","title":"@olai/acp: the ask vocabulary moves to the wall that speaks ACP","done":"2026-08-13T15:25:45-04:00","desc":"Ratified in the 2026-08-13 design debate (item 2); the debate's Position A was chosen by the human, meaning the boundary is drawn where the Fable seat argued it should be. **Shipped as pull request #144** (squash commit fbbc15a) on 2026-08-13.\n- A new workspace package, `@olai/acp`, owns the Agent Client Protocol's vocabulary:\n  - AskField, AskChoice, AskAnswer, AskOutcome and YES_NO live on its `./wire` entry point, re-exported by the surface package so no consumer had to change an import.\n  - FileDiff was judged to belong to the same tenant, with the argument written at its declaration.\n  - Projections for asks and diffs, plus the Form and Refused types, live on the main entry point.\n- **UsageFailure is translated at the boundary rather than moved:** `Refused` is a plain value that never appears on a wire or an Effect error channel, and it is turned into a UsageFailure in exactly one place (`questions.ts`) and read in exactly one (`agent.ts`).\n- **The boundary is machine-checked:** a test asserts six claims over a single scan of the tree (using position-based specifiers and `Bun.Transpiler.scanImports`).\n  - No `@olai/*` import may appear anywhere inside the acp package.\n  - SDK runtime imports may only appear in the subprocess files; the matcher covers SDK subpaths too.\n- **Pipeline:**\n  - Grok's review found nothing that had to be fixed; the boundary judgement was verified file by file against Position A, and the losing position was used to sharpen exactly where the cut falls.\n  - All four optional suggestions were taken, each with the failing test written first (the subpath matcher came from the reviewer's own sabotage attempt). The reviewer re-checked the changes and confirmed them, twice.\n  - Parity: 1,224 unit tests, 384 end-to-end tests run twice (locally and in continuous integration), plus a smoke test of the packaged binary; continuous integration green at commit 8afbc08 on the kolu-ci-1 machine.\n  - One flaky test in the test lane (parse noise from a pre-existing kolu fixture's SDK) was judged unrelated and was green on re-run.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-subjects","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a8","title":"One way to say \"this is about that\": chats, nodes, notes #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"- One association mechanism across three pairs: chat↔node (resume a node's chat from its ••• menu), node↔note (likely an edge like see/after), chat↔note (reduces to the other two).\n- Brainstorm: docs/brainstorming/associations.md. Awaits ratification.","changed":"2026-08-21T17:05:58-04:00"}
{"id":"reads-on-the-floor","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a1","title":"Query-shaped reads move to the format floor","done":"2026-08-14T22:41:42-04:00","desc":"A deferral from pull request #167 (2026-08-14), recorded with THREE traps to avoid:\n1. The shared vocabulary for read requests must go into `@olai/format`, NOT `@olai/ops`.\n2. `Applied` cannot be included — the two sides are deliberately different types.\n3. An `Outline` schema in `format/searching.ts` would collide with the `Outline` the package already exports from `set.ts:62` via `index.ts`.\n\n- **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #170, squash-merged at commit 95d69fc, from the overnight batch of work under the human's standing overnight authorization. By the end it was a pure refactor.\n  - All three traps were respected.\n  - **The anti-drift property** was proven by experiment and extended twice during the review round: the `as SearchHit` cast became a type annotation, so a field that only exists on SearchHit now fails to compile in the operations layer, the web client and the server, where it used to compile cleanly. And `NodeContext` borrows Found's field schemas, so dropping `Found.path` now also fails `@olai/chat`.\n- **Review:** Grok and opencode; nothing blocking; every small suggestion fixed or answered on the record; all commits verified to stand alone.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 95d69fc.\n- **One behaviour change the branch had been carrying** — trimming the answer `list_outlines` gives for a torn file — was REVERTED before merge, because nothing unratified lands overnight; it became its own item needing the human's ruling.\n- **The three declines both reviewers concurred with** became their own technical-debt items: the four different spellings of `{file, line}`; `read()` inferring its own request half; and `outlines()` walking every file times every node.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"per-face-expose","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a7","title":"Upstream: serveSurfaceApp cannot scope a verb per face #upstream #human","done":"2026-08-15T09:03:32-04:00","desc":"A deferral from pull request #167 (2026-08-14): the 18 write tools have no surface procedure, and giving them one would put the write layer's request vocabulary onto the shared surface.\n- `serveSurfaceApp` took its handlers as one whole with no list of what to expose, so every open browser tab could call every verb.\n- **Done 2026-08-15:** the human approved it and kolu pull request [#2170](https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2170) is squash-merged at commit bfb4eb56.\n  - `@kolu/surface/expose` gives every serving face the same grammar for saying what is exposed (an ExposeMap, classified by `classifyExpose`), a checked `FaceExposure` at the handler boundary, and `SurfaceMemberNotExposed` as the per-request refusal.\n  - That night's review round contributed one real fix: keys inherited from `Object.prototype` can no longer be listed as members.\n  - The paired change in the drishti project (srid/drishti#142) was re-pinned to commit bfb4eb56 — its dependency tarball is byte-identical to the reviewed tree — and merged on the human's word; that working branch was torn down cleanly.\n- Issue [#2169](https://github.com/juspay/kolu/issues/2169) STAYS OPEN, for how to spell a writable primitive on a gated face.\n  - The pull request's `Closes #2169` line auto-closed it at merge, and the author reopened it with a written split of what landed versus what is still open.\n- **A lesson worth a line in HACKING.md someday:** an issue covering several slices wants \"Part of #n\" in pull request bodies, never \"Closes #n\".\n- Two questions about the framework's shape stay recorded on kolu #2170 for srid (one currency of exposure across faces; and whether exposure should be required alongside an explicit expose-everything). They are cheapest to answer while a drishti review is open.\n- **What this unblocked:** the MCP bridge, handed to an agent the same day.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp-bridge","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a8","title":"The bridge shape: an external agent attaches to the RUNNING olai","done":"2026-08-15T11:16:32-04:00","desc":"A deferral from pull request #167 (2026-08-14). It was unblocked on 2026-08-15 by kolu #2170 (commit bfb4eb56) and ratified as ONE pull request in three stages.\n- **Status on 2026-08-15:**\n  - Kolu terminal `cd13fd33` (Claude Opus, permissions auto-approved), in the olai repository on branch `mcp-bridge`.\n  - Pull request #175, starting with a dependency pin bump.\n    - ALL write verbs — 19 of them, not the 18 this item names, because markdown editing came later; that is noted in the code — become surface procedures.\n    - The MCP face is exposed; the browser face is denied.\n    - The bridge itself: `olai mcp` attaches to an already-running server over a unix socket, and only boots its own store when no server is running.\n    - Design document: `docs/brainstorming/mcp-bridge.md`.\n  - **A correction to the earlier audit,** carried honestly: the \"418 MB for two stores\" price was misread. An `olai mcp` over an EMPTY directory already uses 213.8 MiB — that is the Bun runtime, not the corpus — so memory does not halve. The file-descriptor story stays with the bun-watcher-bump item; the bridge's remaining 1,048 descriptors belong to the file watcher.\n  - **Decisions for srid when it was put in front of him:**\n    - The writer identity became a per-FACE binding; the `ops.commit` twin was deleted, since it existed only to carry that field and had drifted on its error channel.\n    - The three read verbs became surface procedures too — stage 3 could not leave them behind.\n  - **Deferred on the record:**\n    - `socket.ts:86` calls realpath while `directory.ts:16` merely trims the string — two different ways of deciding \"which directory\", so a symlink gives the same socket but different chat memory. Pre-existing, and not made worse here.\n    - The chat agent face's writer has no end-to-end test for its commit trailer; it is only fenced, the same as before.\n    - Socket latency is unmeasured, and the design document says so.\n  - **Review stage:** Grok (terminal `dc5279c5`) and opencode (terminal `0be27fed`), both briefed to run the drift experiment themselves, re-verify the 213.8 MiB baseline, and attack the two-ways-to-canonicalise-a-directory seam.\n  - **Continuous integration:** 8 of 9 at commit 47c5d52c, with the end-to-end run finishing while the author watched (it had been 10 of 10 at both preceding commits).\n  - **Then:** put in front of the human — the orchestrator merges only on his word.","after":["per-face-expose"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"watcher-fd-cost","parent":"surface-mcp","ord":"a4","title":"The recursive watcher holds one fd per served file, per store, for life #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T23:32:02-04:00","desc":"A finding from measuring two stores in pull request #167 (2026-08-14): the store's recursive file watcher holds one open file descriptor (an operating-system handle for an open file) per served file, per store, for the entire life of the process.\n- 14 descriptors with watching off, against 1,050 with watching on, on a 1,020-file vault.\n- **Closed 2026-08-14** during the overnight batch of work, as an investigation rather than a fix, per the human's ruling that the fix had to be small or else stop. No code changed; one document: pull request #173, squash-merged at commit df2a646, adding `docs/brainstorming/watcher-fd-cost.md`.\n- **The cost is Bun's:** in Bun 1.3.13, `fs.watch` routes through Bun's internal bundler watcher, which calls `open(2)` once per path under the ROOT — including .git and node_modules, which is the real multiplier.\n  - Bun 1.3.14 rewrites this onto inotify, FSEvents and kqueue, dropping it to roughly 15 descriptors and also closing the blind spot where subdirectories created after boot were not watched.\n- Watching per directory — the brief's own proposal — measured at identical cost on 1.3.13, so no small fix existed.\n- **Both reviewers reproduced every conclusion independently.** The fact-checking round produced only small suggestions, all re-measured and folded in, including one honest 13-descriptor divergence between runs that is explained in the document.\n  - Grok's finding that nixpkgs pull request #519796 is held up by a segmentation fault in `bun build` that olai would not hit is left in the review record.\n- **Successor items:** bun-watcher-bump (which needs the human's ruling) and watcher-postboot-blind (the bug the version bump also fixes).","changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"torn-file-answer-trim","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"a9","title":"list_outlines answers an unreadable file with meaningless zeros #human #next #grok","done":"2026-08-22T16:43:30-04:00","desc":"- An unreadable outline answers `{file, nodes: 0, roots: [], unreadable}` — the zeros are noise beside the flag.\n- A fix to `{file, unreadable}` was written and verified safe (both consumers read `.file` only), then reverted pending the human's word.\n- Either shape is one small commit; the case is preserved in reading.ts, format/README.md:270 and commit 525f737a.\n\n**pr** — merged 2026-08-22 as 3b9fe9ab (ruled {file, unreadable}; list_documents' torn row matches)","changed":"2026-08-22T16:43:30-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/350"}}
{"id":"chat-token-usage","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"a9","title":"Chat shows token usage, so /compact stops being a guess","done":"2026-08-15T12:31:51-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human: the chat gave no view of context consumption, so /compact was a guess.\n\n- **MERGED** 2026-08-15 (pre-approved by the human): PR #178, squashed at fcbb155f.\n- Header draws the newest usage_update frame as a FRACTION (22k/1M) beside the model.\n  - A percentage was rejected on captured evidence: the adapter corrects the window mid-conversation (1000000 → 200000 on the wire), and a percentage read 2% on both sides of a fivefold drop in real headroom.\n- Read in @olai/acp (any agent may send usage); newest frame wins — pinned MID-TURN with a hold verb after Grok caught the fake agent reproducing only half the correction, sabotage-verified both directions.\n- cost arrives on the wire and is deliberately undrawn, with a unit test asserting the drop.\n- **Reviews:** Grok (2 findings + the deeper recursion of its own fix) and opencode (1 blocking) — all addressed with per-finding replies.\n  - The round also produced the pending-review-draft lesson (a review created without a submit event sits invisible — the orchestrator submitted it by API) and the detached-HEAD reviewer hazard.\n- **Deferred** in the PR body:\n  - No compaction-threshold cue yet (wants the human's eye on a real session).\n  - A loaded conversation shows nothing until its first turn (session/load replays without a usage frame — documented where a person meets it).","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"olai-subscribes","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"aC","title":"PR: olai watches kolu's terminals itself and updates lane marks","todo":true,"desc":"- The olai server subscribes to kolu's terminal/urgency feeds and updates lane step nodes live — no agent babysitting terminals.\n- Retires the detached watcher shells and hand-flipped marks.\n- Needs mcp-bridge's plumbing first.","after":["lane-cloning","mcp-bridge"],"changed":"2026-08-26T10:43:02-04:00","custom":{"blocker-gone":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: \"needs mcp-bridge's plumbing first\" — mcp-bridge is done (2026-08-15), the blocker is gone; what this PR retires today is the kolu watch background Monitor + the supervision-watcher ledger (instructions.md's ONLY-wake-mechanism), per orchestrator.md rev 2's olai-subscribes-to-padi design"}}
{"id":"chat-at-completion","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aA","title":"Chat: typing @ completes file paths into the message","done":"2026-08-17T00:31:16-04:00","desc":"Requested 2026-08-16.\n- In the chat input, typing `@` should open a completion of the served directory's files (the same set the sidebar knows: outlines and documents), filtered as you type, inserting the path into the message — the way Claude Code's own `@` works in the terminal.\n- **Precedent:** the chat client already has a completion pattern to lean on — the editor's tag autocomplete (input-widgets).\n- **No new server surface needed:** the file list is already on the wire for free — the `outlines` collection's keys plus the `documents` collection's key set.\n- **Decide alongside:** whether a completed `@path` is plain text the agent reads, or carries the same attach semantics the `+` button has.","created":"2026-08-16T11:19:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/213"}}
{"id":"chat-rewind","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aB","title":"Chat: no /rewind yet — waiting on the adapter","todo":true,"desc":"- The chat has no rewind verb; the pinned adapter hasn't shipped one either (its id-mapping plumbing exists but is unused). The CLI's /rewind is terminal-only.\n- Two paths: wait for the adapter's verb (check on each pin bump), or offer \"branch this conversation\" today via `unstable_forkSession`.\n- The human hasn't asked for the branch button; it becomes a lane on their word.","created":"2026-08-16T14:03:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T16:08:20-04:00","custom":{"pin-gap":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: the gap is real (no rewind/forkSession under packages/) but the check-on-bump premise is stale — the pin sits at 0.66.0 while npm latest is 0.70.0, and the pin now carries a local patch (acp/patches/, #365): a bump check is OWED and must re-apply the patch"}}
{"id":"self-edit","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a0","title":"Keyboard editing","done":"2026-08-12T09:15:56-04:00","desc":"Shipped in PR #109 (squash a1d18f7, merged 2026-08-12T13:13:53Z, human-approved).\n- **The Workflowy editing loop through the ops layer:** Enter (new sibling as draft — never writes a blank record), Tab/Shift+Tab indent/outdent, Alt+Shift+↑↓ move, Ctrl+Enter toggle done, ↑↓ row walking with visible caret, Escape\n- titles edit inline via input overlay, descs edit inline on click (post-review fix)\n- drafts commit on blur/Enter/idle and survive a REFUSED write\n- every structural key is one op through the write gate — no optimistic echo\n- **edit.apply intent seam:** client sends indent/outdent/move intents, server resolves placement against the judged snapshot (mirror-anchor bugs fixed under adversarial review)\n- One keyboard registry joined with #104's map; ⌘K/⌘\\/⌘J pinned by test\n- 18 new e2e + unit coverage; 858 unit / 252 e2e green on the merged tree\n- **Human's post-test fixes folded in:** sibling indent, Shift+Enter inline (no box), desc click-to-edit, visible caret\n- DELETE ABSENT by ruling; split/merge/undo/multi-select/drag-drop deferred\n- **Final leg note:** git rerere silently auto-resolved the roadmap during the master merge — caught and restored byte-for-byte; wariness recorded for future merges","after":["chat"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"split-merge","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a1","title":"Split & merge","done":"2026-08-14T00:46:59-04:00","desc":"Pressing Enter in the middle of a node's text splits it in two; pressing Backspace at the start of a line merges it into the previous sibling.\n- These are compound operations (`split_node` and `merge_node`) in the write layer, with both the keyboard and the MCP face calling the same operation. They were deferred out of keyboard editing on 2026-08-09.\n\n**Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #157, squash-merged, from the overnight batch of work; it was classed SIMPLE, so the orchestrator merged it without waking the human under the standing overnight authorization.\n- **Grok's review** found two things that blocked merge, plus four small suggestions; all were addressed at commit 43c1bae, each proven by a test that failed before the fix and passed after.\n  - Splitting at a mirror wrote into the target's home file and lost the caret; it now follows the rule that pressing Enter on a mirror makes a sibling of the PLACEMENT.\n  - Backspace on a row that had been typed but not yet committed was swallowed; merging now commits the draft first.\n- **The undo design was settled by the reviewer:** the inverse of a split stays \"merge\", because removing the node and guarding on its previous title would break where redo places things. The leftover gap — that the inverse cannot record the previous value — is documented in `inverseOf`'s split branch. THE HUMAN MAY OVERRULE THIS.\n- Nine scope decisions are documented on the pull request (merging targets the previous sibling rather than Workflowy's previous visible row; pressing Enter at the start of a line still adds a node; and so on).\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed; 1,362 unit tests and 437 end-to-end scenarios; an evidence page is on the pull request.","after":["self-edit"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"undo","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a2","title":"Undo","done":"2026-08-12T13:23:05-04:00","desc":"Shipped in pull request #120 (squash commit b77f929, merged 2026-08-12T17:22:33Z; the human approved it, and that approval also ratified the \"un-create\" framing).\n- **Undo and redo work as a stack of inverse intents:** every edit made in the browser — structural edits, and, after the human found the gap while using it, committed title and note text too — records a server-derived inverse at the moment it is applied. ⌘Z replays that inverse through the same edit-apply gate against the current snapshot; ⌘⇧Z is symmetric. Refusals are shown word for word over the page.\n- **Four review rounds with Grok, each closing a real defect with a regression test proven to fail first:**\n  1. Redo pollution — a structural edit landing in the middle of a replay repopulated the redo side that had just been cleared. Fixed by putting ALL stack changes through one serial queue, which makes the window unobservable.\n  2. Keeping the ledger clean.\n  3. The human's finding — \"drafts are excluded\" had over-excluded committed text edits. Title and note inverses were added, guarded on the previous value, so undoing a row that has since been retyped is refused, naming the current text.\n  4. A time-of-check-to-time-of-use race in that previous-value guard, across the write layer's stale-write retries: the guard is now re-checked on every attempt to plan the write.\n- **Un-create:** pressing ⌘Z on a row this session created archives it. This only works for childless rows, treats a node that has since gone as a separate case, and cannot be redone (un-archiving is tracked by the parity-unarchive item). There is still NO delete key; that deferral stays open and the docs say so (docs/editing.md, added under #123's documentation split).\n- **Judgement calls ratified in review:** undoing a \"done\" re-derives the timestamp, because no operation writes a chosen mark value, for consistency; and position is restored as a placement (parent plus the neighbour it comes after), not as a byte-identical ordering key.\n- The stack is per session, 100 entries deep, cleared when you switch outlines, and inert inside drafts, forms and the palette.\n- 920 unit tests and 279 end-to-end tests at merge; continuous integration green on both platforms at the shipping commit.","after":["self-edit"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:33:16-04:00"}
{"id":"dragdrop-multiselect","parent":"feat-mouse","ord":"a1","title":"Drag-drop & multi-select","done":"2026-08-14T09:44:30-04:00","desc":"Drag a node to move it along with everything under it, plus Workflowy's multi-select gestures with bulk complete, move, indent and put-away.\n\n**Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #159; the human approved it, and it was squash-merged at commit bbfeec70 after resolving against master's pull requests #161, #163 and #164.\n- A drop is a gap plus a depth. Rows being carried leave the set of candidate positions wherever rows are measured, including rows in other files — a mirror is not a parent, and reordering inside a mirror lands in the mirror's home file.\n- Picking rows and placing the text caret are mutually exclusive, enforced where the gesture opens and starts; the Trash confirmation keys on the identity of what was picked.\n- Grok's review raised 3 blocking findings and 3 small suggestions, all addressed, each proven by a test that failed before the fix and passed after.\n- **When master was merged in,** this branch adopted the shared readers and test steps introduced by pull request #164, deleting its own copies; it took the shared z-index layers for rows and chrome for its drop lines; and it recorded why the refusal bar does NOT use the shared `saying.ts` helper (a message that stops a run follows UndoSaid's rule of not fading away), while gaining the screen-reader live region it had been missing.\n- Its own flakiness caveat was closed by #164: all 478 scenarios pass under the load that used to drop some.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit bbfeec70.\n- **Deferred, named on the pull request and still open:** a drag-across marquee selection, touch dragging, auto-scrolling at the edges, a delete key (which the human's ruling wanted), and unifying the tone and role of the five different \"said\" surfaces.","after":["self-edit"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00"}
{"id":"input-widgets","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a3","title":"Input affordances","done":"2026-08-14T10:04:48-04:00","desc":"The Workflowy input widgets: typing `!` opens a natural-language date list (which sets the date); `#` and `@` autocomplete tags; and `((` opens a search for creating a mirror. That last one is a parity gap nobody had filed — the interface could retire a placement it had no way to make.\n\n**Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #160; the human approved it, and it was squash-merged at commit 5af9f68 after resolving against master's pull requests #159, #161, #163 and #164.\n- Built on the write-operations layer. The command palette's list-cursor machinery grew a third layer in keys.ts and absorbed two copies that had never been migrated.\n- A latent bug where a blur event was swallowed was fixed, with a timing-independent test in redraws.test.ts, proven to fail before the fix and pass after.\n- February 29 walks forward to the next leap year.\n- **When master was merged in,** it adopted #164's shared readers and test steps, and taught the answering code about the completion list's receipt — one spelling of \"there is no list\" (`theListIsGone`) for three callers. It took the shared row z-index layer for the completion popup, with the argued reason that the error line does NOT use the shared `saying.ts` helper (it mirrors a live failure, not a message lingering after the fact), and it closed two parity entries in the brainstorming inventory.\n- Grok's review raised 1 blocking finding and 5 small suggestions, all addressed.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 5af9f68; 497 end-to-end scenarios and 1,491 unit tests on the merged tree.\n- **Deferred, named on the pull request:** `!` opens the list only, not the calendar; the parity items for `see` and `after` edges want this same widget family, but neither verb is on the wire yet; and the inverse of un-mirroring is still empty, with the reason written in that branch of the code.","after":["self-edit"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00"}
{"id":"palette","parent":"feat-mouse","ord":"a4","title":"Command palette #racket-parity","done":"2026-08-14T10:24:50-04:00","desc":"A Ctrl+K command palette over the write operations and navigation.\n- **History:** the panels item shipped the ⌘K shell, and jump-to-node type-ahead shipped with it (fact-checked 2026-08-13); this item delivered the remaining half — the command ACTIONS.\n\n**Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #162; the human approved it, and it was squash-merged at commit 5b99551 after resolving against master's pull requests #159, #160, #161, #163 and #164.\n- The ••• row menu's verbs are available as palette commands, acting on the node you are zoomed into (palette/ops.ts).\n- Quick capture, matching the earlier Racket version's `olai add`-to-Inbox behaviour; the Inbox naming convention is written down in docs/format.md and in the MCP tool text.\n- Grok's review raised 1 blocking finding (a guard against a capture already in flight) and 3 small suggestions, all addressed, each proven by a test that failed before the fix and passed after.\n- Merging master in kept both palette features intact (23 of 23 palette scenarios) on top of #160's third keyboard layer, with an argued exception recorded for the one write whose promise is that the reader does not move.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit 5b99551; 520 end-to-end scenarios and 1,263 unit tests on the merged tree.\n- Evidence artifact: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/524e3160-f738-4b64-b0e6-eb5300eb3bdf (the behaviour shown is current; its screenshot counts predate the final master merge).\n- **Deferred, named on the pull request:** the operation rows apply only to the zoomed node, and the leftover duplication in the confirmation shell is a `<p>` and a flex row.","after":["navigation","panels"],"see":["search","panels"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00"}
{"id":"md-editing","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"aB","title":"Documents become writable: create and edit .md from olai","done":"2026-08-13T19:26:25-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 on the human's word. **Shipped as PR #148 (squash 10fd1fbf), 2026-08-13.**\n- Documents are writable end to end through the one ops path on both faces: `write_document`/`create_document` on MCP and the web editor drive the same planner → store.commit → audit trail (X-Olai-Writer)\n- The was-guard refuses an external edit (vim mid-session) in words with an explicit \"Overwrite what is there\" (which clobbers the CURRENT disk, verified)\n- A bare calendar day mints that day's note per the vault's own convention (path inferred from the newest daily note)\n- Dirty .md files ride the existing commit flow\n- **The review's MUST-FIX:** document→document navigation reused the Solid page instance (mint hand-off broken, cross-file draft possible)\n  - Fixed by construction (keyed identity) with three scenarios, measured against pre-fix code (the cross-file draft was the deterministic red; the creation doors had been green by frame-ordering luck)\n- Seven+ sabotage-verified pins; CI green at merge head e14e0800 on kolu-ci-1; 1249 unit, 396 e2e\n- **Recorded, not fixed (pre-existing):** fs.watch only sees a brand-new subdirectory on the next probe — the store's 60s backstop covers it\n- Follow-up filed: md-rich-editing\n","see":["daily-notes","commit-whole-repo"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"menu-verbs","parent":"feat-mouse","ord":"a3","title":"The ••• menu learns to write: marks, clear-date, remove-placement, archive, copy-as-text","done":"2026-08-12T14:24:01-04:00","desc":"**Shipped as PR #124**, squash 60631fe (2026-08-12). The mouse's first write path: the ••• menu gained five verbs.\n- Contextual mark section (todo/doing/complete/clear, current mark hidden, ops refusals verbatim in the said line)\n- Clear-date (dated rows only)\n- Remove-placement (mirror rows only, op's own fence)\n- Archive (subtree, inline confirm naming DERIVED descendant count — the author's own /simplify pass caught the count reading rendered rows under done-hidden — honest restore sentence since unarchive does not exist)\n- Copy-as-text (tab-indented plain text, target's subtree on a mirror)\n- **Closed four editor-op-parity children for the mouse:** parity-archive, parity-date (removal half), parity-marks (menu half), parity-remove-mirror\n- The menu moved to web/src/client/menu/ (NodeMenu + verbs/actions/writes/subtree, unit-tested)\n- **Reconciled with #120 at merge:**\n  - Mark collapsed to ONE arm and one resolver spoken by both callers\n  - Archive vs remove stay two verbs with different fences\n  - inverseOf extended so a cleared date is restored as the date it cleared\n  - Menu writes record onto the same ⌘Z stack\n- Survived a 10-point adversarial review and two master syncs (#120's 40-file, #122's 49-file)\n- **CI saga:** two red x86_64-linux e2e legs turned out to be the author's own harness error (odu client wrapped in timeout 600, killing the lane mid-suite)\n  - Proven by a controlled experiment on the CI host itself: master control 7/7 clean, branch equally clean once the timeout wrapper was removed\n  - Final gate 21/21 contexts green both platforms\n- Human approved 2026-08-12\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"multiselect-completion","parent":"feat-mouse","ord":"a2","title":"Picking completed: drag-across marquee, edge auto-scroll, touch drag","done":"2026-08-14T16:33:15-04:00","desc":"The items pull request #159 had deferred, taken up on 2026-08-14 at the human's word. **Merged 2026-08-14** as pull request #169; the human approved it, and it was squash-merged at commit e9adceb.\n- **Drag-across marquee selection:** implemented by allowing specific elements rather than by calling preventDefault; the selection is a horizontal band rather than a rectangle; and the flat page gains its rail on the `ul` element so that depth-zero rows sweep like everywhere else.\n- **Auto-scroll at the edges:** the window decides whether to scroll, the page decides where to; a stationary pointer keeps scrolling.\n- **Touch dragging:** a long press on the bullet lifts the row. Scrolling is claimed at the long-press deadline using a non-passive touchmove handler, rather than by setting `touch-action: none`; that fence is proven by mutation testing and is green alongside a sister scenario.\n- **A design ruling that emerged from the panel:** the bullet is the one drag handle on all devices — both reviewers recommended this independently.\n- Review findings: Grok raised 0 blocking and 2 optional; opencode raised 0 blocking and 3 optional; the union of both sets was addressed. The claim that only one place writes the `userSelect` style is enforced by a sweep in `claims.test.ts`.\n- All 9 required continuous-integration checks passed at commit e9adceb; 551 scenarios and 4,110 steps.\n- **Still deferred:** the delete key (which the human's ruling wanted), unifying tone and role across the \"said\" surfaces, a modifier key for adding to a sweep selection, and finger-sweeping (a permanent no).","changed":"2026-08-27T19:34:12-04:00"}
{"id":"move-to-picker","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a4","title":"Move-to picker: fuzzy-search a new parent anywhere, from the keyboard","done":"2026-08-19T10:57:19-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17.\n\n- **Feature:** a keyboard dialog (Workflowy's ctrl-shift-M) — invoke on the current node, fuzzy-search every node across all outlines as a destination, Enter moves the node (with its subtree) under the chosen parent.\n- **Reuse:** the search machinery; the move is the existing move op.\n- **Cross-file moves:** refuse with a clear message if the op layer cannot express them (outlines are independent trees — archiving is what crosses files).\n- Say so in the dialog rather than hiding the limit.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/7d6706c1a","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/245"}}
{"id":"duplicate-subtree","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a5","title":"Duplicate subtree: copy a node and everything under it in one action","done":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17. One keyboard/menu action clones the current node and its whole subtree as a sibling below (fresh ids throughout, marks preserved or reset? — the author decides and documents; Workflowy preserves). Mirrors under the copied subtree: copy the placement, not the identity. For templates and repeated structures.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"recurring-dates","parent":"feat-kbd","ord":"a6","title":"Recurring dates: a repeat rule on dated nodes — done spawns the next occurrence","done":"2026-08-18T10:06:54-04:00","desc":"Approved overnight 2026-08-17.\n\n- **Feature:** a dated node gains an optional repeat rule (every day / week on Mon / month / year) — keep the grammar small and spelled in the file.\n- **Completing it:** stamps done AND creates the next occurrence at the next date (a fresh node, so the journal keeps the honest history of each completion).\n- Agenda and daily pages show the next occurrence like any dated node.\n- Web editing of the rule beside the date; MCP parity in the same PR.","created":"2026-08-17T21:27:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"md-second-class","parent":"feat-docs","ord":"aD","title":"Where markdown still trails outlines #needs-brainstorm","todo":true,"desc":"- Audit (2026-08-18, re-verified 08-19 — the full capability table lives in this node's git history): markdown is first-class where a file is treated as a file, second-class where content is reasoned about.\n- Much has shipped since filing: document addresses, search over bodies, palette rows, backlinks (#251, #253, #256, #258).\n- Still open, each needing its own ruling before PR-shaped children split off:\n  - The `node.doc` field: nobody can write it, no read tool reports it, and node.ts:199 documents a capture door that doesn't exist.\n  - Documents as see/after edge targets.\n  - Structure verbs for a .md: move, rename, trash.\n  - Chat: doc paths in prose and tool frames aren't pressable; documents aren't armable.\n  - The README never says markdown.\n- Design arc: brainstorming/first-class-documents.md (ruled with the human 2026-08-19).","see":["search-document-bodies"],"created":"2026-08-18T18:51:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-24T16:08:22-04:00","custom":{"brainstorm":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md — the hierarchy (Document ⊃ OutlineDocument/MarkdownDocument), the [document]#[element] address grammar, derived md face now, frontmatter later (ruled with the human 2026-08-19)","citation-2026-08-24":"roadmap review: the \"node.ts:199 documents a capture door that doesn't exist\" bullet is stale — the doc door now sits at node.ts:217 reading \"a node names its document when it is captured; write_document writes what is in it\"; the rest of the open list still verifies (no op writes node.doc; no document move/rename/trash in tools.ts)"}}
{"id":"parity-read-documents","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a0","title":"MCP reads documents: list_documents + read_document","done":"2026-08-19T11:01:25-04:00","desc":"Closes the table's \"2 of 30 tools, no read, no list\" row's read half.\n\n- An agent can now list the documents the directory serves and read one whole.\n- A missing path refuses in the tools' own voice.\n- `face.ts`'s charter says the honest thing (\"nodes and whole documents, never bytes\") with a test pinning the retired \"no file access\" sentence.\n- **Flag argued in surface/ops.ts:** `read_document` and the documents collection resource answer differently on purpose (request-shaped vs render-shaped).","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (parity pick a)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/191d7677","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/251"}}
{"id":"parity-palette-documents","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a1","title":"A document is a row in the ⌘K box, matched by its name","done":"2026-08-19T11:01:28-04:00","desc":"Closes the table's \"zero document rows in the palette\" row.\n\n- The palette and the header search box share one module: a served `.md`/`.html` matches by title/filename and opens its page.\n- The served-path matcher moved to `file/matching.ts` beside the `@` completion.\n- **The create row went NO** — the palette has no create row for outlines either, so offering one to documents alone would invert the imbalance, not remove it (argued on the PR and in docs/search.md).\n- **Doctrine held:** the node grammar reads no `.md` body.","created":"2026-08-19T11:01:28-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"from":"overnight-3 (parity pick b)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/4a9ae71b","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/253"}}
{"id":"fcd-pr1-addresses","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a2","title":"Addresses: the [document]#[element] grammar, URL = / + address — PR 1 of first-class documents","done":"2026-08-19T14:38:19-04:00","desc":"Dispatched 2026-08-19 on the human's word, to Opus.\n\n- **Scope:** the brainstorm's \"PR 1 — addresses\", self-contained.\n  - The branded primitives and the Address sum with `parseAddress`/`printAddress` and the bijection test.\n  - `routes.ts` rebuilt so a content URL is `/` + address.\n  - `/o/` `/doc/` `/n/` deleted in the same diff.\n  - `Pins.olai` titles swept to the new spelling.\n- Compat break blessed by the human.\n\n**pr** — (approved by the human 2026-08-19; 874/874 e2e, evidence visually verified)","created":"2026-08-19T12:43:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:09:19-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","branch":"fcd-addresses","from":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/675886e0","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/256","terminal":"6079d86f-0a99-44ae-b591-5c2d7f0a4a25"}}
{"id":"fcd-pr2-face","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a3","title":"The face: Document = Outline | Markdown, the set serves one collection — PR 2 of first-class documents","done":"2026-08-19T17:09:45-04:00","desc":"Dispatched 2026-08-19 on the human's word, to Opus, immediately after PR 1 (#256) merged.\n\n- **Scope:** the brainstorm's \"PR 2 — the face\", self-contained.\n  - The `Outline | Markdown` sum with the derived markdown face.\n  - The set serves it as THE collection, with the parallel nodes/documents exports deleted and every importer migrated.\n  - Search walks Document and hits carry addresses (closes search-document-bodies).\n  - Palette and backlinks ride the same index.\n- **Carries PR 1's two named deferrals that belong here:**\n  - Route's three content arms collapse toward one Address-carrying arm.\n  - `DocumentPath`'s constructor rule moves into a schema filter the day an Address is decoded off a wire.\n\n**pr** — (approved by the human 2026-08-19; grok + opencode both do-not-object; evidence visually verified; final head df2b94a6 was the conflict-free master merge with #257)","created":"2026-08-19T14:38:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T19:10:00-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","branch":"fcd-face","from":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/29f4ec38","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/258"}}
{"id":"md-yaml-frontmatter","parent":"md-second-class","ord":"a4","title":"YAML frontmatter: parsed nowhere, skipped by exactly one scanner — the ruled-later half of first-class documents","done":"2026-08-20T19:53:31-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-20 from a full-repo markdown survey on the human's ask. Frontmatter is not parsed, not rendered, not stripped — only *skipped*, in one place.\n\n**What exists today:**\n- The only frontmatter-aware code is `format/src/slug.ts:166-186` — `headingsIn` skips a leading `---` block so `title: x` above the closing `---` is not misread as a setext heading. Its own comment: \"Nothing here READS frontmatter.\"\n- No `remark-frontmatter`, `gray-matter`, `js-yaml` or `yaml` dependency in any package.json.\n- Dead leftover: `FRONTMATTER_OPENS = 4` at `slug.ts:258` is declared and referenced nowhere.\n- The hole is stated as deliberate in `format/src/document.ts:82-86`, `docs/format.md:216`, `docs/search.md:113`, `docs/brainstorming/first-class-documents.md:168-181`.\n\n**What a `.md` opening with `---` does today (all three verified):**\n1. **Render:** `remarkParse`+`remarkGfm` with no `remark-frontmatter` draws it as a thematic break plus a setext `<h2>title: x</h2>` — which then gets an autolink anchor and a TOC row the face's (frontmatter-skipping) heading list does not carry. No fixture in the vault has frontmatter, so `markdown/slugs.test.ts` never measures this divergence.\n2. **Face title:** `firstLine` (`format/src/documents.ts:329`) does not skip the block, so the sidebar/palette title is the literal `---` or the first YAML line.\n3. **Indexing:** `tagsIn`/`linksIn` scan the whole body including the block, so `#tag`-looking YAML values index as tags.\n\n**The shape of the fix** (per the brainstorm's ruling, needs its own ruling before a PR splits off):\n- `remark-frontmatter` in the browser pipeline, hidden from render (no phantom h2/hr, no TOC row).\n- `firstLine`/`tagsIn`/`linksIn` learn the same skip `headingsIn` already has.\n- Then the door the brainstorm names: surface YAML keys as document *properties*, so `prop:`/`has:`/`date:` stop selecting zero documents (the consequence pinned by `format/src/document.test.ts:72-79`).","created":"2026-08-20T17:17:45-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T19:53:31-04:00","custom":{"brainstorm":"brainstorming/first-class-documents.md:168-181 — frontmatter ruled 'later' with the human 2026-08-19","from":"repo survey 2026-08-20 (Opus explore agent), on the human's ask"}}
{"id":"website","ord":"aJ","title":"Website: GitHub Pages at olai.kolu.dev","done":"2026-08-12T12:16:16-04:00","desc":"**Shipped in PR #123** (squash a8a0105, merged 2026-08-12T16:14:32Z) — authored and merged by the human directly, recorded here by the orchestrator after the fact.\n- A static landing page in the app's chalk palette (website/index.html, no build step)\n- CNAME olai.kolu.dev\n- Pages workflow deploying website/ on master pushes touching it\n\n**THE DOCS CONVENTION IT ESTABLISHES** (binding on all future PRs; relayed to the three in-flight agents at merge):\n- README.md is a POINTER (site + docs/index.md + HACKING.md) — future user-facing copy changes go to website/index.html\n- docs/ split into index.md, running.md, git.md, chat.md beside architecture.md and format.md\n- docs/git.md owns the git-integration story\n\n**One-time settings the human owns:**\n- Repo Pages source = GitHub Actions with the custom domain\n- Cloudflare CNAME olai → juspay.github.io\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:55-04:00"}
{"id":"website-refresh","parent":"website","ord":"a0","title":"Website refresh: six shipped PRs the site never heard about","done":"2026-08-13T11:13:42-04:00","desc":"Shipped as pull request #137 (squash commit df23fe3, merged 2026-08-13).\n- The website caught up with what pull requests #128 to #133 had shipped:\n  - A new Agenda section, whose description of what counts as overdue matches `agenda.ts` word for word, along with how rows are picked and how plain occurrences behave.\n  - A Preferences section showing all fifteen palettes, hex value for hex value, as chips wearing their own colours.\n  - The cycle of marks as actually shipped — the diagram was corrected against `server/src/edit.ts`, because \"done\" is not a stopping point; the author caught the brief's error.\n  - Chat file diffs.\n  - A general sweep of the `#format` section, the hero, and the daily-notes material.\n- **Grok's review:** nothing that had to be fixed; accuracy verified against the packages; both judgement calls accepted.\n  - Two optional suggestions were taken in a final round — the daily-notes mock-up now shows scheduled and finished work side by side, a fact about day pages the site had never shown.\n- Continuous integration was 10 of 10 green on Linux at commit ddf0dea.\n- **A ruling honoured:** the site describes MASTER, so a visual depth touch-up follows only if and when the depth work merges.\n- The website checklist line in HACKING.md keeps it current from here on.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"inbox-count-placement","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aE","title":"Inbox sits by Agenda and wears its count","done":"2026-08-21T08:23:25-04:00","desc":"The human, 2026-08-20 ~21:0x (screenshot ruling): the Inbox door (#297: above Trash, at the sidebar's bottom shelf) should (1) carry a COUNT of what's in the inbox, (2) sit CLOSE TO 'Agenda' rather than down by Trash, and (3) be consistently styled with the rest of the chrome re: count badge and colour — Agenda's badge is the reference. Grok lane; opencode debates; auto-merge on visual evidence.\n\n**pr** — the door beside Agenda, the count in Agenda's own chip (layout/chip.ts), hidden at zero\n\n**merge** — pre-approved after visual evidence","created":"2026-08-20T20:36:54-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:00:40-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/28bf6880","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/308"}}
