{"id":"brainstorm-declares","parent":"feat-outline","ord":"aF","title":"Clean brainstorm's three prose-carrying values, then declare it (doc or path, base root)","todo":true,"created":"2026-08-25T22:06:39-04:00","custom":{"from":"#403's report, 2026-08-25 — 3 of brainstorm's 6 values carry prose after the path; clean-then-declare (the #395 migration's own rule), and the doc-vs-path choice inherits prop-brief's open ruling"}}
{"id":"terminal-door-polish","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a6","title":"Terminal door polish","todo":true,"desc":"The one part still standing from the human's first live session with pull request #405 (2026-08-26): giving the pane a face you can take in at a glance. That would be the themed screenshot kolu's own `screenshot` command renders, which preserves layout, box-drawing characters, colours and the cursor — for use before a pane has been opened, or in overview lists. It was measured at about 1.5 seconds and 170KB including the time to start kolu's command-line tool; the `screen.image` verb is already on padi's surface in the pinned contract.\n\nThe other face — what you get when you actually look — is already settled: the live pane (see the pane-live item).\n\nThe rule agreed at the fifth design-debate sitting applies: if this work reopens kolu's `screen.*` procedures, then the separate request for a screen-tail verb (kolu-screen-tail) should ride along in the same change, since the cost is being paid once anyway.\n\nContext from that session: the human asked whether the text pane is glanceable (measured above; it is nearly free once the app is already running), and was confused by the black dot — a parked terminal draws in the muted colour, which reads as dead. A waiting terminal is deliberately folded into parked, and there is no legend anywhere.","created":"2026-08-26T10:34:55-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human's first live session with #405, 2026-08-26 — the text pane's glanceability question (measured: PNG ~1.5s/170KB incl. CLI boot, near-free in-app) and the black-dot confusion (parked draws --color-muted, reading as dead; waiting deliberately folds into parked; no legend anywhere)"}}
{"id":"orch-kolu","parent":"feat-orch","ord":"a5","title":"Kolu — the padi/fleet integration family","desc":"**THE NORTH STAR (the human, 2026-08-26, on seeing the live board + ordering the live pane): \"With this, there is virtually no reason to check Kolu. I can monitor terminals in Olai itself, until I need to type something in.\"** Olai is the fleet's monitoring surface; kolu is where you go to TYPE. Every item in this family serves that sentence — and the one remaining gap it names (typing) is the actions phase (orch-p3-actions), when its day comes.","created":"2026-08-26T10:38:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-26T16:00:59-04:00"}
{"id":"dock-row","parent":"terminal-door","ord":"a3","title":"The Dock row export","done":"2026-08-27T11:26:10-04:00","desc":"One visual vocabulary: kolu ships its Dock terminal row as a package, and olai's terminal-property face IS that row. Shipped and verified live on pull request #405.\n\n**Layout:** of the three options rendered for the human, they chose the one where the full row IS the terminal property, with the raw terminal id shown beside it (they ruled for both).\n\n**The general seam behind it:** a property's key or kind maps to a block renderer that owns its own row, with the run of small chips as the default. The terminal property is the first thing to use it, and a renderer can register without the property drawer learning anything about it (which has its own test).\n\n**Attention feeds** are mirrored from kolu and were verified correct by design against a live padi daemon: the `waiting` state is just lingering and earns no highlight; only `awaiting_user` does. The violet highlight is rare on purpose.\n\nThe homegrown vocabulary of status faces is deleted. The end-to-end test asserts against the row attributes kolu's package publishes. The wire carries the row's own bundle of fields plus the owner, with the agent's state as a nullable string, narrowed through the row package's own type guard; the @olai/surface package never imports kolu's terminal vocabulary.\n\n**Open questions parked for the human:** (1) the per-repository stripe renders in a neutral colour — kolu's own per-repository identity colour is available, but the palette choice has not been made; (2) \"a terminal idle for an hour should be loud\" — styling by age is a product question and is not wired up.","created":"2026-08-26T10:59:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human, 2026-08-26: \"Why not export the Kolu Dock UI as is as a package… This way we don't have to invent yet another shit in Olai\"","merge":"merge-human","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/405"}}
{"id":"dock-row-kolu","parent":"dock-row","ord":"a0","title":"kolu PR: extract the row","done":"2026-08-27T10:30:45-04:00","desc":"The kolu side of the work, merged as commit f9ace75e: a new package `@kolu/solid-dockrow` containing the Dock row, with kolu's own Dock consuming it so there is no second copy to drift. Plus the attention feeds on `@kolu/padi-client`, and the additions ratified at the fifth design-debate sitting: the row's own type-narrowing guard, the daemon leaf entry points, the canonical dependency-hydration script, and the dependency-closure emitter. The paired change in the drishti project (pull request #147) rode along and is merged.\n\n**The provider-and-consumer rule this piece of work proved:** olai pins the kolu branch's head commit and compiles against it while it is still UNMERGED. Anything olai finds comes back as an amendment round on the kolu branch; kolu's pre-merge quality routine waits until the consumer is satisfied; continuous integration runs once at the end; and the human reviews both pull requests together.\n\nEight findings came back from the consumer through that loop, and all eight were closed before merge — two of them contract gaps that a merge-first, consume-later order would have shipped.","created":"2026-08-26T11:01:17-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/dock-row-kolu.md","dispatched":"2026-08-26T11:14:00-04:00","drishti-pr":"https://github.com/srid/drishti/pull/147","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/commit/f9ace75e6dc25a2bbada31bd1daacf0c28405934","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2217","retired":"2026-08-27T11:02:00-04:00","terminal":"607721a1","worktree":"kolu/.worktrees/dock-row-export"}}
{"id":"debate-kolu","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a7","title":"Debate: kolu code in olai (5th sitting)","done":"2026-08-26T11:52:15-04:00","desc":"The fifth three-agent design debate, ratified by the human at midday on 2026-08-26. The conclusion is filed at [lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-26.md](../lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-26.md); the individual agents' turn files are in the git-ignored directory debates/kolu-maintenance-2026-08-26/.\n\n**Verdict, converged and unanimous** (only two of the three seats — an outage at the kimi model provider took the third): kolu's code is NOT tangled into olai where the feeling pointed. The five places kolu appears are composed rather than tangled, the chat integration is an honest twin rather than a duplicate (the human asked \"can chat use kolu-client?\" — the answer is NO), and olai's projection of a kolu terminal was acquitted by actually counting how much would break under plausible changes.\n\nThey are genuinely intertwined in exactly TWO places:\n1. **Restating kolu's visual vocabulary.** This dies by DELETION along with the dock-row work: olai never again declares its own fixed set of kolu states; the row package's bundle of properties is the one fixed set, and it exports its own narrowing; and @olai/surface never imports @kolu/terminal-vocab.\n2. **The dependency-hydration machinery.** Kolu ships the leaf entry points, the hydration script and a closure emitter; olai deletes its osfacts pin, its guard script, its type graft and its hand-maintained package list, down to a list of seeds.\n\nPublishing to npm or any registry was killed on all four tests.\n\n**The dispatch ruling** (the human): no new pull requests — both fixes ride the lanes already open. Both landed.\n\nThe survey done on 2026-08-27 (the kolu-leak-survey item) audits what this sitting missed: the second point above is still almost entirely unpaid on olai's side, and the sitting did not look at the re-attach policy at all.","created":"2026-08-26T11:11:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/debate-kolu-2026-08-26.md","from":"the human, 2026-08-26: \"I feel like our Kolu integration code in Olai is still complecting/intertwined\" — conduct a hickey & lowy debate on the way kolu code is maintained in the olai codebase"}}
{"id":"debate-kolu-fable","parent":"debate-kolu","ord":"a0","title":"seat: fable — packaging/walls","done":"2026-08-26T11:52:13-04:00","desc":"This agent's assigned question in the debate (the Fable model held this seat), on packaging and boundaries: which of the boundaries between olai and kolu are real — starting from the earlier ruling that @olai/kolu-client exists to prevent a dependency cycle — and which are mere ceremony; what the dock-row package does to those boundaries; and whether the kolu vocabulary inside @olai/surface is a genuine boundary or just a second copy.","created":"2026-08-26T11:11:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"terminal":"d7543299"}}
{"id":"debate-kolu-grok","parent":"debate-kolu","ord":"a1","title":"seat: grok — upstream into kolu","done":"2026-08-26T11:52:13-04:00","desc":"This agent's assigned question in the debate (the Grok model held this seat), on what should move upstream into kolu: which things olai currently maintains ought to be kolu's — the status vocabulary? the attention fold? the job the hydration scripts do? — with every candidate judged against the test agreed at the fourth sitting: a thing belongs upstream when it is generally useful but not specific to kolu's own problem domain.","created":"2026-08-26T11:11:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:05:06-04:00","custom":{"terminal":"ea59d1ef"}}
{"id":"debate-kolu-pi","parent":"debate-kolu","ord":"a2","title":"Seat: module boundaries — the agent holding this seat had to be replaced twice when its model provider went down","cancelled":"2026-08-26T11:28:24-04:00","desc":"This seat's assigned question: treat the five places kolu code lives as a module-boundary exercise in Rich Hickey's sense (which layer each belongs to); decide whether chat/src/kolu.ts and kolu-client are a needless duplication or an honest twin; and own the table of how much would break under each plausible change to kolu.\n\n**What happened to the seat:** the pi agent opened it, and its model provider died mid-read — the kimi-k3 model returned HTTP 429 \"no deployments\", retries failed twice, and it produced no turn files. Its terminal (eca01375) was left warm at 49% of its context window. The orchestrator swapped in Claude Opus (terminal f214e21d), but the human overrode that before its brief was submitted: \"Pi failed, try opencode instead.\" The Opus terminal was killed unused, freeing the Opus slot. Opencode took the seat — which follows the fourth sitting's own precedent, since this was opencode's seat on 2026-08-19 — in terminal c0ea4db8, with its `command` property written onto its roster entry on the human's order (the row had had none since it was retired).\n\nThe human's fallback, ruled in advance: if opencode also failed, the sitting would proceed with two seats (fable and grok).","created":"2026-08-26T11:11:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-opencode","terminal":"c0ea4db8"}}
{"id":"dr-headers","parent":"dock-row","ord":"a1","title":"Five-home header map","done":"2026-08-26T12:06:24-04:00","desc":"Add a cross-referencing comment to the header of each of the five files where kolu code lives in olai (surface/kolu.ts, kolu-client/index.ts, the server-side join, the web property files, and chat/src/kolu.ts), so that a reader finds the map of where things live in the files themselves rather than nowhere.\n\nShipped — and the survey on 2026-08-27 found the map has since drifted: all five copies still say the properties \"draw the chip\", when it is a Dock row now; and claimants.ts, web/src/client/padi/* and TerminalDoor.tsx carry no map at all. Fixing this rides on the \"Make the kolu-import rule machine-checked\" item under the encapsulation fold.","created":"2026-08-26T11:59:37-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"dr-row","parent":"dock-row","ord":"a2","title":"The Dock row IS the terminal property (B)","done":"2026-08-26T16:05:44-04:00","desc":"Pin olai to the head commit of kolu's dock-row branch, and make the terminal property render kolu's imported row exactly as kolu's own Dock draws it — glyph, words, recency, intent, per-repository stripe, pull-request badge, and the way a sleeping terminal recedes.\n\nOn the wire: the row's own bundle of fields plus the owner; the agent's state as a nullable string; narrowing done with the row package's exported type guard; and the check performed in one place, `rowOf`.\n\n**Layout ruled by the human on 2026-08-26 at about 13:17**, from three rendered choices posted on [#405's comment](https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/405#issuecomment-5428553287): option B — the full Dock row IS the terminal property. The row replaces the small chip outright, and always taking two lines per terminal is accepted as the cost. (The screenshots revealed why: the Dock row is a two-line subgrid row that needs a section container, so it cannot sit inline within a run of chips — the inline option was never actually available.)\n\n**With a refactoring directive from the human in the same breath** (\"custom block render\", meaning it in Rich Hickey's sense of composing rather than special-casing): build the general seam today. A property whose kind or key maps to a BLOCK renderer owns its own row, with the run of chips as the default. The terminal-to-Dock-row mapping is the seam's first consumer; the existing special case for the terminal key generalizes into it and never gains a one-off sibling. Future properties plug into the same socket without the drawer being touched.","created":"2026-08-26T11:59:42-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"dr-delete","parent":"dock-row","ord":"a3","title":"Delete the homegrown face vocabulary","done":"2026-08-26T14:17:51-04:00","desc":"Delete olai's homegrown vocabulary for kolu terminal states: the `DotFace` type, the `LIVE_FACES` and `DOT_FACES` tables, face.ts and face.test.ts, the `.olai-dot` and `.olai-dot-slot` CSS classes and the `olai-dot-awaiting` keyframes, and the `FACE_SAYS` table — all gone along with the Dock row adoption.\n\nNo set of kolu states declared by olai survives. (`KoluStatus` stays, because it describes olai's own connection to kolu, not padi's vocabulary.) The @olai/surface package never imports @kolu/terminal-vocab.\n\nThe author caught one that the debate's sweep for recurrences had missed: `FleetTerminal.state` (active | sleeping | parked) was ALSO padi's own record state, declared as a fixed set by olai. It was ruled on the standing authority of the already-ratified rule: it dies into the row bundle's own fields rather than riding along as a plain string.","after":["dr-row"],"created":"2026-08-26T11:59:49-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"dr-attention","parent":"dock-row","ord":"a4","title":"Mirror the attention feeds","done":"2026-08-26T16:05:44-04:00","desc":"Have @olai/kolu-client mirror padi's urgency cell and its activity stream (both of which were grown in the kolu-side pull request). The \"blocked on you\" emphasis then computes exactly what kolu's own Dock computes, by calling the pure function kolu exports for it — full fidelity, which is the option the human chose.","created":"2026-08-26T11:59:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"pane-live","parent":"terminal-door","ord":"a4","title":"The pane live-streams","done":"2026-08-26T17:40:23-04:00","desc":"Open pane = live terminal, shipped on #405. The wire: padi's `streams.terminalAttach` — a snapshot first frame carrying its grid, then deltas; the server holds the attach on the fleet's one connection; the browser never dials padi. Renderer: plain xterm.js, dynamic import.\n\nTHE GRID RULING (the human, 2026-08-26, overruling the orchestrator's observe-only design): \"Attach means the width/height should be the same on all clients, like it used to before.\" The pane is a PLAIN kolu-style attach client — attaches at its measured grid (resizeTo, last-attach-wins), renders 1:1, no scaling transform. A foreign resize re-snapshots and the pane renders the new grid 1:1 wrapped, exactly as kolu's own client does.\n\nStanding rules: read-only by design (typing is kolu's until the actions phase); the terminal renders indistinguishable from kolu; first-frame deadline (4s, olai's own derivation), clean-end-recoverable and refusal-is-an-answer as olai policy; evidence photographed against REAL busy streams.\n\nThe re-attach defects the 2026-08-27 survey found (the unrefilled budget, the false one-attach claim) were FIXED AND FENCED by the kolu-complection fold on this same PR. The pane's implementation moves to @olai/kolu-ui with the encapsulation fold.","created":"2026-08-26T16:00:01-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T18:42:01-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human, 2026-08-26 ~16:05: \"when user opens the terminal to look at snapshot, let's live stream it! Why not?\""}}
{"id":"app-title-hostname","parent":"feat-nav","ord":"aF","title":"App title: olai [hostname]","done":"2026-08-26T19:39:15-04:00","desc":"One pull request: the app names its HOST everywhere it names itself — \"olai [$hostname]\" in the browser tab title, the header wordmark, and the served progressive-web-app manifest. The hostname is the server's, crossing to the browser on a new `app.get` surface procedure (shaped like the existing `who.get`); ONE spelling of the name (`appName`) feeds all three faces; and the test harness pins it via `OLAI_HOSTNAME`, which doubles as an honest, documented override for operators.\n\n**Pipeline state:** the pi agent reported pull request #410 at commit b570c9ee, with the local test suites green, evidence uploaded and no deferrals. (Its first session died because the JUSPAY_API_KEY had expired — see orchestrator/reminders.olai.)\n\nBecause a pi-authored lane gets two reviews, both were done and both terminals have been retired, with the verdicts left permanently on the pull request:\n- Claude Opus approved, with three findings it thought should be fixed: the manifest's `short_name` truncation eats the hostname; the chat notice still says a bare \"olai\"; and composing the name at listen time versus reading it on demand can drift.\n- Grok approved, with one finding that should be fixed — the sharp one: a failed `app.get` is never retried, because surface procedures are not re-issued when the connection redials. So a page that boots during a server restart keeps the bare \"olai\" for its whole life, and the screenshot taken while waiting bakes that in. The fix is to re-ask when the connection comes back up.\n\nNext: a round with pi folding in all four findings, with no deferrals. Then the orchestrator runs continuous integration once, verifies the evidence frame by frame, and merges automatically — this lane needs no mark from the human.","created":"2026-08-26T17:48:56-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/app-title-hostname.md","dispatched":"2026-08-26T17:58:00-04:00","from":"the human, 2026-08-26 ~17:55: the app title today is \"olai\", but it should instead be \"olai [$hostname]\" — in pwa, browser tab title, application header","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/fd4e1b1070628f29fac461f47a605604b8c0d4a4","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/410","terminal":"d6263a84","worktree":".worktrees/app-title-hostname"}}
{"id":"downstream-pins","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a8","title":"Downstream kolu pins: drishti + odu","done":"2026-08-27T11:00:47-04:00","desc":"drishti #147 is merged (476b7674) on the human's green-CI word — drishti runs the merged kolu f9ace75e. The odu half is SKIPPED by the human's word (2026-08-27 ~11:00): it is not a pin bump but an effect migration, carried to its own item.","created":"2026-08-27T10:32:28-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T11:00:47-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","dispatched":"2026-08-27T10:34:00-04:00","drishti-merge":"https://github.com/srid/drishti/commit/476b7674","drishti-pr":"https://github.com/srid/drishti/pull/147","from":"the human, 2026-08-27 ~10:33: kolu#2217 approved — \"I want both drishti and odu PRs using the new Kolu\"","merge":"merge-human","terminal":"607721a1"}}
{"id":"odu-effect-migration","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"a9","title":"odu onto the new kolu","cancelled":"2026-08-28T08:53:10-04:00","desc":"THE HUMAN'S OWN ITEM, not an orchestrator lane — recorded so the board knows where odu's kolu pin stands.\n\nTheir first attempt, odu pull request #91 (\"Advance kolu to master — Effect holds at 4.0.0-rc.110\"), was CLOSED UNMERGED: its premise — holding Effect at rc.110 — was inverted the same day when kolu master itself moved to Effect 4.0.0-rc.112 (kolu #2218). Any next attempt targets rc.112 directly.\n\nStill worth shedding whenever odu is next opened: its own copy of the dependency-hydration script (kolu owns the canonical one now; the two had diverged by 56 lines) and its hand-rolled per-package extraction (kolu's nix/consumer.nix plus the emitted closure replace it — the same machinery olai adopted in #405).","created":"2026-08-27T11:00:37-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:10-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human, 2026-08-27 ~11:00: \"I decided to skip the Odu PR\" — skipped as an ORCHESTRATOR lane; they took it themselves","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/odu/pull/91"}}
{"id":"kolu-leak-survey","parent":"orch-kolu","ord":"aA","title":"Where kolu still leaks into olai","done":"2026-08-27T11:26:10-04:00","desc":"The survey that produced the fold. Five read-only passes (web, server+surface, chat+ops+format, build+nix+pin, the kolu-client wall) over #405's worktree at 7c218136, measured against the fifth sitting's ledger. Its findings are now the todo list under td-kolu-fold.\n\nTHE VERDICT WORTH KEEPING: the wall holds where it was built — the padi dial, reconnect, backoff and contract-skew handling live only in @olai/kolu-client, no browser file imports @kolu/padi-client, @olai/surface declares no padi shape, and the FleetTerminal narrowing (agent state as a plain string, guards imported from kolu) is honest and single-exit at both ends. What is NOT encapsulated is not the wire but kolu's semantics, vocabulary and packaging — which is why the fold's items are about labels, rules and build machinery, and none of them move the wall.","see":["td-kolu-fold","kolu-consumer-exports"],"created":"2026-08-27T11:02:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T11:26:10-04:00","custom":{"dispatched":"2026-08-27T11:05:00-04:00","from":"the human, 2026-08-27 ~11:00: \"while kolu-client encapsulates most of the Kolu stuff, I still think not all of Kolu stuff has been encapsulated. so Kolu code is still spread across Olai (complect'ing). Identify all those areas\""}}
{"id":"chat-subagents-pinned","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aH","title":"Subagents leave the transcript","done":"2026-08-27T13:13:02-04:00","desc":"The chat transcript carries the MAIN agent's voice only. A fan-out used to draw every subagent's tool call inline behind a rail, and five concurrent subagents buried the conversation — the human's screenshot was the before.\n\nREADY FOR THE HUMAN'S MERGE WORD at head 0fb81235: mergeStateStatus CLEAN, ten required contexts pass including CodeQL, zero open alerts, one CI run, no re-verdict round. Evidence frame-verified by the orchestrator: the strip naming four real subagents by their work against the pinned adapter (not \"Task\"), a permission form whose lane reads the agent's own description, and a 390×844 handset with the shelf open and the composer reachable.\n\nThe shape: a subagent's calls are filed under the Agent frame that spawned them and reached through a door — the strip above the scroll while the agent is out, the spawning row's own door forever after. The spawning row keeps its place, its ending and its report. A question a subagent ASKS is the deliberate exception and stays in the conversation, its lane naming who is asking.\n\nTHE ROUND'S REAL FINDING was the fixture, not the fix: both reviewers independently found that the ask form's lane still said \"Task\" against the shipped adapter, and the suite could not see it because the fake agent titled its spawns with descriptions while the real adapter titles them with the tool name. The fake now lies the way the adapter lies, three vacuous assertions became falsifiable, and reverting the one-line fix now fails the suite — verified by reverting. Running the feature end to end then turned up one nobody flagged: the new transcript floor pushed the composer off a handset with no shelf open, the same failure one surface along, since somebody who cannot reach the box cannot answer the form.\n\nOne observation for the human's own eyes on deploy: a spawning row still reads \"Task\" in the transcript by the pinned-title rule (a row is what the call was called when it appeared) — the strip and the doors carry the real names. Legible, but four identical row titles is a judgement call only they can make.","created":"2026-08-27T11:08:23-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T18:44:02-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/chat-subagent-pin.md","dispatched":"2026-08-27T11:14:00-04:00","from":"the human, 2026-08-27 ~11:07 (with a screenshot of the panel drowning in subagent rows): \"When subagents run, their outputs go to chat interleaved. I don't think this should be the case. Only main agent output comes in chat. But the subagents are pinned at the top, and if the user clicks on them we can perhaps preview their output somehow.\"","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/e0a476a800129a7f9037c61bb910f254cdb07955","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/412","retired":"2026-08-27T13:14:00-04:00","terminal":"db1f0d7a","worktree":".worktrees/subagent-pin"}}
{"id":"leak-machinery","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a4","title":"The packaging machinery","done":"2026-08-27T13:37:30-04:00","desc":"The last piece of the untangling work, ordered onto pull request #405 as its own commit (following the human's no-new-pull-requests rule and their demand for an \"exhaustive/expensive/perfect win\"). Kolu pull request #2219 unblocked it, and the author verified at that pin that the seed packages now evaluate and that kolu's `consumer.nix` walks to 32 package members — one for one with olai's hand-maintained list — including the new build-time check that refuses if the osfacts dependency pin has drifted (it wants `{ src; revision; }` and retires olai's check-osfacts-pin.sh).\n\n**What the migration does:** nix/kolu.nix shrinks to a list of seed packages plus the pinned-sources graft; olai's copy of the hydration step is deleted and its three call sites moved to kolu's own hydration script; check-kolu-deps.sh shrinks to an assertion about external dependencies; check-osfacts-pin.sh is retired; and the Bun process-signals shim is deleted once link.ts, fake-padi.ts and mirror.test.ts move to the leaf entry points. This ripples through shell.nix, default.nix, flake.nix, nix/nixpkgs.nix and the justfile, and renames the Nix attributes (for example `kolu-src-surface` becomes `kolu-surface`).\n\n**A standing requirement carried into it:** the version `effect 4.0.0-rc.110` is spelled out in 14 places, and only the root manifest is guarded — the root's overrides block is all that saves the other 13. That constraint must land CHECKED rather than hoped for; it is exactly the class of problem that silently broke odu.","created":"2026-08-27T11:12:50-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"leak-vocabulary","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a3","title":"Kolu's words, typed by hand","done":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00","desc":"Small pieces of kolu's closed vocabulary spelled as literals in olai, where nothing can catch a rename. The wire deliberately carries these as plain strings (ratified, finding 1) and the GUARDS are correctly imported from @kolu/solid-dockrow/rowValues — but the FALLBACKS are not: TerminalDoor.tsx:219-221,231 types \"idle\", \"shell\", \"none\", \"idle\" as the values used exactly when kolu has moved.\n\nThe drift detector is already reading positive: props/terminal.test.ts:32-33 and held.browsertest.ts:34-35 use glyph \"claude\" (kolu's set is claude-code|codex|opencode|grok|pi|shell) and motion \"pulse\" (kolu's set is spin|glow|none). Neither exists. The suite is green.\n\nOther members: props/recency.ts:42 restates the Dock's time phrases and diverges in both modes (olai \"7m\"/\"\" vs kolu \"5m ago\"/\"just now\" and the DASH — kolu's RecencyCell explicitly forbids the empty violet capsule olai now renders for a blocked terminal with no activity); @kolu/terminal-vocab exports relativeTime + DASH and is already hydrated. surface/src/terminals.ts:78 reimplements kolu's unique-prefix addressing rule and is deliberately byte-exact where kolu is case-insensitive (documented as a choice — honest, but it is kolu's rule living in the one package the browser bundles). PADI_SOCKET is spelled in olai ~11 times while @kolu/detect:66 exports PADI_SOCKET_ENV and chat/src/kolu.ts's own docstring falsely claims it consumes it.\n\nWhat kolu would have to grow, stated once: narrowPip(bag) (the four guards WITH their fallbacks) and recencyText(mode, at, now) on @kolu/solid-dockrow/rowValues; both pure, both in packages olai already hydrates.","created":"2026-08-27T11:12:50-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00"}
{"id":"leak-protocol","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a2","title":"The attach rules, restated instead of imported","done":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00","desc":"web/src/client/props/attaching.ts names kolu's four re-attach rules in its header and imports NOTHING from @kolu/padi-client/attach — the rules are held by a comment agreeing with a fold. Kolu's four policy helpers (isSnapshotFrame, snapshotGrid, snapshotGridMoved, snapshotAnswersGrid) are exported, hydrated, and used nowhere in olai; they are typed on padi's frame, which olai re-types at the mirror, so nothing above the seam can apply them. LivePane.tsx:206,282 open-codes two of them.\n\nRule 2c — the mid-stream foreign resize, where another viewer reflows the shared pty and you receive deltas laid out for a grid you never asked for, indefinitely, with nothing to time out — is not handled at all, and cannot be until the pty grid rides the wire.\n\nFIRST_FRAME_MS = 4_000 is documented as \"kolu's own number and adopted rather than re-derived\". Kolu's number is 45_000, and its derivation is that the deadline must sit ABOVE the cheap repairs it depends on (1s stream retry, the 25s watchdog) — \"under 25s the deadline PREEMPTS the watchdog it depends on\". Kolu's attach contract says the number is legitimately the consumer's policy; the provenance claim is what is false, and a reader who trusts it will \"fix\" olai toward a number kolu does not have.","created":"2026-08-27T11:12:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00"}
{"id":"live-defects-405","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a1","title":"Two live defects in #405","done":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00","desc":"Not tidiness — both confirmed by the orchestrator reading the code directly, both in the PR holding for the merge word.\n\n1. THE RE-ATTACH BUDGET IS NEVER REFILLED. attaching.ts's onFrame sets seen:true but never resets attempts; LivePane.tsx:353's `carried` persists for the pane's whole life; LivePane.tsx:206 bumps a new generation on EVERY grid change, i.e. every pane resize, and :248 spends budget via again(carried). Six resizes over a pane's lifetime exhausts ATTEMPT_BUDGET=6 and the pane prints \"this terminal stopped answering — it has probably closed\" over a live agent. Kolu's own re-attaching stream carries the missing half out loud: \"a delivered frame refills both budgets\". The fix is one line in onFrame.\n\n2. \"ONE ATTACH\" IS FALSE. kolu.md:9 says twelve tabs watching one terminal are twelve readers and one attach; kolu-client/src/mirror.ts:428 opens a fresh face per call with no refcount, no dedup, and @kolu/surface invokes a stream's source once per subscription with no dedup by input key. N panes on one terminal are N padi attaches. link.ts:22-24 promises the refcount exists (\"phase 6's stream … is refcounted BECAUSE it is per-terminal\") — phase 6's stream is mirror.ts:428 and it is not. It converges rather than resize-warring (epoch ≥1 carries no grid), so the harm is the claim, in four comments and one user-facing doc.","created":"2026-08-27T11:12:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00"}
{"id":"leak-map-wrong","parent":"td-encapsulation","ord":"a0","title":"Make the kolu-import rule machine-checked","done":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00","desc":"Three leftover jobs, all part of the encapsulation fold this node sits under:\n\n1. Add a script check that FAILS if any package outside kolu-client and kolu-ui imports kolu's code. Today nothing enforces the rule — it's only review discipline.\n2. Fix architecture.md's dependency table, which currently admits the rule is \"enforced by nothing but review\".\n3. Rewrite the five identical code comments that tell a reader where kolu code lives — they still describe the old layout; after the fold they should say: two packages, kolu-client and kolu-ui.\n\nDone when the fold's check-script commit lands.","created":"2026-08-27T11:12:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:05-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-consumer-exports","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a0","title":"The exports olai hand-copies (kolu)","done":"2026-08-27T17:21:30-04:00","desc":"Open as kolu pull request #2219 at commit 71eab045, titled \"The eleven exports a consumer was hand-copying — and the three that argued back\"; later MERGED as squash commit 39206d18 on the human's approval. Olai's pull request #405 pinned the branch head and compiled against it while it was still unmerged — the arrangement where the consumer validates the provider before merge; findings were relayed through the orchestrator.\n\n**Exported by kolu so olai can delete its copies:** `narrowRowVocab(wire)`, returning `{ pip, bucket, known, unrecognised }`, and `recencyText(mode, at, now)`, both on `@kolu/solid-dockrow/rowValues`; `compactDelta`, `agoPhrase` and `compactPhrase` in a new `@kolu/terminal-vocab/duration`; `resolveTerminalId` and `ResolveResult` in a new `@kolu/padi-client/terminalId`; `tailLines` in a new `@kolu/padi-client/screenTail`; `@kolu/detect` and terminal-themes declared safe to vendor (this is the item that unblocked kolu's consumer.nix for olai); the osfacts pin plus a build-time refusal on drift, in consumer-closure.json and consumer.nix (retiring olai's check-osfacts-pin.sh); `DEFAULT_FONT_SIZE` on terminal-themes; `gridsEqual` on `@kolu/terminal-vocab/schema`; `HASHED_NAMING`, `chunkPattern` and `chunkUrlPattern` on `@kolu/surface-app`; and all.css carrying its own prerequisites.\n\n**Three requests were argued down, each with evidence:**\n- A Solid-free leaf entry point for rowValues is unnecessary — that entry point's dependency graph is 35 files with zero edges to solid-js, so the missing thing was the GUARANTEE, and the promise became a test instead.\n- The xterm-kit lifecycle and its `<Xterm>` component are not offered: they statically import xterm plus six add-ons, which would defeat the consuming pane's lazy import, and vendoring the package would cost a consumer ten new root dependencies to reach one integer. The two things actually needed came out instead as `DEFAULT_FONT_SIZE` and `gridsEqual`.\n- The fake-padi test library is declined: neither drishti nor odu imports padi-client, so the \"three consumers justify it\" arithmetic fails a simple search, and exporting a fake with no corpus of contract tests would move the drift into kolu, where continuous integration has no signal about it. (The thing that actually did break was `tailLines`, which shipped.)\n\n**One design note worth keeping:** `recencyText` could NOT be built on terminal-vocab's `relativeTime`, because that clamps clock skew to \"0s\" where the Dock renders a dash, and says \"5s\" where the Dock says \"just now\" — which would have reintroduced a fixed bug (a waiting chip reading \"—\" beside a timestamp reading \"just now\", from the same clock on the same row). The ladder of phrasings moved into the vocabulary both readers already declare; three ladders in the tree became two, and unifying `relativeTime` with kaval-tui's copy is a follow-up.","created":"2026-08-27T11:22:54-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/kolu-exports.md","dispatched":"2026-08-27T11:22:00-04:00","drishti-pr":"WAIVED by the human on this lane, 2026-08-27: \"consumer-exports: no need for drishti PR\" — a one-off, not a change to kolu's paired-drishti rule","from":"the kolu-leak survey, 2026-08-27 — olai cannot delete its copies until kolu exports the pieces","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/commit/39206d18","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2219","terminal":"e43a2368","worktree":"kolu/.worktrees/consumer-exports"}}
{"id":"td-kolu-fold","parent":"terminal-door","ord":"a5","title":"The kolu-complection fold","done":"2026-08-27T18:37:51-04:00","desc":"Complete. The findings of the 2026-08-27 survey were folded into pull request #405: both live defects fixed, with tests in the browser that hold them fixed; kolu's re-attach helpers imported instead of restated in olai; seven hand-copied pieces deleted against kolu pull request #2219 (merged as commit 39206d18); the packaging migration (nix/kolu.nix down to six seed packages plus kolu's consumer.nix, with the hydration and check scripts retired); the upgrade to Effect 4.0.0-rc.112 together with an assertion covering all three dependency sections of the manifest, proven by watching it fail before the fix and pass after; and two re-pins ending at kolu's master.\n\nReviewed: grok approved; the one finding pi said must be fixed was closed. Continuous integration was green at every commit that mattered.\n\nThe children record how each thread ended: the case-sensitivity question was ruled (exact match stays), the id-shortening cleanup was dropped by the human, and the machine-checked import rule rides the encapsulation fold.","created":"2026-08-27T11:22:54-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/td-fold-kolu-survey.md","dispatched":"2026-08-27T11:23:00-04:00","from":"the kolu-leak survey, 2026-08-27; the human ruled it rides THIS PR — \"Why the fuck would you open two more PRs in olai when terminal-door PR is unmerged?\""}}
{"id":"sp-review-grok","parent":"chat-subagents-pinned","ord":"a0","title":"review: grok","done":"2026-08-27T12:20:33-04:00","created":"2026-08-27T12:12:15-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T12:20:33-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/review-412.md","reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"eb5088e9","verdict":"MUST — the ask exception is half-built: titleOf (Transcript.tsx:269) reads the parent row's pinned .text, which the shipped adapter sets to \"Task\", so the one row where being wrong about who is speaking changes what a person presses does not name who is asking; same at transcript.ts:708/:888's death notices. Everything else PASSED on the read — membership (isAgentOut, independent of armed), stranding recounted at every door, #ended row-keyed, ElapsedProvider placement, opencode degrading flat, docs rewritten. 3 SHOULDs (previewing.ts:33's false docstring; watching.ts:83's no-longer-where-you-are-looking; chat.ts:466) + 1 NIT. No probe. PR comment on #412."}}
{"id":"sp-review-pi","parent":"chat-subagents-pinned","ord":"a1","title":"review: pi","done":"2026-08-27T12:20:33-04:00","created":"2026-08-27T12:12:15-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T12:20:33-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/review-412.md","reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"a009d693","verdict":"MUST — INDEPENDENTLY the same defect grok found (lanes.ts:162 via titleOf): two fan-out agents asking different permissions produce two indistinguishable \"↓ Task\" forms, a blocking decision with no usable attribution; unreachable in e2e because the fake titles spawns with descriptions. Everything else PASSED: the ask is unrepresentable as a filed row so it cannot be buried; the shelf is a shrinkable in-flow sibling with a transcript floor, so no geometry including the 268px phone case can cover a form; stranding recounted on begins/settle/replayEnded/gone/sessionOver; #ended re-keyed by row genuinely fixes the double-report; ElapsedProvider verifiably the only one and its consumer chain inside it. 1 SHOULD (previewing.ts:33, same as grok) + 1 NIT. No probe. PR comment on #412."}}
{"id":"fold-resolve-ruling","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a6","title":"Ruled: a wrong-case terminal id does not resolve","done":"2026-08-27T18:33:49-04:00","desc":"RULED (the human, 2026-08-27 ~18:31, question tool): olai keeps EXACT MATCH — a wrong-case terminal id does NOT resolve. Ids are machine-written lowercase hex; a wrong-case value is a typo, and \"not found\" said loudly beats a silent guess. Olai keeps its own resolver (moving to the wire slice with the encapsulation fold), documented as a deliberate divergence from kolu's case-folding resolveTerminalId. The ledger's L4 (the semantics-diff table) closes with this ruling — the divergence is now ruled, not pending.","created":"2026-08-27T12:59:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T18:33:55-04:00","custom":{"from":"#405's author, 2026-08-27 — raised as a ruling rather than a swap, correctly"}}
{"id":"fold-review-grok","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a7","title":"review: grok","done":"2026-08-27T13:48:23-04:00","created":"2026-08-27T13:37:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T13:48:23-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/review-405-fold.md","reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"8eb80be1","verdict":"APPROVE, no MUST — \"the packaging fold evaluates AND the graph it evaluates is the one the old hand list named\". Verified by nix eval at the pin: 32 names one-for-one with the old members incl. osfacts-client, 16 externals, the hydrate script present, the osfacts revision matching both pins, and every refusal path (unknown seed, empty seeds, unvendored seed, catalog:, extra graft, revision drift) throwing by name. Overlay-out confirmed correct (splicing a set that needs pkgs into the overlay list is a cycle); every consumer moved, no kolu-src-* attr left. 3 SHOULDs: FIRST_FRAME_MS's const JSDoc still carries the false provenance the round's own header rewrote (the sentence a grep hits first is the lie the header exists to kill); nix/kolu.nix:56 names the WRONG load-bearing seed (detect is a 1-member closure — padi-client and terminal-vocab arrive through solid-dockrow's 26, so an edit trusting that comment could hydrate without padi-client); and check-kolu-deps.sh:50 reads root dependencies ONLY while package.json:32 claims the overrides block is checked — so the silent-rewrite hole the assertion was written to close moved one block down. 3 NITs (stale bunfig/sourceDirs comments, the geometry harness still double-feeding the recency seam). PR comment on #405."}}
{"id":"fold-review-pi","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a8","title":"review: pi","done":"2026-08-27T13:54:25-04:00","created":"2026-08-27T13:37:30-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T13:54:25-04:00","custom":{"brief":"briefs/review-405-fold.md","reviewer":"agent-pi","terminal":"cfd92675","verdict":"CHANGES REQUESTED — one MUST that grok missed: rowRecency's null window SILENTLY ERASES the ago line (TerminalDoor.tsx:257). displayRecencyAt routes every non-wait-chip mode to the window channel, so a non-asking inactive row now renders the empty string where every state up to the reviewed 7c218136 rendered the row's age — an idle terminal that finished three hours ago stops saying so, and NO test can see it because nothing asserts an ago string anywhere. The author's stated reason (passing the same instant twice would make the modes agree by accident) is inverted by kolu's own producer: useDockRowBag passes input.recencyAt() as window and rowRecencyAt(meta) as own, and rankDockRows folds the window as rowRecencyAt(meta) over sub-rows — so for a tile with NO SPLITS the two channels carry the same instant BY CONSTRUCTION, which is exactly olai's shape. Everything else verified against the pinned tree: the packaging migration sound (six seeds walk to the same 32), every deletion's protection replaced not dropped (the hydrate body diffed identical, the osfacts throw architecturally stronger than a just leg, the .d.ts's three leaf imports confirmed present in the pin), the effect assertion reaching all three dep sections and naming both versions, the budget refill and its browser fence real, motion off the wire with the schema stripping it, and NarrowedRowVocab's claim compiler-fenced rather than hopeful. 4 SHOULDs (two of them grok's independently) + 3 NITs, incl. correcting the round's own '17 spellings' to fourteen across thirteen manifests. PR comment on #405."}}
{"id":"odu-rc112","parent":"odu-effect-migration","ord":"a0","title":"kolu master is on rc.112 now","cancelled":"2026-08-28T08:53:13-04:00","desc":"The human merged kolu#2218 (475815cb) — \"Effect 4.0.0-rc.112 and the authoring law its JSON Schema fix retires\". odu#91's whole argument was that rc.112 exists on npm but is deliberately NOT taken, because cp -rL hydration makes odu's overrides the only authority over which effect kolu's sources compile against, and following kolu's catalog would silently rebind rc.110-vetted sources. That premise has moved: kolu's own sources are now rc.112-vetted, so holding odu at rc.110 inverts the argument — it becomes odu compiling kolu's rc.112 sources against an effect that predates them, which is the exact failure the migration exists to fix. Worth the human's eye before odu#91 merges.","created":"2026-08-27T13:39:57-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T08:53:13-04:00"}
{"id":"fold-ci-sequence","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"a9","title":"Both PRs to green CI while the human is out","done":"2026-08-27T17:16:09-04:00","desc":"Done. The human's order while away — \"full CI on both PRs\" — was run as a SEQUENCE because the two pull requests were coupled through the Effect version: kolu #2219 finished its pre-merge quality routine at its final commit; olai re-pinned to that commit and compiled cleanly (the consumer-validates-provider gate); one olai continuous-integration run came back green; then kolu's own run passed on both platforms (the macOS lane on the machine called petit, whose full Nix store had been cleared by then). Both pull requests ended green and cleanly mergeable, verified by the orchestrator reading GitHub directly rather than trusting any agent's claim.\n\nOne stumble along the way is recorded in git: the human pressed \"Update branch\" on #405, which detached the green statuses from the moved head, and the orchestrator briefly ordered a premature re-run before countermanding it under the ruling that terminal-door waits for kolu to be fully ready.","after":["kolu-consumer-exports"],"created":"2026-08-27T14:02:27-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human, 2026-08-27 ~13:57: \"i'll brb - remember to full CI on both PRs\""}}
{"id":"rc112-facts","parent":"fold-ci-sequence","ord":"a0","title":"What rc.112 actually costs olai","done":"2026-08-27T14:17:35-04:00","desc":"Established by the orchestrator reading kolu pull request #2218 directly, so that neither author has to re-derive it. (rc.112 means release candidate 112 of Effect 4.0.0, the framework olai and kolu both build on.)\n\n1. **For source code it is a pure version change.** Kolu's own typechecking was green on the bare version bump with zero source edits, and it CHECKED rather than assumed that nothing else in rc.111 or rc.112 touches anything in the repository: every pool is a local RemotePool, so Effect's reshaping of `Pool.State` is invisible; nothing reads `Scope.State`; there are no hand-written Matcher annotations; and `@standard-schema/spec` only enters through `@vitest/expect`.\n\n2. **A second dependency pin moves with it.** Kolu could not name rc.112 while the grafted osfacts-client manifest still said rc.110; that was blocked on juspay/osfacts#8, which has now landed. Kolu master's pinned osfacts revision is 24d3e87a, while pull request #405's worktree still pinned 72a794c2 at the time. The build-time drift refusal that #405 adopted THREW on the re-pin until olai bumped to 24d3e87a — the check working on its first day, and a known step rather than an hour lost to a confusing evaluation failure.\n\n3. **The rule this retires is an opportunity, not just something to survive.** Up to rc.110, annotating a schema that already carried a check buried the description inside an `allOf` branch, and the property node itself carried none — so even a bare `Schema.Int.annotate({description})` lost its text, because `Schema.Int` is `Schema.Number.check(isInt())`. rc.111 compacts checks onto the node they constrain. So annotations olai wrote under the old rule may have been INVISIBLE to host programs and models all along, and will now render. A description nobody could read is the same class of defect as an erased \"ago\" line.\n\n4. **Two traps survive, so the fix is not total:** a check whose JSON Schema keyword collides with one already on the node still splits into an `allOf`; and a check built with `Schema.makeFilter` contributes no JSON Schema keyword at all, so it is dropped WHOLE, annotation included.","created":"2026-08-27T14:15:02-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp-buried-blurb","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"aG","title":"One MCP description was never readable","todo":true,"desc":"Found by #405's author standing up olai's REAL MCP server through the test harness and walking every tool's advertised inputSchema — not by grepping. Buried count across the whole surface: ONE.\n\n`empty_trash.properties.was` — a careful paragraph explaining that `was` is a concurrency guard: supply the count a human was shown, and the write is refused naming both numbers if a record arrived meanwhile. Under effect rc.110 that description sat in an allOf branch, so no host rendered it and no agent calling empty_trash has ever been able to read it. rc.112 makes it render. One line of value falling out of a version bump.\n\nThe dynamic count matched the static scan exactly (Schema.Int is the only checked primitive olai uses — 29 uses, one annotated — and there are zero .check().annotate() chains), so the audit is closed with evidence rather than inference.\n\nThe surviving makeFilter trap costs olai nothing: exactly one makeFilter in the tree (DocumentPath at address.ts:97), carrying no annotation to lose — though it emits bare {\"type\":\"string\"} at BOTH RCs, so it already drops its expected shape today. A standing gap, not a regression, and worth its own item some day.","created":"2026-08-27T14:17:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T17:16:06-04:00"}
{"id":"kolu-shortid-copies","parent":"td-kolu-fold","ord":"aA","title":"shortId is copied more widely than stated","cancelled":"2026-08-27T18:33:52-04:00","desc":"Recorded on olai's side so it would survive independently of kolu's branch history — a finding that exists only in a debate transcript dies when the branch does.\n\nKolu's simplicity-review pass found that the id-shortening helper is copied more widely than kolu pull request #2219's description states: six more `.slice(0, 8)` sites in the browser package alone. The question parked at the time was whether `shortId` should travel together with `resolveTerminalId` as a single export; the author kept it OUT of #2219 (churning an exported signature while olai was mid-way through re-pinning against that commit costs more than the ergonomic gain), and the orchestrator endorsed that.\n\n**DROPPED by the human, 2026-08-27:** not worth a pull request. Cancelled.","after":["kolu-consumer-exports"],"created":"2026-08-27T14:20:07-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"consume-caught-2","parent":"fold-ci-sequence","ord":"a2","title":"The consumer caught two moved shapes","done":"2026-08-27T16:07:57-04:00","desc":"The arrangement where olai compiles against kolu's unmerged branch paid for itself again, in three minutes.\n\nThe kolu author reported that no exported signature had changed at commit 5892a95d. That was true of kolu pull request #2218's own diff, but false of the branch head, because a simplicity-review pass rode in with the master merge. Olai's compile found two changes: on `StatePipBind`, the field `shellLive` became `hasAgent`; and `rowRecency` collapsed from four arguments to three, with the two timestamp channels becoming a single record `{ window, own }`.\n\nOlai took both in the same commit as the re-pin. The wire change is `shellLive` → `hasAgent` on `FleetTerminal.pip`, and the author generalized its own earlier argument rather than treating this as a second special case: A DERIVED FIELD IS NOT A WIRE FACT. `shellLive` is derivable, and a bundle carrying it independently could spell `{ variant: \"working\", shellLive: true }` — a combination no producer ever generates. `hasAgent` stays because it is the one input the bundle cannot derive. This is the same rule that took `motion` off the wire.\n\n**The standing lesson for reporting, given to the kolu author:** after any round that touched exports, say what MOVED rather than what you believe held. A simplicity or refactoring stage is exactly where a shape changes while the author is thinking about something else.","created":"2026-08-27T14:25:20-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-camera-capture","parent":"feat-chat","ord":"aI","title":"Camera capture on the phone: the + takes a photo too","done":"2026-08-27T15:56:24-04:00","desc":"The composer's `+` clicks one hidden file picker (`Composer.tsx`), and `capture` is deliberately absent — the comment argues the photo roll must stay first-class. On a phone outside, the human wanted to point the camera at something and send it; today that is shoot-in-camera-app, then find it in the picker. The shape: the `+` grows a second entry (or becomes a two-item menu) so roll and camera are BOTH first-class — `capture=\"environment\"` on a second hidden input is the whole mechanism, and any source just feeds `holding.take()` like paste/drop/pick already do. Desktop keeps the single-click picker; the menu only where a camera exists, or the camera entry simply hides.","created":"2026-08-27T14:56:17-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T15:56:25-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/chat-camera-capture.md","from":"the human 2026-08-27 — \"I was outside yesterday and used Olai in my phone and wanted to capture a photo directly to chat\"","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/7ecbcf1d","ruled":"the human 2026-08-27 — dispatch to pi; AUTO-MERGE on the orchestrator's visual verification; MUST support capturing several photos into one message before sending","terminal":"a2a9923f"}}
